se websearch to find current protocols and best practices, and potentially arxiv_search for any recent methodological papers on this topic.", "time": {"start": 1773730268448, "end": 1773730279387}}, {"id": "prt_cfa902104001BDITDLlrgeyRQc", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff272001mB6C026lBoFRWw", "type": "text", "text": "\n\nI'll help you with both requests. Let me search for current best practices on murine naïve B cell isolation and troubleshooting approaches.", "time": {"start": 1773730279387, "end": 1773730279387}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9023f5001tvjmzG78AVhU3o", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff272001mB6C026lBoFRWw", "type": "tool", "callID": "call_a01f603b841142b3ae32a76e", "tool": "websearch", "state": {"status": "error", "input": {"query": "murine naive B cell negative selection isolation protocol magnetic activated cell sorting MACS", "numResults": 10}, "error": "Error: Unable to connect. Is the computer able to access the url?", "time": {"start": 1773730277221, "end": 1773730277336}}}, {"id": "prt_cfa902846001vcD8z1cYhD6sQX", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff272001mB6C026lBoFRWw", "type": "tool", "callID": "call_3a74f6135f63402d8a12f744", "tool": "websearch", "state": {"status": "error", "input": {"query": "B cell isolation low viability purity troubleshooting common problems causes", "numResults": 10}, "error": "Error: Unable to connect. Is the computer able to access the url?", "time": {"start": 1773730278213, "end": 1773730278321}}}, {"id": "prt_cfa902c25001v9ZeNyo7rZGVHj", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff272001mB6C026lBoFRWw", "type": "tool", "callID": "call_c1513dcff33244f2ab3a1320", "tool": "websearch", "state": {"status": "error", "input": {"query": "in vitro murine B cell differentiation activation protocol LPS anti-IgM cytokines", "numResults": 8}, "error": "Error: Unable to connect. Is the computer able to access the url?", "time": {"start": 1773730279324, "end": 1773730279440}}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9030130014R91jZf1u2ODc3", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff272001mB6C026lBoFRWw", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "tool-calls", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 25739, "output": 300, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa90301f001oUFN184BX42toS", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa90301f001oUFN184BX42toS", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730279455, "completed": 1773730300993}, "parentID": "msg_cfa8ff241001EWR0zD7CIiaKyc", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__6120__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__6120__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 25994, "output": 112, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "tool-calls"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa9034660014BVWsCemz36PfW", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa90301f001oUFN184BX42toS", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa903540001ov57lhW0Gc6Z6Q", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa90301f001oUFN184BX42toS", "type": "text", "text": "\n\nLet me try a different approach and search for academic literature on this topic:", "time": {"start": 1773730298326, "end": 1773730298326}}, {"id": "prt_cfa904e4b001OEoj7YTOJKeu2o", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa90301f001oUFN184BX42toS", "type": "tool", "callID": "call_87fb1b417c584bd3bde7002d", "tool": "arxiv_search", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"query": "murine B cell isolation negative selection magnetic cell sorting", "topK": 5}, "output": "Found 5 papers (showing 5):\n\n| # | arXiv ID | Title | Authors | Categories | Published |\n|---|----------|-------|---------|------------|-----------|\n| 1 | 2312.05785v3 | On Possible Indicators of Negative Selection in Germinal Cen | Bertrand Ottino-Loffler, Gabriel Victora | q-bio.PE, math.DS | 2023-12-10 |\n| 2 | 1407.5503v1 | Rare event simulation in immune biology: Models of negative  | Corinna Ernst, Ellen Baake | q-bio.CB, math.PR | 2014-07-21 |\n| 3 | 2508.09519v1 | Bayesian inference of antibody evolutionary dynamics using m | Athanasios G. Bakis, Ashni A. Vora, Tatsuya Araki et al. | stat.ME | 2025-08-13 |\n| 4 | 2012.05817v3 | Understanding Memory B Cell Selection | Stephen Lindsly, Maya Gupta, Cooper Stansbury et al. | q-bio.CB | 2020-12-09 |\n| 5 | 1209.0337v1 | Quorum sensing contributes to activated B cell homeostasis a | Caroline Montaudouin, Marie Anson, Yi Hao et al. | q-bio.CB | 2012-09-03 |\n\n**Paper Details:**\n\n### 2312.05785v3: On Possible Indicators of Negative Selection in Germinal Centers\n**Authors:** Bertrand Ottino-Loffler, Gabriel Victora\n**Categories:** q-bio.PE, math.DS\n**Published:** 2023-12-10\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.05785v3\n\n**Abstract:** A central feature of vertebrate immune response is affinity maturation,\nwherein antibody-producing B cells undergo evolutionary selection in\nmicroanatomical structures called germinal centers, which form in secondary\nlymphoid organs upon antigen exposure. While it has been shown that the median\nB cell affinity dependably increases over the course of maturation, the exact\nlogic behind this evolution remains vague. Three potential selection methods\ninclude encouraging the reproduction of high affinity cells (``birth/positive\nselection''), encouraging cell death in low affinity cells (``death/negative\nselection''), and adjusting the mutation rate based on cell affinity\n(``mutational selection''). While all three forms of selection would lead to a\nnet increase in affinity, different selection methods may lead to distinct\nstatistical dynamics. We present a tractable model of selection, and analyze\nproposed signatures of negative selection. Given the simplicity of the model,\nsuch signatures should be stronger here than in real systems. However, we find\na number of intuitively appealing metrics -- such as preferential ancestry\nratios, terminal node counts, and mutation count skewness -- are all ill-suited\nfor detecting selection method.\n\n---\n\n### 1407.5503v1: Rare event simulation in immune biology: Models of negative selection in T-cell maturation\n**Authors:** Corinna Ernst, Ellen Baake\n**Categories:** q-bio.CB, math.PR, q-bio.MN, 92C99, 92-08, 60F10\n**Published:** 2014-07-21\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5503v1\n\n**Abstract:** We present a probabilistic T-cell model that includes negative selection and\ntakes contrasting models of tissue-restricted antigen (TRA) expression in the\nthymus into account. We start from the basic model of van den Berg, Rand, and\nBurroughs (2001) and include negative selection via individual-based T-cell\nmodelling, in which each T-cell is defined by its stimulation rates to all\nrelevant self antigens. We present a simulation approach based on partial\ntilting of the stimulation rates recognized by a single T-cell. We investigate\nthe effects of negative selection for diverging modes of thymic antigen\npresentation, namely arbitrary TRA presentation, and more or less strict\nemulation of tissue-specific cell lines. We observe that negative selection\nleads to truncation of the tail of the distribution of the stimulation rates\nmature T-cells receive from self antigens, i.e., the self background is\nreduced. This increases the activation probabilities of single T-cells in the\npresence of non-self antigens.\n\n---\n\n### 2508.09519v1: Bayesian inference of antibody evolutionary dynamics using multitype branching processes\n**Authors:** Athanasios G. Bakis, Ashni A. Vora, Tatsuya Araki, Tongqiu Jia, Jared G. Galloway, Chris Jennings-Shaffer, Gabriel D. Victora, Yun S. Song, William S. DeWitt, Frederick A. Matsen IV, Volodymyr M. Minin\n**Categories:** stat.ME\n**Published:** 2025-08-13\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.09519v1\n\n**Abstract:** When our immune system encounters foreign antigens (i.e., from pathogens),\nthe B cells that produce our antibodies undergo a cyclic process of\nproliferation, mutation, and selection, improving their ability to bind to the\nspecific antigen. Immunologists have recently developed powerful experimental\ntechniques to investigate this process in mouse models. In one such experiment,\nmice are engineered with a monoclonal B-cell precursor and immunized with a\nmodel antigen. B cells are sampled from sacrificed mice after the immune\nresponse has progressed, and the mutated genetic loci encoding antibodies are\nsequenced. This experiment allows parallel replay of antibody evolution, but\nproduces data at only one time point; we are unable to observe the evolutionary\ntrajectories that lead to optimized antibody affinity in each mouse. To address\nthis, we model antibody evolution as a multitype branching process and\nintegrate over unobserved histories conditioned on phylogenetic signal in\nsequence data, leveraging parallel experimental replays for parameter\ninference. We infer the functional relationship between B-cell fitness and\nantigen binding affinity in a Bayesian framework, equipped with an efficient\nlikelihood calculation algorithm and Markov chain Monte Carlo posterior\napproximation. In a simulation study, we demonstrate that a sigmoidal\nrelationship between fitness and binding affinity can be recovered from\nrealizations of the branching process. We then perform inference for\nexperimental data from 52 replayed B-cell lineages sampled 15 days after\nimmunization, yielding a total of 3,758 sampled B cells. The recovered\nsigmoidal curve indicates that the fitness of high-affinity B cells is over six\ntimes larger than that of low-affinity B cells, with a sharp transition from\nlow to high fitness values as affinity increases.\n\n---\n\n### 2012.05817v3: Understanding Memory B Cell Selection\n**Authors:** Stephen Lindsly, Maya Gupta, Cooper Stansbury, Indika Rajapakse\n**Categories:** q-bio.CB\n**Published:** 2020-12-09\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.05817v3\n\n**Abstract:** The mammalian adaptive immune system has evolved over millions of years to\nbecome an incredibly effective defense against foreign antigens. The adaptive\nimmune system's humoral response creates plasma B cells and memory B cells,\neach with their own immunological objectives. The affinity maturation process\nis widely viewed as a heuristic to solve the global optimization problem of\nfinding B cells with high affinity to the antigen. However, memory B cells\nappear to be purposely selected earlier in the affinity maturation process and\nhave lower affinity. We propose that this memory B cell selection process may\nbe an approximate solution to two optimization problems: optimizing for\naffinity to similar antigens in the future despite mutations or other minor\ndifferences, and optimizing to warm start the generation of plasma B cells in\nthe future. We use simulations to provide evidence for our hypotheses, taking\ninto account data showing that certain B cell mutations are more likely than\nothers. Our findings are consistent with memory B cells having high-affinity to\nmutated antigens, but do not provide strong evidence that memory B cells will\nbe more useful than selected naive B cells for seeding the secondary germinal\ncenters.\n\n---\n\n### 1209.0337v1: Quorum sensing contributes to activated B cell homeostasis and to prevent autoimmunity\n**Authors:** Caroline Montaudouin, Marie Anson, Yi Hao, Susanne V. Duncker, Tahia Fernandez, Emmanuelle Gaudin, Michael Ehrenstein, William G. Kerr, Jean-Herve Colle, Pierre Bruhns, Marc Daeron, Antonio A. Freitas\n**Categories:** q-bio.CB\n**Published:** 2012-09-03\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/1209.0337v1\n\n**Abstract:** Maintenance of plasma IgM levels is critical for immune system function and\nhomeostasis in humans and mice. However, the mechanisms that control\nhomeostasis of the activated IgM-secreting B cells are unknown. After adoptive\ntransfer into immune-deficient hosts, B-lymphocytes expand poorly but fully\nreconstitute the pool of natural IgM-secreting cells and circulating IgM\nlevels. By using sequential cell transfers and B cell populations from several\nmutant mice, we were able to identify novel mechanisms regulating the size of\nthe IgM-secreting B cell pool. Contrary to previous mechanisms described\nregulating homeostasis, which involve competition for the same niche by cells\nhaving overlapping survival requirements, homeostasis of the innate\nIgM-secreting B cell pool is also achieved when B cells populations are able to\nmonitor the number of activated B cells by detecting their secreted products.\nNotably, B cell populations are able to assess the density of activated B cells\nby sensing their secreted IgG. This process involves the Fc{\\gamma}RIIB, a\nlow-affinity IgG receptor that is expressed on B cells and acts as a negative\nregulator of B cell activation, and its intracellular effector the inositol\nphosphatase SHIP. As a result of the engagement of this inhibitory pathway the\nnumber of activated IgM-secreting B cells is kept under control. We hypothesize\nthat malfunction of this quorum-sensing mechanism may lead to uncontrolled B\ncell activation and autoimmunity.\n\n---\n", "title": "5 papers found", "metadata": {"total": 5, "shown": 5, "truncated": false}, "time": {"start": 1773730296954, "end": 1773730299884}}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9076ae001Fa4oGYO4YnmtH6", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa90301f001oUFN184BX42toS", "type": "tool", "callID": "call_6bf7a58ac98445ffa68a816d", "tool": "arxiv_search", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"query": "primary B cell culture viability differentiation in vitro", "topK": 5}, "output": "Found 5 papers (showing 5):\n\n| # | arXiv ID | Title | Authors | Categories | Published |\n|---|----------|-------|---------|------------|-----------|\n| 1 | 2510.02812 | Dynamics of memory B cells and plasmablasts in healthy indiv | Andrea Mazzolini, Aleksandra M. Walczak, Thierry Mora | q-bio.PE | 2025-10-03 |\n| 2 | 2510.02812v1 | Dynamics of memory B cells and plasmablasts in healthy indiv | Andrea Mazzolini, Aleksandra M. Walczak, Thierry Mora | q-bio.PE | 2025-10-03 |\n| 3 | 2304.02794v4 | Nonequilibrium antigen recognition during infections and vac | Roberto Morán-Tovar, Michael Lässig | q-bio.PE, q-bio.SC | 2023-04-05 |\n| 4 | 1505.03785v1 | A dynamical model of the adaptive immune system: effects of  | Silvia Bartolucci, Alessia Annibale | q-bio.CB, cond-mat.soft | 2015-05-14 |\n| 5 | 2010.11580v2 | Survival probability and size of lineages in antibody affini | Marco Molari, Rémi Monasson, Simona Cocco | q-bio.PE | 2020-10-22 |\n\n**Paper Details:**\n\n### 2510.02812: Dynamics of memory B cells and plasmablasts in healthy individuals\n**Authors:** Andrea Mazzolini, Aleksandra M. Walczak, Thierry Mora\n**Categories:** q-bio.PE\n**Published:** 2025-10-03\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.02812\n\n**Abstract:** Our adaptive immune system relies on the persistence over long times of a\ndiverse set of antigen-experienced B cells to encode our memories of past\ninfections and to protect us against future ones. While longitudinal repertoire\nsequencing promises to track the long-term dynamics of many B cell clones\nsimultaneously, sampling and experimental noise make it hard to draw reliable\nquantitative conclusions. Leveraging statistical inference, we infer the\ndynamics of memory B cell clonal dynamics and conversion to plasmablasts, which\nincludes clone creation, degradation, abundance fluctuations, and\ndifferentiation. We find that memory B cell clones degrade slowly, with a\nhalf-life of 10 years. Based on the inferred parameters, we predict that it\ntakes about 50 years to renew 50\\% of the repertoire, with most observed clones\nsurviving for a lifetime. We infer that, on average, 1 out of 100 memory B\ncells differentiates into a plasmablast each year, more than expected from\npurely antigen-stimulated differentiation, and that plasmablast clones degrade\nwith a half-life of about one year in the absence of memory imports. Our method\nis general and could be applied to other longitudinal repertoire sequencing B\ncell subsets.\n\n---\n\n### 2510.02812v1: Dynamics of memory B cells and plasmablasts in healthy individuals\n**Authors:** Andrea Mazzolini, Aleksandra M. Walczak, Thierry Mora\n**Categories:** q-bio.PE\n**Published:** 2025-10-03\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.02812v1\n\n**Abstract:** Our adaptive immune system relies on the persistence over long times of a\ndiverse set of antigen-experienced B cells to encode our memories of past\ninfections and to protect us against future ones. While longitudinal repertoire\nsequencing promises to track the long-term dynamics of many B cell clones\nsimultaneously, sampling and experimental noise make it hard to draw reliable\nquantitative conclusions. Leveraging statistical inference, we infer the\ndynamics of memory B cell clonal dynamics and conversion to plasmablasts, which\nincludes clone creation, degradation, abundance fluctuations, and\ndifferentiation. We find that memory B cell clones degrade slowly, with a\nhalf-life of 10 years. Based on the inferred parameters, we predict that it\ntakes about 50 years to renew 50\\% of the repertoire, with most observed clones\nsurviving for a lifetime. We infer that, on average, 1 out of 100 memory B\ncells differentiates into a plasmablast each year, more than expected from\npurely antigen-stimulated differentiation, and that plasmablast clones degrade\nwith a half-life of about one year in the absence of memory imports. Our method\nis general and could be applied to other longitudinal repertoire sequencing B\ncell subsets.\n\n---\n\n### 2304.02794v4: Nonequilibrium antigen recognition during infections and vaccinations\n**Authors:** Roberto Morán-Tovar, Michael Lässig\n**Categories:** q-bio.PE, q-bio.SC\n**Published:** 2023-04-05\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.02794v4\n\n**Abstract:** The immune response to an acute primary infection is a coupled process of\nantigen proliferation, molecular recognition by naive B cells, and their\nsubsequent proliferation and antibody shedding. This process contains a\nfundamental problem: the recognition of an exponentially time-dependent antigen\nsignal. Here we show that B cells can efficiently recognise new antigens by a\ntuned kinetic proofreading mechanism, where the molecular recognition machinery\nis adapted to the complexity of the immune repertoire. This process produces\npotent, specific and fast recognition of antigens, maintaining a spectrum of\ngenetically distinct B cell lineages as input for affinity maturation. We show\nthat the proliferation-recognition dynamics of a primary infection is a\ngeneralised Luria-Delbr\\\"uck process, akin to the dynamics of the classic\nfluctuation experiment. This map establishes a link between signal recognition\ndynamics and evolution. We derive the resulting statistics of the activated\nimmune repertoire: antigen binding affinity, expected size, and frequency of\nactive B cell clones are related by power laws, which define the class of\ngeneralised Luria-Delbr\\\"uck processes. Their exponents depend on the antigen\nand B cell proliferation rate, the number of proofreading steps, and the\nlineage density of the naive repertoire. We extend the model to include\nspatio-temporal processes, including the diffusion-recognition dynamics of a\nvaccination. Empirical data of activated mouse immune repertoires are found to\nbe consistent with activation involving about three proofreading steps. The\nmodel predicts key clinical characteristics of acute infections and\nvaccinations, including the emergence of elite neutralisers and the effects of\nimmune ageing. More broadly, our results establish infections and vaccinations\nas a new probe into the global architecture and functional principles of immune\nrepertoires.\n\n---\n\n### 1505.03785v1: A dynamical model of the adaptive immune system: effects of cells promiscuity, antigens and B-B interactions\n**Authors:** Silvia Bartolucci, Alessia Annibale\n**Categories:** q-bio.CB, cond-mat.soft\n**Published:** 2015-05-14\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/1505.03785v1\n\n**Abstract:** We analyse a minimal model for the primary response in the adaptive immune\nsystem comprising three different players: antigens, T and B cells. We assume\nB-T interactions to be diluted and sampled locally from heterogeneous degree\ndistributions, which mimic B cells receptors' promiscuity. We derive dynamical\nequations for the order parameters quantifying the B cells activation and study\nthe nature and stability of the stationary solutions using linear stability\nanalysis and Monte Carlo simulations.The system's behaviour is studied in\ndifferent scaling regimes of the number of B cells, dilution in the\ninteractions and number of antigens. Our analysis shows that: (i) B cells\nactivation depends on the number of receptors in such a way that cells with an\ninsufficient number of triggered receptors cannot be activated; (ii) idiotypic\n(i.e. B-B) interactions enhance parallel activation of multiple clones,\nimproving the system's ability to fight different pathogens in parallel; (iii)\nthe higher the fraction of antigens within the host the harder is for the\nsystem to sustain parallel signalling to B cells, crucial for the homeostatic\ncontrol of cell numbers.\n\n---\n\n### 2010.11580v2: Survival probability and size of lineages in antibody affinity maturation\n**Authors:** Marco Molari, Rémi Monasson, Simona Cocco\n**Categories:** q-bio.PE\n**Published:** 2020-10-22\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.11580v2\n\n**Abstract:** Affinity Maturation (AM) is the process through which the immune system is\nable to develop potent antibodies against new pathogens it encounters, and is\nat the base of the efficacy of vaccines. At its core AM is analogous to a\nDarwinian evolutionary process, where B-cells mutate and are selected on the\nbase of their affinity for an Antigen (Ag), and Ag availability tunes the\nselective pressure. In cases when this selective pressure is high the number of\nB-cells might quickly decrease and the population might risk extinction in what\nis known as a population bottleneck. Here we study the probability for a B-cell\nlineage to survive this bottleneck scenario as a function of the progenitor\naffinity for the Ag. Using recursive relations and probability generating\nfunctions we derive expressions for the average extinction time and progeny\nsize for lineages that go extinct. We then extend our results to the full\npopulation, both in the absence and presence of competition for T-cell help,\nand quantify the population survival probability as a function of Ag\nconcentration and initial population size. Our study suggests the population\nbottleneck phenomenology might represent a limit case in the space of\nbiologically plausible maturation scenarios, whose characterization could help\nguide the process of vaccine development.\n\n---\n", "title": "5 papers found", "metadata": {"total": 5, "shown": 5, "truncated": false}, "time": {"start": 1773730298259, "end": 1773730300984}}}, {"id": "prt_cfa908439001WpBkqehPlhZae7", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa90301f001oUFN184BX42toS", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "tool-calls", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 25994, "output": 112, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa908444001GbLNF8pMEgQaTZ", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa908444001GbLNF8pMEgQaTZ", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730300996, "completed": 1773730532521}, "parentID": "msg_cfa8ff241001EWR0zD7CIiaKyc", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__6120__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__6120__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 30786, "output": 3287, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa9088eb001UNRl7n4CxzhLLR", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa908444001GbLNF8pMEgQaTZ", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa9089de001eYxEWCNRspRMQ1", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa908444001GbLNF8pMEgQaTZ", "type": "text", "text": "\n\nThe academic papers focus on B cell biology theory rather than practical protocols. Let me provide you with comprehensive experimental protocols based on established immunological methods:\n\n---\n\n## **Complete Protocol: Murine Naïve B Cell Purification via Negative Selection and In Vitro Differentiation**\n\n### **Part 1: Naïve B Cell Isolation by Negative Selection**\n\n#### **Materials Required**\n\n**Tissue & Reagents:**\n- Spleen and/or lymph nodes from mice (6-12 weeks old)\n- MACS buffer: PBS + 2% FBS + 2 mM EDTA, pH 7.2, sterile-filtered\n- Red blood cell (RBC) lysis buffer (ammonium chloride-based)\n- Murine Naïve B Cell Isolation Kit (negative selection) - commercial options:\n  - Miltenyi Biotec: B Cell Isolation Kit II, mouse (cat# 130-090-862)\n  - STEMCELL Technologies: EasySep™ Mouse B Cell Isolation Kit (cat# 19854)\n  - BioLegend: MojoSort™ Mouse Pan B Cell Isolation Kit\n- Fluorochrome-conjugated antibodies for phenotyping (optional):\n  - anti-B220 (RA3-6B2), anti-IgD, anti-CD43, anti-CD5, anti-CD138\n\n**Equipment:**\n- MACS columns (LS or LD columns, Miltenyi)\n- MACS separator magnet\n- 70 μm cell strainers\n- 15 mL and 50 mL conical tubes\n- Hemocytometer or automated cell counter\n- Flow cytometer (for purity assessment)\n\n---\n\n#### **Step-by-Step Protocol**\n\n**Day 0: Tissue Harvest and Single Cell Suspension**\n\n1. **Euthanize mouse** following approved IACUC protocol\n2. **Harvest spleen and/or lymph nodes** (inguinal, axillary, brachial, mesenteric)\n   - Place tissue in cold MACS buffer on ice\n   - Work quickly to maintain viability\n\n3. **Prepare single-cell suspension:**\n   - Place tissue on 70 μm cell strainer in 15 mL conical tube\n   - Gently dissociate with plunger end of syringe\n   - Rinse strainer with 5-10 mL cold MACS buffer\n   - Centrifuge at 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n\n4. **RBC lysis (for spleen):**\n   - Resuspend pellet in 2-5 mL RBC lysis buffer\n   - Incubate 2-3 min at room temperature (NOT on ice)\n   - Quench with 10 mL MACS buffer\n   - Centrifuge 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n   - Repeat if necessary until pellet is white\n\n5. **Filter and count:**\n   - Pass through 70 μm strainer again to remove clumps\n   - Count viable cells with trypan blue\n   - Expected yield: 5-8 × 10⁷ cells/spleen\n\n---\n\n**Negative Selection (MACS)**\n\n6. **Fc receptor blocking:**\n   - Resuspend cells at 10⁸ cells/mL in MACS buffer\n   - Add FcR blocking reagent (10 μL per 10⁷ cells)\n   - Incubate 10 min, 4°C\n\n7. **Biotin antibody cocktail incubation:**\n   - Add biotinylated antibody cocktail (targets: CD4, CD8, CD43, CD11b, CD11c, CD49b, CD90.2, Ter-119, IgD⁺ for some kits)\n   - 100 μL per 10⁷ cells\n   - Incubate 10 min, 4°C\n\n8. **Magnetic bead labeling:**\n   - Add anti-biotin microbeads (20 μL per 10⁷ cells)\n   - Incubate 15 min, 4°C\n   - Wash with 10 mL MACS buffer\n   - Centrifuge 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n\n9. **Column separation:**\n   - Place LS column in magnetic separator\n   - Equilibrate with 3 mL MACS buffer\n   - Resuspend cells in 3 mL MACS buffer\n   - Apply cell suspension to column\n   - Collect **flow-through** (this contains naïve B cells)\n   - Wash column 3× with 3 mL MACS buffer\n   - Combine all flow-through fractions\n\n10. **Final processing:**\n    - Centrifuge flow-through 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n    - Resuspend in appropriate culture medium\n    - Count and assess viability\n\n---\n\n### **Part 2: In Vitro B Cell Differentiation**\n\n#### **Culture Medium**\n- RPMI 1640 + 10% FBS (heat-inactivated)\n- 2 mM L-glutamine\n- 1 mM sodium pyruvate\n- 10 mM HEPES\n- 100 U/mL penicillin/streptomycin\n- 50 μM β-mercaptoethanol (critical for B cell survival)\n\n#### **Differentiation Conditions**\n\n| **Stimulation** | **Reagent** | **Final Concentration** | **Outcome** |\n|-----------------|-------------|------------------------|-------------|\n| **T-independent** | LPS (E. coli O111:B4) | 10-25 μg/mL | Polyclonal activation, plasmablast differentiation |\n| **BCR stimulation** | F(ab')₂ anti-IgM | 10 μg/mL | BCR crosslinking |\n| **T-dependent mimic** | anti-CD40 + IL-4 | 1 μg/mL + 20 ng/mL | GC-like differentiation |\n| **Plasma cell** | anti-CD40 + IL-21 | 1 μg/mL + 50 ng/mL | Plasmablast/plasma cell |\n| **Class switch** | LPS + IL-4 | 10 μg/mL + 20 ng/mL | IgG1 switching |\n\n#### **Culture Protocol**\n\n1. **Resuspend naïve B cells** at 0.5-1 × 10⁶ cells/mL in complete medium\n2. **Plate in 96-well U-bottom or 24-well plates**\n   - 200 μL/well (96-well) or 1 mL/well (24-well)\n3. **Add stimuli** as appropriate for differentiation goal\n4. **Incubate** 37°C, 5% CO₂, humidified\n5. **Feed every 2-3 days:**\n   - Gently resuspend cells\n   - Remove half the medium\n   - Add fresh complete medium with cytokines\n6. **Harvest** at desired timepoint:\n   - Day 1-2: Early activation (CD69, CD86 upregulation)\n   - Day 3-4: Proliferation peak (CFSE dilution)\n   - Day 5-7: Plasmablast differentiation (CD138⁺, Blimp-1)\n\n---\n\n### **Part 3: Quality Control**\n\n#### **Purity Assessment (Flow Cytometry)**\nStain with:\n- **B220⁺ CD19⁺** (B cell markers) - should be >90%\n- **IgD⁺ CD23⁺ CD43⁻ CD5⁻** (naïve phenotype)\n- **CD4⁻ CD8⁻ CD11b⁻** (exclusion markers)\n\n#### **Viability Assessment**\n- Trypan blue exclusion\n- 7-AAD or propidium iodide by flow cytometry\n- Annexin V/PI for apoptosis\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## **Troubleshooting Experimental Plan: Low Viability and Purity**\n\n### **Systematic Investigation Design**\n\nI'll design a comprehensive troubleshooting plan addressing both issues:\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 1: Identify Stage of Cell Loss**\n\n**Objective:** Determine whether cell death occurs during isolation or post-culture\n\n**Design:**\n```\nTrack cell number and viability at each step:\n├─ Starting tissue (post-RBC lysis)\n├─ Post-Fc block\n├─ Post-antibody labeling\n├─ Post-magnetic separation (flow-through)\n└─ 24h post-culture (unstimulated)\n```\n\n**Expected outcome:** Pinpoint whether loss is mechanical/enzymatic (isolation) or culture-related\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 2: Test Critical Variables Affecting Viability**\n\n**2A. Temperature Sensitivity**\n| Condition | Treatment |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| A | All steps on ice (4°C) |\n| B | Room temperature during labeling |\n| C | 37°C during labeling (positive control for damage) |\n\n**2B. Enzyme/Buffer Toxicity**\n| Condition | RBC Lysis Time | EDTA Concentration |\n|-----------|---------------|-------------------|\n| A | 2 min | 2 mM (standard) |\n| B | 5 min | 2 mM |\n| C | 2 min | 10 mM |\n| D | No RBC lysis (lymph nodes only) |\n\n**2C. Mechanical Stress**\n| Condition | Dissociation Method |\n|-----------|--------------------|\n| A | Gentle plunger grinding |\n| B | Vigorous pipetting |\n| C | Enzymatic (collagenase D, 30 min) |\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 3: Purity Investigation**\n\n**3A. Antibody Cocktail Titration**\n- Test 0.5×, 1×, 2× recommended antibody concentration\n- Measure contamination (T cells: CD3⁺, myeloid: CD11b⁺)\n\n**3B. Column Overloading**\n| Cells per LS Column | Expected Purity |\n|--------------------|-----------------|\n| 5 × 10⁷ | >95% |\n| 1 × 10⁸ | 90-95% |\n| 2 × 10⁸ | <85% (overloaded) |\n\n**3C. Alternative Method Comparison**\n- Compare negative selection vs. **positive selection** (anti-B220 beads)\n- Compare MACS vs. **FACS sorting** (gold standard for purity)\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 4: Culture Condition Optimization**\n\n**4A. β-Mercaptoethanol Titration**\n| Concentration | Viability at 48h |\n|--------------|------------------|\n| 0 μM | Expected: poor |\n| 25 μM | Suboptimal |\n| 50 μM | Standard |\n| 100 μM | Test toxicity |\n\n**4B. Serum Batch Testing**\n- Test 3 different FBS lots\n- Some batches contain B cell toxins\n\n**4C. Endotoxin Contamination**\n- Use endotoxin-free reagents\n- Test LAL assay on medium components\n- Unintended TLR4 activation causes activation-induced cell death\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 5: Mouse-Related Variables**\n\n**5A. Age Effects**\n| Age | Expected Viability |\n|-----|-------------------|\n| 4-6 weeks | High |\n| 8-12 weeks | Standard |\n| >16 weeks | Lower (immunosenescence) |\n\n**5B. Health Status**\n- Specific pathogen-free (SPF) vs. conventional\n- Check for subclinical infections\n\n**5C. Genetic Background**\n- C57BL/6 vs. BALB/c (different B cell biology)\n\n---\n\n### **Root Cause Decision Tree**\n\n```\nLow Viability\n├─ If death during isolation:\n│  ├─ RBC lysis too long → Reduce to 2 min, RT only\n│  ├─ Temperature fluctuation → Keep everything cold\n│  └─ Mechanical stress → Gentle dissociation\n│\n└─ If death during culture:\n   ├─ Missing β-mercaptoethanol → Add 50 μM\n   ├─ Endotoxin contamination → Use ultra-pure reagents\n   ├─ Serum batch issue → Test alternative lots\n   └─ Overstimulation → Reduce LPS/cytokine concentration\n\nLow Purity\n├─ If T cell contamination:\n│  ├─ Insufficient CD4/CD8 depletion → Increase antibody\n│  └─ Column overloaded → Use fewer cells per column\n│\n├─ If myeloid contamination:\n│  └─ Inadequate CD11b depletion → Check antibody activity\n│\n└─ If dead cells present:\n   └─ Add viability dye sorting or dead cell removal kit\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Immediate Actions to Implement**\n\n1. **Always work cold** (4°C) during isolation - warm temperatures activate B cells and reduce viability\n2. **Minimize RBC lysis time** - 2-3 minutes maximum at room temperature\n3. **Include β-mercaptoethanol** (50 μM) in ALL culture media\n4. **Use fresh reagents** - old antibodies lose activity, reducing purity\n5. **Don't overload columns** - maximum 1 × 10⁸ cells per LS column\n6. **Add DNase I** (10 μg/mL) during tissue dissociation to prevent clumping\n7. **Pre-block Fc receptors** - reduces non-specific binding\n8. **Consider FACS** if purity remains <85% - sort for B220⁺ IgD⁺ CD43⁻\n\n---\n\n### **Quality Control Checklist**\n\nBefore each experiment, verify:\n- [ ] MACS buffer is cold and fresh (≤1 week old)\n- [ ] FBS batch tested for B cell culture\n- [ ] β-mercaptoethanol added to medium (prepare fresh weekly)\n- [ ] Antibodies not expired\n- [ ] Columns properly equilibrated\n- [ ] Viability >90% before loading onto column\n- [ ] Post-sort purity check by flow cytometry\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to elaborate on any specific aspect of the protocol or troubleshooting plan? I can also provide guidance on:\n- Specific differentiation readouts (ELISA for antibody secretion, qPCR for transcription factors)\n- Alternative isolation methods (FACS, density gradient)\n- Downstream applications (RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, functional assays)", "time": {"start": 1773730532509, "end": 1773730532509}}, {"id": "prt_cfa940c9f001zKiPX7L8O0wX7D", "sessionID": "ses_305700dd3ffepJnR1ycTZWsWo7", "messageID": "msg_cfa908444001GbLNF8pMEgQaTZ", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 30786, "output": 3287, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "The academic papers focus on B cell biology theory rather than practical protocols. Let me provide you with comprehensive experimental protocols based on established immunological methods:\n\n---\n\n## **Complete Protocol: Murine Naïve B Cell Purification via Negative Selection and In Vitro Differentiation**\n\n### **Part 1: Naïve B Cell Isolation by Negative Selection**\n\n#### **Materials Required**\n\n**Tissue & Reagents:**\n- Spleen and/or lymph nodes from mice (6-12 weeks old)\n- MACS buffer: PBS + 2% FBS + 2 mM EDTA, pH 7.2, sterile-filtered\n- Red blood cell (RBC) lysis buffer (ammonium chloride-based)\n- Murine Naïve B Cell Isolation Kit (negative selection) - commercial options:\n  - Miltenyi Biotec: B Cell Isolation Kit II, mouse (cat# 130-090-862)\n  - STEMCELL Technologies: EasySep™ Mouse B Cell Isolation Kit (cat# 19854)\n  - BioLegend: MojoSort™ Mouse Pan B Cell Isolation Kit\n- Fluorochrome-conjugated antibodies for phenotyping (optional):\n  - anti-B220 (RA3-6B2), anti-IgD, anti-CD43, anti-CD5, anti-CD138\n\n**Equipment:**\n- MACS columns (LS or LD columns, Miltenyi)\n- MACS separator magnet\n- 70 μm cell strainers\n- 15 mL and 50 mL conical tubes\n- Hemocytometer or automated cell counter\n- Flow cytometer (for purity assessment)\n\n---\n\n#### **Step-by-Step Protocol**\n\n**Day 0: Tissue Harvest and Single Cell Suspension**\n\n1. **Euthanize mouse** following approved IACUC protocol\n2. **Harvest spleen and/or lymph nodes** (inguinal, axillary, brachial, mesenteric)\n   - Place tissue in cold MACS buffer on ice\n   - Work quickly to maintain viability\n\n3. **Prepare single-cell suspension:**\n   - Place tissue on 70 μm cell strainer in 15 mL conical tube\n   - Gently dissociate with plunger end of syringe\n   - Rinse strainer with 5-10 mL cold MACS buffer\n   - Centrifuge at 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n\n4. **RBC lysis (for spleen):**\n   - Resuspend pellet in 2-5 mL RBC lysis buffer\n   - Incubate 2-3 min at room temperature (NOT on ice)\n   - Quench with 10 mL MACS buffer\n   - Centrifuge 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n   - Repeat if necessary until pellet is white\n\n5. **Filter and count:**\n   - Pass through 70 μm strainer again to remove clumps\n   - Count viable cells with trypan blue\n   - Expected yield: 5-8 × 10⁷ cells/spleen\n\n---\n\n**Negative Selection (MACS)**\n\n6. **Fc receptor blocking:**\n   - Resuspend cells at 10⁸ cells/mL in MACS buffer\n   - Add FcR blocking reagent (10 μL per 10⁷ cells)\n   - Incubate 10 min, 4°C\n\n7. **Biotin antibody cocktail incubation:**\n   - Add biotinylated antibody cocktail (targets: CD4, CD8, CD43, CD11b, CD11c, CD49b, CD90.2, Ter-119, IgD⁺ for some kits)\n   - 100 μL per 10⁷ cells\n   - Incubate 10 min, 4°C\n\n8. **Magnetic bead labeling:**\n   - Add anti-biotin microbeads (20 μL per 10⁷ cells)\n   - Incubate 15 min, 4°C\n   - Wash with 10 mL MACS buffer\n   - Centrifuge 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n\n9. **Column separation:**\n   - Place LS column in magnetic separator\n   - Equilibrate with 3 mL MACS buffer\n   - Resuspend cells in 3 mL MACS buffer\n   - Apply cell suspension to column\n   - Collect **flow-through** (this contains naïve B cells)\n   - Wash column 3× with 3 mL MACS buffer\n   - Combine all flow-through fractions\n\n10. **Final processing:**\n    - Centrifuge flow-through 300 × g, 4°C, 5 min\n    - Resuspend in appropriate culture medium\n    - Count and assess viability\n\n---\n\n### **Part 2: In Vitro B Cell Differentiation**\n\n#### **Culture Medium**\n- RPMI 1640 + 10% FBS (heat-inactivated)\n- 2 mM L-glutamine\n- 1 mM sodium pyruvate\n- 10 mM HEPES\n- 100 U/mL penicillin/streptomycin\n- 50 μM β-mercaptoethanol (critical for B cell survival)\n\n#### **Differentiation Conditions**\n\n| **Stimulation** | **Reagent** | **Final Concentration** | **Outcome** |\n|-----------------|-------------|------------------------|-------------|\n| **T-independent** | LPS (E. coli O111:B4) | 10-25 μg/mL | Polyclonal activation, plasmablast differentiation |\n| **BCR stimulation** | F(ab')₂ anti-IgM | 10 μg/mL | BCR crosslinking |\n| **T-dependent mimic** | anti-CD40 + IL-4 | 1 μg/mL + 20 ng/mL | GC-like differentiation |\n| **Plasma cell** | anti-CD40 + IL-21 | 1 μg/mL + 50 ng/mL | Plasmablast/plasma cell |\n| **Class switch** | LPS + IL-4 | 10 μg/mL + 20 ng/mL | IgG1 switching |\n\n#### **Culture Protocol**\n\n1. **Resuspend naïve B cells** at 0.5-1 × 10⁶ cells/mL in complete medium\n2. **Plate in 96-well U-bottom or 24-well plates**\n   - 200 μL/well (96-well) or 1 mL/well (24-well)\n3. **Add stimuli** as appropriate for differentiation goal\n4. **Incubate** 37°C, 5% CO₂, humidified\n5. **Feed every 2-3 days:**\n   - Gently resuspend cells\n   - Remove half the medium\n   - Add fresh complete medium with cytokines\n6. **Harvest** at desired timepoint:\n   - Day 1-2: Early activation (CD69, CD86 upregulation)\n   - Day 3-4: Proliferation peak (CFSE dilution)\n   - Day 5-7: Plasmablast differentiation (CD138⁺, Blimp-1)\n\n---\n\n### **Part 3: Quality Control**\n\n#### **Purity Assessment (Flow Cytometry)**\nStain with:\n- **B220⁺ CD19⁺** (B cell markers) - should be >90%\n- **IgD⁺ CD23⁺ CD43⁻ CD5⁻** (naïve phenotype)\n- **CD4⁻ CD8⁻ CD11b⁻** (exclusion markers)\n\n#### **Viability Assessment**\n- Trypan blue exclusion\n- 7-AAD or propidium iodide by flow cytometry\n- Annexin V/PI for apoptosis\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## **Troubleshooting Experimental Plan: Low Viability and Purity**\n\n### **Systematic Investigation Design**\n\nI'll design a comprehensive troubleshooting plan addressing both issues:\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 1: Identify Stage of Cell Loss**\n\n**Objective:** Determine whether cell death occurs during isolation or post-culture\n\n**Design:**\n```\nTrack cell number and viability at each step:\n├─ Starting tissue (post-RBC lysis)\n├─ Post-Fc block\n├─ Post-antibody labeling\n├─ Post-magnetic separation (flow-through)\n└─ 24h post-culture (unstimulated)\n```\n\n**Expected outcome:** Pinpoint whether loss is mechanical/enzymatic (isolation) or culture-related\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 2: Test Critical Variables Affecting Viability**\n\n**2A. Temperature Sensitivity**\n| Condition | Treatment |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| A | All steps on ice (4°C) |\n| B | Room temperature during labeling |\n| C | 37°C during labeling (positive control for damage) |\n\n**2B. Enzyme/Buffer Toxicity**\n| Condition | RBC Lysis Time | EDTA Concentration |\n|-----------|---------------|-------------------|\n| A | 2 min | 2 mM (standard) |\n| B | 5 min | 2 mM |\n| C | 2 min | 10 mM |\n| D | No RBC lysis (lymph nodes only) |\n\n**2C. Mechanical Stress**\n| Condition | Dissociation Method |\n|-----------|--------------------|\n| A | Gentle plunger grinding |\n| B | Vigorous pipetting |\n| C | Enzymatic (collagenase D, 30 min) |\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 3: Purity Investigation**\n\n**3A. Antibody Cocktail Titration**\n- Test 0.5×, 1×, 2× recommended antibody concentration\n- Measure contamination (T cells: CD3⁺, myeloid: CD11b⁺)\n\n**3B. Column Overloading**\n| Cells per LS Column | Expected Purity |\n|--------------------|-----------------|\n| 5 × 10⁷ | >95% |\n| 1 × 10⁸ | 90-95% |\n| 2 × 10⁸ | <85% (overloaded) |\n\n**3C. Alternative Method Comparison**\n- Compare negative selection vs. **positive selection** (anti-B220 beads)\n- Compare MACS vs. **FACS sorting** (gold standard for purity)\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 4: Culture Condition Optimization**\n\n**4A. β-Mercaptoethanol Titration**\n| Concentration | Viability at 48h |\n|--------------|------------------|\n| 0 μM | Expected: poor |\n| 25 μM | Suboptimal |\n| 50 μM | Standard |\n| 100 μM | Test toxicity |\n\n**4B. Serum Batch Testing**\n- Test 3 different FBS lots\n- Some batches contain B cell toxins\n\n**4C. Endotoxin Contamination**\n- Use endotoxin-free reagents\n- Test LAL assay on medium components\n- Unintended TLR4 activation causes activation-induced cell death\n\n---\n\n### **Experiment 5: Mouse-Related Variables**\n\n**5A. Age Effects**\n| Age | Expected Viability |\n|-----|-------------------|\n| 4-6 weeks | High |\n| 8-12 weeks | Standard |\n| >16 weeks | Lower (immunosenescence) |\n\n**5B. Health Status**\n- Specific pathogen-free (SPF) vs. conventional\n- Check for subclinical infections\n\n**5C. Genetic Background**\n- C57BL/6 vs. BALB/c (different B cell biology)\n\n---\n\n### **Root Cause Decision Tree**\n\n```\nLow Viability\n├─ If death during isolation:\n│  ├─ RBC lysis too long → Reduce to 2 min, RT only\n│  ├─ Temperature fluctuation → Keep everything cold\n│  └─ Mechanical stress → Gentle dissociation\n│\n└─ If death during culture:\n   ├─ Missing β-mercaptoethanol → Add 50 μM\n   ├─ Endotoxin contamination → Use ultra-pure reagents\n   ├─ Serum batch issue → Test alternative lots\n   └─ Overstimulation → Reduce LPS/cytokine concentration\n\nLow Purity\n├─ If T cell contamination:\n│  ├─ Insufficient CD4/CD8 depletion → Increase antibody\n│  └─ Column overloaded → Use fewer cells per column\n│\n├─ If myeloid contamination:\n│  └─ Inadequate CD11b depletion → Check antibody activity\n│\n└─ If dead cells present:\n   └─ Add viability dye sorting or dead cell removal kit\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Immediate Actions to Implement**\n\n1. **Always work cold** (4°C) during isolation - warm temperatures activate B cells and reduce viability\n2. **Minimize RBC lysis time** - 2-3 minutes maximum at room temperature\n3. **Include β-mercaptoethanol** (50 μM) in ALL culture media\n4. **Use fresh reagents** - old antibodies lose activity, reducing purity\n5. **Don't overload columns** - maximum 1 × 10⁸ cells per LS column\n6. **Add DNase I** (10 μg/mL) during tissue dissociation to prevent clumping\n7. **Pre-block Fc receptors** - reduces non-specific binding\n8. **Consider FACS** if purity remains <85% - sort for B220⁺ IgD⁺ CD43⁻\n\n---\n\n### **Quality Control Checklist**\n\nBefore each experiment, verify:\n- [ ] MACS buffer is cold and fresh (≤1 week old)\n- [ ] FBS batch tested for B cell culture\n- [ ] β-mercaptoethanol added to medium (prepare fresh weekly)\n- [ ] Antibodies not expired\n- [ ] Columns properly equilibrated\n- [ ] Viability >90% before loading onto column\n- [ ] Post-sort purity check by flow cytometry\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to elaborate on any specific aspect of the protocol or troubleshooting plan? I can also provide guidance on:\n- Specific differentiation readouts (ELISA for antibody secretion, qPCR for transcription factors)\n- Alternative isolation methods (FACS, density gradient)\n- Downstream applications (RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, functional assays)", "case_id": 6120, "language": "global", "tags": {"topics": ["Natural Sciences", "Biology", "Molecular Biology"], "time_sensitivity": {"time_sensitivity": "Time-agnostic", "year_month": "NA", "day": "NA"}}, "rubric_count": 15}, "metrics": {"solve_time_seconds": 271.5, "tokens": {"input": 82519, "output": 3699, "total": 86218}, "cost": 0.0, "step_count": 3}, "error": ""}}
{"task_id": "industry/5768/global", "answer": "# GPON Service Degradation Analysis\n\n## 1. Fault Localization\n\n### Root Cause Determination\n\n**Primary Issue: Marginal Optical Signal Quality + Peak-Hour Congestion**\n\nThe evidence points to a **physical layer degradation** manifesting during peak usage hours:\n\n| Indicator | Value | Status | Implication |\n|-----------|-------|--------|-------------|\n| ONU Rx Power | -24.5 dBm | ⚠️ Marginal | Near lower threshold (-27~-28 dBm) |\n| OLT Rx Power | -21.0 dBm | ✓ Acceptable | Within normal range |\n| BIP Errors | Increasing | ✗ Abnormal | Physical layer bit errors |\n| Speedtest | >900 Mbps | ✓ Pass | Bandwidth capacity intact |\n| TCP Retransmissions | High | ✗ Abnormal | Packet loss triggering retransmission |\n\n**Most Probable Root Cause:**\n\nThe -24.5 dBm receive power at the ONU is operating at the **edge of the acceptable range**. During peak hours (14:00-16:00), this marginal signal quality becomes critical due to:\n\n1. **Increased splitter port contention** (more active ONTs on the 1:64 splitter)\n2. **Temperature-induced optical drift** (afternoon heat affecting fiber characteristics)\n3. **Accumulated BIP errors** causing packet corruption → TCP retransmissions → latency spikes\n\n---\n\n## 2. In-Depth Analysis\n\n### 2.1 Optical Power ↔ BIP Errors ↔ Service Performance Correlation\n\n```\nMarginal Rx Power (-24.5 dBm)\n        ↓\nReduced Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR)\n        ↓\nIncreased Bit Error Rate during peak contention\n        ↓\nBIP Error Counter Increment (GEM port level)\n        ↓\nCorrupted GEM Frames → Discarded at ONU/OLT\n        ↓\nTCP Packet Loss → Retransmission Triggered\n        ↓\nIncreased RTT + Jitter → Application-layer stuttering\n```\n\n**Why BIP errors matter:**\n- BIP (Bit Interleaved Parity) is the GPON physical layer error detection mechanism\n- Continuous increment during 14:00-16:00 indicates **time-correlated degradation**\n- Each BIP error represents corrupted bits that must be retransmitted at the TCP layer\n\n### 2.2 Why Speedtest Passes But Interactive Services Fail\n\n| Metric | Single-Thread Speedtest | Web/Video Conferencing |\n|--------|------------------------|------------------------|\n| **What it measures** | Sustained throughput over 10-30s | Real-time responsiveness |\n| **Tolerance to loss** | High (TCP retransmission invisible) | Low (visible as stutter) |\n| **Jitter sensitivity** | Low (averaged over time) | Critical (>30ms causes freeze) |\n| **Packet timing** | Bulk transfer, burst-tolerant | Isochronous, timing-critical |\n\n**Key insight:** A single-thread Speedtest can achieve 900+ Mbps even with 5-10% packet loss because:\n- TCP congestion control recovers throughput over the test duration\n- Retransmissions add latency but not reduce final throughput measurement\n- No real-time deadline to meet\n\nVideo conferencing and web browsing fail because:\n- **Video**: Jitter >30-50ms causes buffer underrun → freeze\n- **Web**: TCP retransmission timeout (RTO) delays page object fetch → perceived as \"slow\"\n- **One-way jitter** (observed in Wireshark) indicates asymmetric path degradation\n\n---\n\n## 3. Optimization Recommendations\n\n### 3.1 Physical Layer Remediation (Priority: HIGH)\n\n**Immediate actions:**\n\n```bash\n# 1. Verify optical threshold configuration on OLT\ndisplay ont autofind all\ndisplay ont info <ont-id>\n\n# 2. Check current optical alarm thresholds\ndisplay ont optical-threshold <ont-id>\n\n# Expected thresholds (adjust if needed):\n#   Lower receive threshold: -27 dBm (current -24.5 is too close)\n#   Upper receive threshold: -8 dBm\n```\n\n**Physical interventions:**\n\n| Action | Rationale |\n|--------|-----------|\n| Clean fiber connectors at ONU and OLT | Contamination causes additional 1-3 dB loss |\n| Inspect optical splitter port for damage | Faulty port can cause intermittent loss |\n| Consider reducing split ratio (1:64 → 1:32) | Adds 3 dB margin to optical budget |\n| Replace ONU if optical module aging | Transmit power drift indicates potential degradation |\n\n**Target optical power after remediation:**\n- ONU Rx: **-18 to -22 dBm** (add 3-6 dB margin)\n- OLT Rx: **-15 to -20 dBm**\n\n### 3.2 OLT-Side Parameter Inspection\n\n```bash\n# 1. Check DBA profile configuration\ndisplay dba-profile all\n\n# Verify Type 4 profile settings:\n#   - Maximum bandwidth: 1000000 kbps (1000M)\n#   - Ensure no unintended rate limiting\n\n# 2. Check traffic profile / service priority\ndisplay traffic-profile all\n\n# Priority 5 is correct for best-effort, but verify:\n#   - No policer dropping packets during congestion\n#   - WRR/SP scheduling not starving priority 5\n\n# 3. Monitor real-time error statistics during peak hours\ndisplay ont error-statistics <ont-id> verbose\n# Watch for:\n#   - BIP errors incrementing\n#   - FEC correction count (if available)\n#   - Dying Gasp events\n\n# 4. Check OLT uplink utilization during 14:00-16:00\ndisplay interface gpon <slot>/<port>\ndisplay board <slot>\n\n# 5. Verify no broadcast storm or MAC flapping\ndisplay mac-address ont <ont-id>\n```\n\n### 3.3 Diagnostic Script Logic (Automated Monitoring)\n\n```bash\n# Pseudo-script for peak-hour monitoring\n# Run every 5 minutes during 13:00-17:00\n\nwhile [ $(date +%H) -ge 13 ] && [ $(date +%H) -lt 17 ]; do\n    # Capture optical power\n    ONU_RX=$(display ont optical-info <ont-id> | grep \"Receive optical power\")\n    OLT_RX=$(display ont info | grep \"OLT receive optical power\")\n    \n    # Capture BIP error count\n    BIP_COUNT=$(display ont error-statistics <ont-id> | grep \"BIP error\")\n    \n    # Log with timestamp\n    echo \"$(date): ONU_RX=$ONU_RX, OLT_RX=$OLT_RX, BIP=$BIP_COUNT\" >> /var/log/gpon_monitor.log\n    \n    # Alert if degradation detected\n    if [ $BIP_DELTA -gt 100 ]; then\n        echo \"ALERT: BIP error spike detected at $(date)\" | mail -s \"GPON Degradation\" noc@company.com\n    fi\n    \n    sleep 300\ndone\n```\n\n### 3.4 QoS Optimization (If Physical Layer Cannot Be Immediately Improved)\n\n```bash\n# 1. Upgrade service priority from 5 to 3 or 4 for video conferencing traffic\n#    (requires traffic classification based on DSCP/port)\n\n# 2. Configure traffic shaping to smooth bursts\ntraffic-shape <profile-id> \n  cir 900000\n  pir 1000000\n  queue-length 1024\n\n# 3. Enable FEC (Forward Error Correction) if supported\n#    Adds redundancy to correct bit errors without retransmission\n```\n\n---\n\n## Summary & Action Plan\n\n| Priority | Action | Expected Outcome |\n|----------|--------|------------------|\n| **P0** | Clean/replace fiber connectors, verify optical path | Improve Rx power by 2-4 dB |\n| **P0** | Monitor BIP errors during peak hours with script | Quantify error correlation |\n| **P1** | If optical margin cannot be improved, reduce split ratio | Add 3 dB optical budget |\n| **P1** | Review OLT uplink utilization during 14:00-16:00 | Rule out backbone congestion |\n| **P2** | Upgrade traffic priority for real-time services | Reduce queuing delay |\n\n**Expected resolution:** After improving optical margin to -20 dBm or better, BIP errors should stabilize, TCP retransmissions will decrease, and interactive service quality will normalize even during peak hours.", "score": {"rubric_results": [{"rubric_number": 1, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型明确指出 -24.5 dBm 处于可接受范围边缘，接近下限阈值（-27~-28 dBm），符合评分标准。"}, {"rubric_number": 2, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型将 14:00-16:00 时间段与温度升高联系起来，并推断会导致光漂移或光纤特性变化，符合逻辑推断要求。"}, {"rubric_number": 3, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型明确将 BIP 错误计数器的持续增加识别为物理层比特错误的直接证据，并解释了其含义。"}, {"rubric_number": 4, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型仅在建议中提到启用 FEC 作为修复手段，未在分析中解释 FEC 机制本身如何引入处理延迟并放大抖动。"}, {"rubric_number": 5, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型详细解释了单线程 Speedtest 通过 TCP 重传掩盖丢包从而测速达标，而交互业务对延迟和抖动敏感导致体验差的区别。"}, {"rubric_number": 6, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型未在分析部分明确指出 Type 4 DBA 配置文件在 1:64 分光下强制用户竞争时隙导致无法保证延迟的具体行为机制。"}, {"rubric_number": 7, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型建议清洁光纤连接器，但未明确提及检查客户 premises 的冷接端面（cold-splice end-face）这一具体物理层细节。"}, {"rubric_number": 8, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型明确建议将 ONU 接收光功率优化至 -18 到 -22 dBm（目标 -20 dBm），以提供足够的安全余量。"}, {"rubric_number": 9, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型建议升级业务优先级（Priority 5 到 3 或 4），但未明确建议将 DBA 配置文件类型从 Type 4 更改为 Type 3 或 Type 2。"}, {"rubric_number": 10, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型未提供创建新 DBA 配置文件的具体命令逻辑，缺少包含 `assure` 和 `max` 参数的配置示例。"}, {"rubric_number": 11, "weight": 3, "met": false, "reason": "模型未提供修改 ONU T-CONT 绑定的具体命令（如 `ont modify ... tcont ...`）及 commit 操作。"}, {"rubric_number": 12, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型建议更改优先级数值，未提供确保优先级 5 流量进入高优先级队列或 Gemport 的具体配置逻辑。"}, {"rubric_number": 13, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型关于 BIP 和 Speedtest 原理的解释紧扣故障分析任务，属于必要的深度分析，不属于无关的冗余教育内容。"}, {"rubric_number": 14, "weight": -8, "met": false, "reason": "模型将物理层光路优化列为 P0 最高优先级，未出现将修改 DBA 或重启设备置于光路排查之前的主次颠倒情况。"}, {"rubric_number": 15, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型提供了 `display ont info`、`display ont optical-info`、`display dba-profile` 等具体的 OLT 侧参数查询命令。"}, {"rubric_number": 16, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型的排查建议遵循了从物理层到逻辑层、从简单（清洁）到复杂（改分光比）的正确顺序。"}, {"rubric_number": 17, "weight": -8, "met": true, "reason": "模型结论仅提到错误稳定、重传减少等定性描述，未提供如抖动<30ms、丢包率<0.5% 等具体的量化验收标准。"}], "score": 32, "max_score": 73, "raw_response": "[\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 1,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型明确指出 -24.5 dBm 处于可接受范围边缘，接近下限阈值（-27~-28 dBm），符合评分标准。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 2,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型将 14:00-16:00 时间段与温度升高联系起来，并推断会导致光漂移或光纤特性变化，符合逻辑推断要求。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 3,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型明确将 BIP 错误计数器的持续增加识别为物理层比特错误的直接证据，并解释了其含义。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 4,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型仅在建议中提到启用 FEC 作为修复手段，未在分析中解释 FEC 机制本身如何引入处理延迟并放大抖动。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 5,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型详细解释了单线程 Speedtest 通过 TCP 重传掩盖丢包从而测速达标，而交互业务对延迟和抖动敏感导致体验差的区别。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 6,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未在分析部分明确指出 Type 4 DBA 配置文件在 1:64 分光下强制用户竞争时隙导致无法保证延迟的具体行为机制。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 7,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型建议清洁光纤连接器，但未明确提及检查客户 premises 的冷接端面（cold-splice end-face）这一具体物理层细节。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 8,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型明确建议将 ONU 接收光功率优化至 -18 到 -22 dBm（目标 -20 dBm），以提供足够的安全余量。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 9,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型建议升级业务优先级（Priority 5 到 3 或 4），但未明确建议将 DBA 配置文件类型从 Type 4 更改为 Type 3 或 Type 2。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 10,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未提供创建新 DBA 配置文件的具体命令逻辑，缺少包含 `assure` 和 `max` 参数的配置示例。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 11,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未提供修改 ONU T-CONT 绑定的具体命令（如 `ont modify ... tcont ...`）及 commit 操作。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 12,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型建议更改优先级数值，未提供确保优先级 5 流量进入高优先级队列或 Gemport 的具体配置逻辑。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 13,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型关于 BIP 和 Speedtest 原理的解释紧扣故障分析任务，属于必要的深度分析，不属于无关的冗余教育内容。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 14,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型将物理层光路优化列为 P0 最高优先级，未出现将修改 DBA 或重启设备置于光路排查之前的主次颠倒情况。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 15,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型提供了 `display ont info`、`display ont optical-info`、`display dba-profile` 等具体的 OLT 侧参数查询命令。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 16,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型的排查建议遵循了从物理层到逻辑层、从简单（清洁）到复杂（改分光比）的正确顺序。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 17,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型结论仅提到错误稳定、重传减少等定性描述，未提供如抖动<30ms、丢包率<0.5% 等具体的量化验收标准。\"\n  }\n]"}, "error": null, "stage": null, "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "user_prompt": "A government–enterprise leased line customer reports that their 1000M bandwidth service delivered over GPON experiences severe web page response latency and frequent video conferencing freezes every day between 14:00 and 16:00. However, when a single-thread Speedtest is performed, the downstream rate exceeds 900 Mbps.\n\nCurrent network environment and measured indicators:\n1. Topology:\n   OLT (MA5800) -> Optical splitter (1:64) -> ONU\n2. Optical path indicators:\n   ONU receive optical power: -24.5dBm; OLT-side receive optical power: -21.0dBm.\n3. ONU status:\n   `display ont info` shows the ONT state is Up.\n   `display ont optical-info` shows the upstream transmit optical power is 2.5dBm.\n   `display ont error-statistics` shows that the BIP error counter increases slowly but continuously during the affected time window.\n4. Bandwidth configuration:\n   DBA profile type is Type 4 (maximum bandwidth 1000M); the service flow uses priority 5.\n5. User-side observations:\n   Wireshark packet captures taken by the user show that the stuttering is accompanied by a large number of TCP Retransmissions and high one-way jitter.\n\nYour tasks are:\n1. Fault localization:\n   Analyze why the user experiences service stuttering even though the speed test result meets the bandwidth specification, and determine the most probable root cause based on the provided indicators.\n2. In-depth analysis:\n   Explain how the specific optical power levels and BIP error counts are correlated with service performance.\n   Explain why a single-thread download speed test can pass while the user experience of interactive services is extremely poor.\n3. Optimization recommendations:\n   Propose concrete troubleshooting and remediation schemes, including physical-layer adjustment suggestions and OLT-side parameter inspection commands or script logic.", "response": {"request_id": "omb-batch", "status": "completed", "session_id": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "workspace": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__5768__global/omb-batch", "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "final_prompt": "A government–enterprise leased line customer reports that their 1000M bandwidth service delivered over GPON experiences severe web page response latency and frequent video conferencing freezes every day between 14:00 and 16:00. However, when a single-thread Speedtest is performed, the downstream rate exceeds 900 Mbps.\n\nCurrent network environment and measured indicators:\n1. Topology:\n   OLT (MA5800) -> Optical splitter (1:64) -> ONU\n2. Optical path indicators:\n   ONU receive optical power: -24.5dBm; OLT-side receive optical power: -21.0dBm.\n3. ONU status:\n   `display ont info` shows the ONT state is Up.\n   `display ont optical-info` shows the upstream transmit optical power is 2.5dBm.\n   `display ont error-statistics` shows that the BIP error counter increases slowly but continuously during the affected time window.\n4. Bandwidth configuration:\n   DBA profile type is Type 4 (maximum bandwidth 1000M); the service flow uses priority 5.\n5. User-side observations:\n   Wireshark packet captures taken by the user show that the stuttering is accompanied by a large number of TCP Retransmissions and high one-way jitter.\n\nYour tasks are:\n1. Fault localization:\n   Analyze why the user experiences service stuttering even though the speed test result meets the bandwidth specification, and determine the most probable root cause based on the provided indicators.\n2. In-depth analysis:\n   Explain how the specific optical power levels and BIP error counts are correlated with service performance.\n   Explain why a single-thread download speed test can pass while the user experience of interactive services is extremely poor.\n3. Optimization recommendations:\n   Propose concrete troubleshooting and remediation schemes, including physical-layer adjustment suggestions and OLT-side parameter inspection commands or script logic.", "session_data": {"scope_id": "09e589e8a075fffd058be68467715e3bb60e39a9", "session_id": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "info": {"id": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "version": "local", "scopeID": "09e589e8a075fffd058be68467715e3bb60e39a9", "directory": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__5768__global/omb-batch", "title": "onemillion:industry/5768/global", "time": {"created": 1773730387428, "updated": 1773730499961}, "summary": {"additions": 0, "deletions": 0, "files": 0}}, "messages": [{"id": "msg_cfa91d5f8001v8DAqxGlq03Uvl", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa91d5f8001v8DAqxGlq03Uvl", "sessionID": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "role": "user", "time": {"created": 1773730387448}, "summary": {"title": "Diagnosing service latency and video freezes on leased line", "diffs": []}, "agent": "synergy", "model": {"providerID": "sii-holos", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B"}}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa91d5fa001Uz0svZXXXGo2lj", "sessionID": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "messageID": "msg_cfa91d5f8001v8DAqxGlq03Uvl", "type": "text", "text": "\"A government–enterprise leased line customer reports that their 1000M bandwidth service delivered over GPON experiences severe web page response latency and frequent video conferencing freezes every day between 14:00 and 16:00. However, when a single-thread Speedtest is performed, the downstream rate exceeds 900 Mbps.\n\nCurrent network environment and measured indicators:\n1. Topology:\n   OLT (MA5800) -> Optical splitter (1:64) -> ONU\n2. Optical path indicators:\n   ONU receive optical power: -24.5dBm; OLT-side receive optical power: -21.0dBm.\n3. ONU status:\n   `display ont info` shows the ONT state is Up.\n   `display ont optical-info` shows the upstream transmit optical power is 2.5dBm.\n   `display ont error-statistics` shows that the BIP error counter increases slowly but continuously during the affected time window.\n4. Bandwidth configuration:\n   DBA profile type is Type 4 (maximum bandwidth 1000M); the service flow uses priority 5.\n5. User-side observations:\n   Wireshark packet captures taken by the user show that the stuttering is accompanied by a large number of TCP Retransmissions and high one-way jitter.\n\nYour tasks are:\n1. Fault localization:\n   Analyze why the user experiences service stuttering even though the speed test result meets the bandwidth specification, and determine the most probable root cause based on the provided indicators.\n2. In-depth analysis:\n   Explain how the specific optical power levels and BIP error counts are correlated with service performance.\n   Explain why a single-thread download speed test can pass while the user experience of interactive services is extremely poor.\n3. Optimization recommendations:\n   Propose concrete troubleshooting and remediation schemes, including physical-layer adjustment suggestions and OLT-side parameter inspection commands or script logic.\"\n"}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa91d6260015NsfG6HGDzIw0d", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa91d6260015NsfG6HGDzIw0d", "sessionID": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730387494, "completed": 1773730499954}, "parentID": "msg_cfa91d5f8001v8DAqxGlq03Uvl", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__5768__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__5768__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26083, "output": 2454, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa91e155001oPjc3WuB4fKIh4", "sessionID": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "messageID": "msg_cfa91d6260015NsfG6HGDzIw0d", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa91e156001fWA6t8QZtSBCpr", "sessionID": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "messageID": "msg_cfa91d6260015NsfG6HGDzIw0d", "type": "reasoning", "text": "This is a technical troubleshooting task about a GPON network issue. Let me analyze the problem systematically.\n\nThe user is asking me to:\n1. Analyze why service stuttering occurs despite good speed test results\n2. Explain the correlation between optical power levels, BIP errors, and service performance\n3. Provide concrete troubleshooting and remediation recommendations\n\nThis is a research/analysis task that requires technical expertise in GPON networks. Let me work through this systematically.\n\nKey observations from the problem:\n1. 1000M bandwidth service over GPON\n2. Severe latency 14:00-16:00 daily (peak hours)\n3. Single-thread Speedtest shows >900 Mbps downstream (good)\n4. But web pages slow, video conferencing freezes\n5. TCP Retransmissions and high one-way jitter observed\n\nNetwork indicators:\n1. ONU receive optical power: -24.5dBm (this is at the edge of acceptable range)\n2. OLT receive optical power: -21.0dBm (this is acceptable)\n3. ONU transmit optical power: 2.5dBm (normal)\n4. BIP error counter increases continuously during affected time window\n5. DBA profile Type 4, priority 5\n\nLet me analyze:\n\n**Optical Power Analysis:**\n- GPON typical receive power range: -8 to -27 dBm (some specs say -28 dBm)\n- -24.5dBm is at the lower edge of acceptable range\n- This indicates marginal optical signal quality\n\n**BIP Errors:**\n- BIP (Bit Interleaved Parity) errors indicate physical layer bit errors\n- Continuous increase during peak hours suggests intermittent signal degradation\n- This correlates with the 14:00-16:00 timeframe (likely congestion or environmental factors)\n\n**Why Speedtest passes but user experience is poor:**\n1. Speedtest measures throughput over time, tolerating some packet loss/retransmission\n\n\n2. Interactive services like web browsing and video calls demand low latency and minimal jitter\n3. TCP retransmissions inflate round-trip times, which throughput tests don't penalize heavily\n4. High jitter specifically degrades real-time applications more than bulk data transfers\n\n**Root cause:**\nThe marginal optical power combined with BIP errors during peak hours creates packet loss that triggers TCP retransmissions. While speed tests can absorb this through retransmission, interactive services cannot tolerate the resulting latency spikes and jitter.\n\nI should provide concrete troubleshooting steps and specific OLT commands to diagnose and resolve this.", "time": {"start": 1773730390358, "end": 1773730499939}}, {"id": "prt_cfa92329a001SBeN5n5NiBOtib", "sessionID": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "messageID": "msg_cfa91d6260015NsfG6HGDzIw0d", "type": "text", "text": "\n\n# GPON Service Degradation Analysis\n\n## 1. Fault Localization\n\n### Root Cause Determination\n\n**Primary Issue: Marginal Optical Signal Quality + Peak-Hour Congestion**\n\nThe evidence points to a **physical layer degradation** manifesting during peak usage hours:\n\n| Indicator | Value | Status | Implication |\n|-----------|-------|--------|-------------|\n| ONU Rx Power | -24.5 dBm | ⚠️ Marginal | Near lower threshold (-27~-28 dBm) |\n| OLT Rx Power | -21.0 dBm | ✓ Acceptable | Within normal range |\n| BIP Errors | Increasing | ✗ Abnormal | Physical layer bit errors |\n| Speedtest | >900 Mbps | ✓ Pass | Bandwidth capacity intact |\n| TCP Retransmissions | High | ✗ Abnormal | Packet loss triggering retransmission |\n\n**Most Probable Root Cause:**\n\nThe -24.5 dBm receive power at the ONU is operating at the **edge of the acceptable range**. During peak hours (14:00-16:00), this marginal signal quality becomes critical due to:\n\n1. **Increased splitter port contention** (more active ONTs on the 1:64 splitter)\n2. **Temperature-induced optical drift** (afternoon heat affecting fiber characteristics)\n3. **Accumulated BIP errors** causing packet corruption → TCP retransmissions → latency spikes\n\n---\n\n## 2. In-Depth Analysis\n\n### 2.1 Optical Power ↔ BIP Errors ↔ Service Performance Correlation\n\n```\nMarginal Rx Power (-24.5 dBm)\n        ↓\nReduced Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR)\n        ↓\nIncreased Bit Error Rate during peak contention\n        ↓\nBIP Error Counter Increment (GEM port level)\n        ↓\nCorrupted GEM Frames → Discarded at ONU/OLT\n        ↓\nTCP Packet Loss → Retransmission Triggered\n        ↓\nIncreased RTT + Jitter → Application-layer stuttering\n```\n\n**Why BIP errors matter:**\n- BIP (Bit Interleaved Parity) is the GPON physical layer error detection mechanism\n- Continuous increment during 14:00-16:00 indicates **time-correlated degradation**\n- Each BIP error represents corrupted bits that must be retransmitted at the TCP layer\n\n### 2.2 Why Speedtest Passes But Interactive Services Fail\n\n| Metric | Single-Thread Speedtest | Web/Video Conferencing |\n|--------|------------------------|------------------------|\n| **What it measures** | Sustained throughput over 10-30s | Real-time responsiveness |\n| **Tolerance to loss** | High (TCP retransmission invisible) | Low (visible as stutter) |\n| **Jitter sensitivity** | Low (averaged over time) | Critical (>30ms causes freeze) |\n| **Packet timing** | Bulk transfer, burst-tolerant | Isochronous, timing-critical |\n\n**Key insight:** A single-thread Speedtest can achieve 900+ Mbps even with 5-10% packet loss because:\n- TCP congestion control recovers throughput over the test duration\n- Retransmissions add latency but not reduce final throughput measurement\n- No real-time deadline to meet\n\nVideo conferencing and web browsing fail because:\n- **Video**: Jitter >30-50ms causes buffer underrun → freeze\n- **Web**: TCP retransmission timeout (RTO) delays page object fetch → perceived as \"slow\"\n- **One-way jitter** (observed in Wireshark) indicates asymmetric path degradation\n\n---\n\n## 3. Optimization Recommendations\n\n### 3.1 Physical Layer Remediation (Priority: HIGH)\n\n**Immediate actions:**\n\n```bash\n# 1. Verify optical threshold configuration on OLT\ndisplay ont autofind all\ndisplay ont info <ont-id>\n\n# 2. Check current optical alarm thresholds\ndisplay ont optical-threshold <ont-id>\n\n# Expected thresholds (adjust if needed):\n#   Lower receive threshold: -27 dBm (current -24.5 is too close)\n#   Upper receive threshold: -8 dBm\n```\n\n**Physical interventions:**\n\n| Action | Rationale |\n|--------|-----------|\n| Clean fiber connectors at ONU and OLT | Contamination causes additional 1-3 dB loss |\n| Inspect optical splitter port for damage | Faulty port can cause intermittent loss |\n| Consider reducing split ratio (1:64 → 1:32) | Adds 3 dB margin to optical budget |\n| Replace ONU if optical module aging | Transmit power drift indicates potential degradation |\n\n**Target optical power after remediation:**\n- ONU Rx: **-18 to -22 dBm** (add 3-6 dB margin)\n- OLT Rx: **-15 to -20 dBm**\n\n### 3.2 OLT-Side Parameter Inspection\n\n```bash\n# 1. Check DBA profile configuration\ndisplay dba-profile all\n\n# Verify Type 4 profile settings:\n#   - Maximum bandwidth: 1000000 kbps (1000M)\n#   - Ensure no unintended rate limiting\n\n# 2. Check traffic profile / service priority\ndisplay traffic-profile all\n\n# Priority 5 is correct for best-effort, but verify:\n#   - No policer dropping packets during congestion\n#   - WRR/SP scheduling not starving priority 5\n\n# 3. Monitor real-time error statistics during peak hours\ndisplay ont error-statistics <ont-id> verbose\n# Watch for:\n#   - BIP errors incrementing\n#   - FEC correction count (if available)\n#   - Dying Gasp events\n\n# 4. Check OLT uplink utilization during 14:00-16:00\ndisplay interface gpon <slot>/<port>\ndisplay board <slot>\n\n# 5. Verify no broadcast storm or MAC flapping\ndisplay mac-address ont <ont-id>\n```\n\n### 3.3 Diagnostic Script Logic (Automated Monitoring)\n\n```bash\n# Pseudo-script for peak-hour monitoring\n# Run every 5 minutes during 13:00-17:00\n\nwhile [ $(date +%H) -ge 13 ] && [ $(date +%H) -lt 17 ]; do\n    # Capture optical power\n    ONU_RX=$(display ont optical-info <ont-id> | grep \"Receive optical power\")\n    OLT_RX=$(display ont info | grep \"OLT receive optical power\")\n    \n    # Capture BIP error count\n    BIP_COUNT=$(display ont error-statistics <ont-id> | grep \"BIP error\")\n    \n    # Log with timestamp\n    echo \"$(date): ONU_RX=$ONU_RX, OLT_RX=$OLT_RX, BIP=$BIP_COUNT\" >> /var/log/gpon_monitor.log\n    \n    # Alert if degradation detected\n    if [ $BIP_DELTA -gt 100 ]; then\n        echo \"ALERT: BIP error spike detected at $(date)\" | mail -s \"GPON Degradation\" noc@company.com\n    fi\n    \n    sleep 300\ndone\n```\n\n### 3.4 QoS Optimization (If Physical Layer Cannot Be Immediately Improved)\n\n```bash\n# 1. Upgrade service priority from 5 to 3 or 4 for video conferencing traffic\n#    (requires traffic classification based on DSCP/port)\n\n# 2. Configure traffic shaping to smooth bursts\ntraffic-shape <profile-id> \n  cir 900000\n  pir 1000000\n  queue-length 1024\n\n# 3. Enable FEC (Forward Error Correction) if supported\n#    Adds redundancy to correct bit errors without retransmission\n```\n\n---\n\n## Summary & Action Plan\n\n| Priority | Action | Expected Outcome |\n|----------|--------|------------------|\n| **P0** | Clean/replace fiber connectors, verify optical path | Improve Rx power by 2-4 dB |\n| **P0** | Monitor BIP errors during peak hours with script | Quantify error correlation |\n| **P1** | If optical margin cannot be improved, reduce split ratio | Add 3 dB optical budget |\n| **P1** | Review OLT uplink utilization during 14:00-16:00 | Rule out backbone congestion |\n| **P2** | Upgrade traffic priority for real-time services | Reduce queuing delay |\n\n**Expected resolution:** After improving optical margin to -20 dBm or better, BIP errors should stabilize, TCP retransmissions will decrease, and interactive service quality will normalize even during peak hours.", "time": {"start": 1773730499940, "end": 1773730499940}}, {"id": "prt_cfa938d660013iJQRhm5gfx9iI", "sessionID": "ses_3056e2a1bffejsY6XQNiZVWSr7", "messageID": "msg_cfa91d6260015NsfG6HGDzIw0d", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26083, "output": 2454, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "# GPON Service Degradation Analysis\n\n## 1. Fault Localization\n\n### Root Cause Determination\n\n**Primary Issue: Marginal Optical Signal Quality + Peak-Hour Congestion**\n\nThe evidence points to a **physical layer degradation** manifesting during peak usage hours:\n\n| Indicator | Value | Status | Implication |\n|-----------|-------|--------|-------------|\n| ONU Rx Power | -24.5 dBm | ⚠️ Marginal | Near lower threshold (-27~-28 dBm) |\n| OLT Rx Power | -21.0 dBm | ✓ Acceptable | Within normal range |\n| BIP Errors | Increasing | ✗ Abnormal | Physical layer bit errors |\n| Speedtest | >900 Mbps | ✓ Pass | Bandwidth capacity intact |\n| TCP Retransmissions | High | ✗ Abnormal | Packet loss triggering retransmission |\n\n**Most Probable Root Cause:**\n\nThe -24.5 dBm receive power at the ONU is operating at the **edge of the acceptable range**. During peak hours (14:00-16:00), this marginal signal quality becomes critical due to:\n\n1. **Increased splitter port contention** (more active ONTs on the 1:64 splitter)\n2. **Temperature-induced optical drift** (afternoon heat affecting fiber characteristics)\n3. **Accumulated BIP errors** causing packet corruption → TCP retransmissions → latency spikes\n\n---\n\n## 2. In-Depth Analysis\n\n### 2.1 Optical Power ↔ BIP Errors ↔ Service Performance Correlation\n\n```\nMarginal Rx Power (-24.5 dBm)\n        ↓\nReduced Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR)\n        ↓\nIncreased Bit Error Rate during peak contention\n        ↓\nBIP Error Counter Increment (GEM port level)\n        ↓\nCorrupted GEM Frames → Discarded at ONU/OLT\n        ↓\nTCP Packet Loss → Retransmission Triggered\n        ↓\nIncreased RTT + Jitter → Application-layer stuttering\n```\n\n**Why BIP errors matter:**\n- BIP (Bit Interleaved Parity) is the GPON physical layer error detection mechanism\n- Continuous increment during 14:00-16:00 indicates **time-correlated degradation**\n- Each BIP error represents corrupted bits that must be retransmitted at the TCP layer\n\n### 2.2 Why Speedtest Passes But Interactive Services Fail\n\n| Metric | Single-Thread Speedtest | Web/Video Conferencing |\n|--------|------------------------|------------------------|\n| **What it measures** | Sustained throughput over 10-30s | Real-time responsiveness |\n| **Tolerance to loss** | High (TCP retransmission invisible) | Low (visible as stutter) |\n| **Jitter sensitivity** | Low (averaged over time) | Critical (>30ms causes freeze) |\n| **Packet timing** | Bulk transfer, burst-tolerant | Isochronous, timing-critical |\n\n**Key insight:** A single-thread Speedtest can achieve 900+ Mbps even with 5-