le": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730439453, "completed": 1773730541071}, "parentID": "msg_cfa920eb7001xtscBn1dPG5icv", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__10185__cn/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__10185__cn/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26484, "output": 1487, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa92a4c1001pq3crd7mgPtqI4", "sessionID": "ses_3056df15dffe2w7Qwwdbj8iRbZ", "messageID": "msg_cfa92a11d001sbOBVTfq7Au83o", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa92a4c100258NlSG26eBdC1m", "sessionID": "ses_3056df15dffe2w7Qwwdbj8iRbZ", "messageID": "msg_cfa92a11d001sbOBVTfq7Au83o", "type": "text", "text": "\n\n由于网络访问受限，我将基于KDIGO 2024指南的核心内容进行回答。该指南于2024年发布，是对AAV管理的重要更新。\n\n---\n\n## 问题1：糖皮质激素减量方案及预防用药\n\n### 糖皮质激素减量建议（前2周）\n\n根据KDIGO 2024指南，对于接受利妥昔单抗（RTX）联合糖皮质激素诱导缓解的AAV患者，推荐**低剂量激素方案**：\n\n**前2周具体减量方案：**\n\n| 时间 | 甲泼尼龙/泼尼松剂量 |\n|------|---------------------|\n| **第1-3天** | 甲泼尼龙静脉冲击 1000mg/天（可选，用于危重患者） |\n| **第4-7天** | 泼尼松 0.5-0.75 mg/kg/天（约30-45mg/天） |\n| **第8-14天** | 泼尼松 0.5 mg/kg/天（约30mg/天） |\n\n**关键要点：**\n- KDIGO 2024强调**低剂量激素策略**（相比传统高剂量方案），以减少感染风险\n- 对于该患者（肺肾综合征、呼吸衰竭），可考虑前3天甲泼尼龙冲击后快速减至口服\n- 2周后继续逐步减量，目标是在3-4个月内停用\n\n### 预防用药\n\n**必须添加：复方磺胺甲噁唑（TMP-SMX）**\n\n**理由：**\n- 用于预防**肺孢子菌肺炎（PJP/PCP）**\n- AAV患者接受RTX+激素治疗时PJP风险显著增高\n- KDIGO 2024强烈推荐所有接受诱导免疫抑制治疗的AAV患者进行PJP预防\n- 剂量：TMP-SMX 400/80mg，每日1次或每周3次\n- 持续时间：至少覆盖免疫抑制治疗期间及停药后6个月\n\n**其他考虑：**\n- 根据患者具体情况，可能还需：胃黏膜保护剂、钙剂+维生素D（骨质疏松预防）、抗病毒预防（如HBsAg阳性者）\n\n---\n\n## 问题2：血浆置换（PLEX）指征\n\n### 该患者**具备血浆置换指征**\n\n**依据：**\n\n根据KDIGO 2024指南，血浆置换在AAV中的指征已**显著收窄**，主要基于PEXIVAS试验结果。但该患者符合以下特殊情况：\n\n**支持PLEX的理由：**\n\n1. **弥漫性肺泡出血（DAH）**\n   - 患者咯血伴呼吸困难加重\n   - SpO2下降至88%（吸氧5L/min）\n   - 胸片示肺部浸润影**迅速扩大**\n   - 符合危及生命的肺泡出血\n\n2. **KDIGO 2024对PLEX的推荐：**\n   - **不推荐**常规用于AAV伴严重肾衰竭（SCr>500μmol/L）——基于PEXIVAS阴性结果\n   - **但保留**用于以下情况：\n     - 弥漫性肺泡出血（危及生命）\n     - 同时存在抗GBM抗体双阳性（该患者抗GBM阴性，排除）\n     - 血栓性微血管病（TMA）重叠\n\n3. **该患者具体情况：**\n   - SCr 420μmol/L（未达500μmol/L阈值）\n   - 但存在**进行性加重的肺泡出血**，这是独立的PLEX指征\n   - p-ANCA强阳性，抗GBM阴性，符合单纯AAV伴DAH\n\n**结论：** 该患者因**危及生命的弥漫性肺泡出血**，具备血浆置换指征，建议尽早启动。\n\n---\n\n## 问题3：ANCA滴度持续阳性的临床决策\n\n### ANCA滴度持续阳性**不应**作为加强免疫抑制的依据\n\n**临床决策逻辑：**\n\n**1. 指南明确立场（KDIGO 2024）：**\n- **不推荐**根据ANCA滴度调整治疗强度\n- 治疗决策应基于**临床表现和器官功能**，而非血清学标志物\n\n**2. 该患者的\"血清学-临床分离\"现象解读：**\n\n| 指标 | 状态 | 临床意义 |\n|------|------|----------|\n| p-ANCA滴度 | 持续高水平 | 血清学未完全缓解 |\n| 临床症状 | 明显好转（咯血消失、尿量增加） | **临床缓解** |\n| SCr | 下降 | **肾功能改善** |\n\n**3. 决策原则：**\n\n**应继续原方案或按计划减量，而非加强免疫抑制**\n\n**理由：**\n\na) **ANCA滴度与疾病活动度的相关性有限**\n   - 约30-40%的患者在临床缓解后ANCA仍可持续阳性数月\n   - ANCA转阴可能需要6-12个月甚至更长\n   - 持续阳性不等同于活动性疾病\n\nb) **过度治疗的风险**\n   - 加强免疫抑制会显著增加：\n     - 严重感染风险\n     - 恶性肿瘤风险\n     - 代谢并发症\n   - PEXIVAS等研究已证实低剂量策略的优越性\n\nc) **KDIGO 2024推荐：**\n   - ANCA滴度可用于**监测趋势**，但不应单独作为治疗依据\n   - 如ANCA持续升高**且**出现新发/复发临床症状，才考虑疾病复发\n   - 单纯血清学阳性而临床缓解 = 继续当前管理策略\n\n**4. 建议的随访策略：**\n- 继续按计划进行激素减量和RTX维持治疗\n- 定期监测：临床症状、肾功能、尿沉渣、炎症指标（CRP/ESR）\n- ANCA滴度可作为长期随访的参考，但非决策主导因素\n- 如后续出现ANCA滴度**持续上升**且伴临床恶化，再评估复发可能\n\n---\n\n## 总结\n\n| 问题 | 核心答案 |\n|------|----------|\n| 1. 激素减量 | 前3天甲泼尼龙冲击（可选），第4-14天泼尼松0.5-0.75mg/kg/天；必须加用TMP-SMX预防PJP |\n| 2. PLEX指征 | **具备**，依据是危及生命的弥漫性肺泡出血（非肾衰竭本身） |\n| 3. ANCA持续阳性 | **不加强**免疫抑制；治疗决策基于临床表现，而非血清学；该患者属\"血清学-临床分离\"，应继续原方案 |\n\n该患者病情危重，需多学科协作（ICU、肾内科、呼吸科），密切监测治疗反应和并发症。", "time": {"start": 1773730541062, "end": 1773730541062}}, {"id": "prt_cfa942e07001eSF0v1tehhoZMe", "sessionID": "ses_3056df15dffe2w7Qwwdbj8iRbZ", "messageID": "msg_cfa92a11d001sbOBVTfq7Au83o", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26484, "output": 1487, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "由于网络访问受限，我将基于KDIGO 2024指南的核心内容进行回答。该指南于2024年发布，是对AAV管理的重要更新。\n\n---\n\n## 问题1：糖皮质激素减量方案及预防用药\n\n### 糖皮质激素减量建议（前2周）\n\n根据KDIGO 2024指南，对于接受利妥昔单抗（RTX）联合糖皮质激素诱导缓解的AAV患者，推荐**低剂量激素方案**：\n\n**前2周具体减量方案：**\n\n| 时间 | 甲泼尼龙/泼尼松剂量 |\n|------|---------------------|\n| **第1-3天** | 甲泼尼龙静脉冲击 1000mg/天（可选，用于危重患者） |\n| **第4-7天** | 泼尼松 0.5-0.75 mg/kg/天（约30-45mg/天） |\n| **第8-14天** | 泼尼松 0.5 mg/kg/天（约30mg/天） |\n\n**关键要点：**\n- KDIGO 2024强调**低剂量激素策略**（相比传统高剂量方案），以减少感染风险\n- 对于该患者（肺肾综合征、呼吸衰竭），可考虑前3天甲泼尼龙冲击后快速减至口服\n- 2周后继续逐步减量，目标是在3-4个月内停用\n\n### 预防用药\n\n**必须添加：复方磺胺甲噁唑（TMP-SMX）**\n\n**理由：**\n- 用于预防**肺孢子菌肺炎（PJP/PCP）**\n- AAV患者接受RTX+激素治疗时PJP风险显著增高\n- KDIGO 2024强烈推荐所有接受诱导免疫抑制治疗的AAV患者进行PJP预防\n- 剂量：TMP-SMX 400/80mg，每日1次或每周3次\n- 持续时间：至少覆盖免疫抑制治疗期间及停药后6个月\n\n**其他考虑：**\n- 根据患者具体情况，可能还需：胃黏膜保护剂、钙剂+维生素D（骨质疏松预防）、抗病毒预防（如HBsAg阳性者）\n\n---\n\n## 问题2：血浆置换（PLEX）指征\n\n### 该患者**具备血浆置换指征**\n\n**依据：**\n\n根据KDIGO 2024指南，血浆置换在AAV中的指征已**显著收窄**，主要基于PEXIVAS试验结果。但该患者符合以下特殊情况：\n\n**支持PLEX的理由：**\n\n1. **弥漫性肺泡出血（DAH）**\n   - 患者咯血伴呼吸困难加重\n   - SpO2下降至88%（吸氧5L/min）\n   - 胸片示肺部浸润影**迅速扩大**\n   - 符合危及生命的肺泡出血\n\n2. **KDIGO 2024对PLEX的推荐：**\n   - **不推荐**常规用于AAV伴严重肾衰竭（SCr>500μmol/L）——基于PEXIVAS阴性结果\n   - **但保留**用于以下情况：\n     - 弥漫性肺泡出血（危及生命）\n     - 同时存在抗GBM抗体双阳性（该患者抗GBM阴性，排除）\n     - 血栓性微血管病（TMA）重叠\n\n3. **该患者具体情况：**\n   - SCr 420μmol/L（未达500μmol/L阈值）\n   - 但存在**进行性加重的肺泡出血**，这是独立的PLEX指征\n   - p-ANCA强阳性，抗GBM阴性，符合单纯AAV伴DAH\n\n**结论：** 该患者因**危及生命的弥漫性肺泡出血**，具备血浆置换指征，建议尽早启动。\n\n---\n\n## 问题3：ANCA滴度持续阳性的临床决策\n\n### ANCA滴度持续阳性**不应**作为加强免疫抑制的依据\n\n**临床决策逻辑：**\n\n**1. 指南明确立场（KDIGO 2024）：**\n- **不推荐**根据ANCA滴度调整治疗强度\n- 治疗决策应基于**临床表现和器官功能**，而非血清学标志物\n\n**2. 该患者的\"血清学-临床分离\"现象解读：**\n\n| 指标 | 状态 | 临床意义 |\n|------|------|----------|\n| p-ANCA滴度 | 持续高水平 | 血清学未完全缓解 |\n| 临床症状 | 明显好转（咯血消失、尿量增加） | **临床缓解** |\n| SCr | 下降 | **肾功能改善** |\n\n**3. 决策原则：**\n\n**应继续原方案或按计划减量，而非加强免疫抑制**\n\n**理由：**\n\na) **ANCA滴度与疾病活动度的相关性有限**\n   - 约30-40%的患者在临床缓解后ANCA仍可持续阳性数月\n   - ANCA转阴可能需要6-12个月甚至更长\n   - 持续阳性不等同于活动性疾病\n\nb) **过度治疗的风险**\n   - 加强免疫抑制会显著增加：\n     - 严重感染风险\n     - 恶性肿瘤风险\n     - 代谢并发症\n   - PEXIVAS等研究已证实低剂量策略的优越性\n\nc) **KDIGO 2024推荐：**\n   - ANCA滴度可用于**监测趋势**，但不应单独作为治疗依据\n   - 如ANCA持续升高**且**出现新发/复发临床症状，才考虑疾病复发\n   - 单纯血清学阳性而临床缓解 = 继续当前管理策略\n\n**4. 建议的随访策略：**\n- 继续按计划进行激素减量和RTX维持治疗\n- 定期监测：临床症状、肾功能、尿沉渣、炎症指标（CRP/ESR）\n- ANCA滴度可作为长期随访的参考，但非决策主导因素\n- 如后续出现ANCA滴度**持续上升**且伴临床恶化，再评估复发可能\n\n---\n\n## 总结\n\n| 问题 | 核心答案 |\n|------|----------|\n| 1. 激素减量 | 前3天甲泼尼龙冲击（可选），第4-14天泼尼松0.5-0.75mg/kg/天；必须加用TMP-SMX预防PJP |\n| 2. PLEX指征 | **具备**，依据是危及生命的弥漫性肺泡出血（非肾衰竭本身） |\n| 3. ANCA持续阳性 | **不加强**免疫抑制；治疗决策基于临床表现，而非血清学；该患者属\"血清学-临床分离\"，应继续原方案 |\n\n该患者病情危重，需多学科协作（ICU、肾内科、呼吸科），密切监测治疗反应和并发症。", "case_id": 10185, "language": "cn", "tags": {"topics": ["医疗健康", "临床医学", "肾内科"], "time_sensitivity": {"time_sensitivity": "Weakly time-sensitive", "year_month": "NA", "day": "NA"}}, "rubric_count": 12}, "metrics": {"solve_time_seconds": 141.7, "tokens": {"input": 79028, "output": 1932, "total": 80960}, "cost": 0.0, "step_count": 3}, "error": ""}}
{"task_id": "industry/4164/global", "answer": "This is a classic offline-online metric mismatch problem. Let me analyze each question systematically.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Can We Directly Conclude Model B is Inferior?\n\n**No, we cannot directly conclude this.** The evidence is confounded by multiple factors:\n\n| Confounder | Impact |\n|------------|--------|\n| **Push notification change** | Concurrent experiment violates SUTVA (Stable Unit Treatment Value Assumption) |\n| **0.3% higher fallback rate** | ~3 in 1000 requests use rule-based ranking, contaminating treatment effect |\n| **Real-time feature latency** | 5-minute window features may have different freshness distribution online vs. offline replay |\n| **No counterfactual correction** | Offline AUC is biased by historical policy; cannot estimate true on-policy performance |\n\nThe observed -2.1% CTR could be partially or entirely attributable to these confounders rather than model quality.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mechanisms for \"Good Offline, Poor Online\"\n\n### **Class 1: Distributional Shift (Statistical)**\n- **Training-serving skew**: Offline replay uses historical impressions from Model A's policy. Model B sees a different feature distribution when deployed (different items shown → different user responses → different feature values for sequence features).\n- **Temporal non-stationarity**: User behavior in Q1 2025 may differ from the training period. The 5-minute real-time features are particularly sensitive to this.\n- **Selection bias**: Offline evaluation only observes outcomes for items that were historically shown. Model B may want to rank different items, but offline metrics cannot evaluate counterfactual outcomes.\n\n### **Class 2: System-Level Artifacts (Engineering)**\n- **Feature freshness degradation**: Real-time features computed on 5-minute windows may have higher latency in production than simulated offline. Stale features → degraded predictions.\n- **Fallback contamination**: 0.3% higher failure rate means more requests use rule-based ranking. If fallbacks are systematically lower quality, this directly reduces observed CTR independent of model quality.\n- **Inference latency effects**: More complex architecture may increase p95 latency, causing timeouts that manifest as failed requests or poor UX (reduced dwell time).\n\n### **Class 3: Causal Interference (Experimental Design)**\n- **Concurrent changes**: The push notification strategy change is a confounder. If push traffic has different CTR characteristics and the change affected treatment/control groups asymmetrically, this biases the estimate.\n- **Network effects / interference**: User assignment via hash bucketing assumes no interference between units. But recommendation systems have network effects (e.g., content creators respond to engagement patterns, affecting item supply).\n- **Feedback loops**: Model B's different ranking → different user behavior → different real-time features → different predictions. This dynamic feedback cannot be captured in static offline evaluation.\n\n### **Class 4: Metric Mismatch (Evaluation)**\n- **AUC vs. CTR optimization**: AUC measures ranking quality across all thresholds; CTR measures actual clicks at the deployed threshold. A model can improve AUC while harming CTR if it miscalibrates scores at the operating point.\n- **Position bias uncorrected**: Offline replay doesn't account for position bias in historical logs. Model B may be more sensitive to position effects.\n- **Dwell time not optimized**: Model B improved AUC/logloss but decreased dwell time—suggesting it may optimize for \"clicky\" but low-quality engagement.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Flaws in Current A/B Experiment Design\n\n| Flaw | Severity | Why It Matters |\n|------|----------|----------------|\n| **Concurrent push notification change** | High | Violates isolation assumption; cannot attribute effect to model alone |\n| **No fallback rate accounting** | Medium-High | 0.3% differential fallback is ~15% of the observed -2.1% effect; should be measured and corrected |\n| **No counterfactual correction in offline eval** | Medium | Offline AUC is biased; cannot reliably predict online performance |\n| **Real-time feature validation gap** | Medium | No verification that 5-minute features have same distribution/freshness online vs. offline |\n| **Single metric focus (CTR)** | Medium | Dwell time decreased and complaints increased—multi-metric dashboard needed before launch decision |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practical Improvement Proposal\n\n**Implement inverse propensity scoring (IPS) or doubly robust estimation for offline evaluation.**\n\n**Why this helps:**\n- Corrects for selection bias in historical logs\n- Provides unbiased estimate of what Model B would achieve under its own policy\n- Can be computed retroactively on existing data\n\n**Implementation:**\n```python\n# Pseudocode for IPS-weighted offline evaluation\nfor impression in historical_logs:\n    propensity = P(item_was_shown | context, Model_A)  # logging policy\n    weight = 1 / propensity\n    ips_auc += weight * indicator(model_B_would_be_correct)\n```\n\n**Complementary action:** Re-run the A/B test with the push notification change held constant, and instrument fallback events to measure their CTR separately.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Conditions to Take Model B Offline\n\nI would support taking Model B offline if **any** of the following evidential conditions are met:\n\n| Condition | Evidence Required |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| **Clean A/B confirms degradation** | Re-run experiment with no concurrent changes; 95% CI for CTR lift excludes 0 and is < -1.0% |\n| **Fallback rate is the culprit** | After correcting for fallback contamination, adjusted CTR shows no significant difference, but engineering cannot reduce failure rate to parity within 2 weeks |\n| **User harm is significant** | Complaint rate increase is statistically significant (p < 0.01) AND dwell time decrease exceeds -3% with narrow confidence intervals |\n| **Offline-online correlation is broken** | IPS-corrected offline metrics still predict improvement, but online degradation persists across 3+ independent experiments (suggests fundamental model mismatch) |\n| **Feature freshness is unfixable** | Audit shows >20% of real-time features are stale (>5 min old) in production, and infrastructure cannot improve this within reasonable timeline |\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation\n\n**Do not conclude Model B is inferior yet.** The evidence is confounded. Priority actions:\n\n1. **Immediate**: Instrument fallback events to measure their CTR; quantify how much of the -2.1% is attributable to the 0.3% higher fallback rate.\n2. **Short-term**: Re-run A/B test with push notification strategy held constant.\n3. **Medium-term**: Implement IPS/doubly robust offline evaluation to improve offline-online correlation.\n4. **Parallel**: Audit real-time feature freshness distribution in production vs. offline simulation.\n\nOnly after these steps should a launch decision be made.", "score": {"rubric_results": [{"rubric_number": 1, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型在第一节明确指出'No, we cannot directly conclude this'，符合评分标准。"}, {"rubric_number": 2, "weight": 10, "met": false, "reason": "模型虽然提到了失败率的影响，但未具体指出失败率集中在'高活跃度用户'或'长行为序列用户'这一特定分布特征。"}, {"rubric_number": 3, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型在系统层面分析了实时特征的延迟和新鲜度问题（Feature freshness degradation），指出了在线与离线的数据分布不匹配。"}, {"rubric_number": 4, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型解释了 AUC 与 CTR 的阈值差异，但未明确提及 AUC 无法捕捉'top-k 排序质量'、'用户反感'或'长期留存'等具体局限性。"}, {"rubric_number": 5, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型指出了推送策略变更是混淆因素，但未具体分析其如何改变'用户意图分布'或引入'冷启动用户'。"}, {"rubric_number": 6, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型指出了 fallback 流量会'污染处理效应'（contaminating treatment effect），并建议单独测量，隐含识别了当前聚合评估的缺陷。"}, {"rubric_number": 7, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型提到了并发实验违反 SUTVA 假设，但未明确分析'user_id hash bucketing'与'推送策略'之间缺乏正交性这一具体设计缺陷。"}, {"rubric_number": 8, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型提出了具体的工程诊断建议，如'instrument fallback events'和'audit real-time feature freshness'，符合具体工程诊断的要求。"}, {"rubric_number": 9, "weight": 10, "met": false, "reason": "模型的下线标准中包含了特征新鲜度和失败率，但未包含'TP99 延迟'或'GPU 资源成本'等具体的工程性能阈值。"}, {"rubric_number": 10, "weight": 3, "met": true, "reason": "模型回复完整覆盖了提示中的五个任务：结论评估、机制分析、缺陷识别、改进提议和下线标准。"}, {"rubric_number": 11, "weight": 3, "met": true, "reason": "模型使用了清晰的章节标题（## 1. 到 ## 5.）对应每个任务，结构格式良好。"}, {"rubric_number": 12, "weight": -10, "met": false, "reason": "模型没有冗余地重述提示中的背景信息，而是直接进行分析，未触发扣分项。"}, {"rubric_number": 13, "weight": -10, "met": false, "reason": "模型将 0.3% 失败率视为系统性偏差（contamination）而非统计噪声，未触发扣分项。"}, {"rubric_number": 14, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型优先建议工程诊断和实验修复，而非算法调整，未触发扣分项。"}, {"rubric_number": 15, "weight": -10, "met": false, "reason": "模型分析了多种工程和因果偏差，未将差异 solely 归因于离线过拟合，未触发扣分项。"}, {"rubric_number": 16, "weight": -10, "met": false, "reason": "模型建议重新运行实验并固定变量，而非简单地排除数据后直接下结论，未触发扣分项。"}, {"rubric_number": 17, "weight": -10, "met": false, "reason": "模型未推荐影子测试或全量流量复制等高负载评估方法，未触发扣分项。"}, {"rubric_number": 18, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型建议在进行进一步分析前不做结论，但未明确推荐'暂停 rollout'、'回滚'或'维持小流量'等具体风控措施。"}], "score": 41, "max_score": 81, "raw_response": "[\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 1,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在第一节明确指出'No, we cannot directly conclude this'，符合评分标准。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 2,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型虽然提到了失败率的影响，但未具体指出失败率集中在'高活跃度用户'或'长行为序列用户'这一特定分布特征。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 3,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在系统层面分析了实时特征的延迟和新鲜度问题（Feature freshness degradation），指出了在线与离线的数据分布不匹配。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 4,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型解释了 AUC 与 CTR 的阈值差异，但未明确提及 AUC 无法捕捉'top-k 排序质量'、'用户反感'或'长期留存'等具体局限性。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 5,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型指出了推送策略变更是混淆因素，但未具体分析其如何改变'用户意图分布'或引入'冷启动用户'。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 6,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型指出了 fallback 流量会'污染处理效应'（contaminating treatment effect），并建议单独测量，隐含识别了当前聚合评估的缺陷。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 7,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型提到了并发实验违反 SUTVA 假设，但未明确分析'user_id hash bucketing'与'推送策略'之间缺乏正交性这一具体设计缺陷。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 8,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型提出了具体的工程诊断建议，如'instrument fallback events'和'audit real-time feature freshness'，符合具体工程诊断的要求。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 9,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型的下线标准中包含了特征新鲜度和失败率，但未包含'TP99 延迟'或'GPU 资源成本'等具体的工程性能阈值。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 10,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型回复完整覆盖了提示中的五个任务：结论评估、机制分析、缺陷识别、改进提议和下线标准。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 11,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型使用了清晰的章节标题（## 1. 到 ## 5.）对应每个任务，结构格式良好。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 12,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型没有冗余地重述提示中的背景信息，而是直接进行分析，未触发扣分项。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 13,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型将 0.3% 失败率视为系统性偏差（contamination）而非统计噪声，未触发扣分项。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 14,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型优先建议工程诊断和实验修复，而非算法调整，未触发扣分项。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 15,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型分析了多种工程和因果偏差，未将差异 solely 归因于离线过拟合，未触发扣分项。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 16,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型建议重新运行实验并固定变量，而非简单地排除数据后直接下结论，未触发扣分项。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 17,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未推荐影子测试或全量流量复制等高负载评估方法，未触发扣分项。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 18,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型建议在进行进一步分析前不做结论，但未明确推荐'暂停 rollout'、'回滚'或'维持小流量'等具体风控措施。\"\n  }\n]"}, "error": null, "stage": null, "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "user_prompt": "I am the owner of a recommendation system at an internet company. My team is responsible for a large-scale recommender system, whose core model is a deep-learning-based CTR prediction model used to rank items in the home feed.\n\nIn the first quarter of 2025, we upgraded the model from the legacy Model A to Model B. Model B incorporates richer user behavior sequence features and a more complex network architecture. On the same offline validation set, Model B outperforms Model A with an AUC improvement of 0.015 and a significantly lower log loss. However, after a two-week A/B test, Model B shows a 2.1% decrease in online CTR compared to Model A, along with a decline in average user dwell time and an increase in user complaints.\n\nAdditional information:\n1. User assignment in the A/B test is done via user_id hash bucketing, but the new model introduces real-time user features that depend on user behavior within the last 5 minutes.\n2. During the online experiment, there was a change in the push-notification strategy, which the PM believes should have minimal impact on CTR.\n3. According to the logging system, the request failure rate of the new model is 0.3% higher than that of the old model. Failed requests fall back to a rule-based ranking strategy.\n4. Offline evaluation is conducted via historical log replay, without any counterfactual correction.\n\nYour tasks:\n1. Can we directly conclude, based on the above evidence, that Model B is inferior to Model A?\n2. Systematically enumerate at least four classes of mechanisms that could lead to the phenomenon of 'good offline performance but poor online performance'. Your analysis must cover statistical, system-level, and causal perspectives.\n3. Identify at least three non-negligible flaws in the current A/B experiment design.\n4. Propose one practical and implementable improvement to the experimental or analytical setup.\n5. Clearly specify under what evidential conditions you would support taking Model B offline.", "response": {"request_id": "omb-batch", "status": "completed", "session_id": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "workspace": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__4164__global/omb-batch", "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "final_prompt": "I am the owner of a recommendation system at an internet company. My team is responsible for a large-scale recommender system, whose core model is a deep-learning-based CTR prediction model used to rank items in the home feed.\n\nIn the first quarter of 2025, we upgraded the model from the legacy Model A to Model B. Model B incorporates richer user behavior sequence features and a more complex network architecture. On the same offline validation set, Model B outperforms Model A with an AUC improvement of 0.015 and a significantly lower log loss. However, after a two-week A/B test, Model B shows a 2.1% decrease in online CTR compared to Model A, along with a decline in average user dwell time and an increase in user complaints.\n\nAdditional information:\n1. User assignment in the A/B test is done via user_id hash bucketing, but the new model introduces real-time user features that depend on user behavior within the last 5 minutes.\n2. During the online experiment, there was a change in the push-notification strategy, which the PM believes should have minimal impact on CTR.\n3. According to the logging system, the request failure rate of the new model is 0.3% higher than that of the old model. Failed requests fall back to a rule-based ranking strategy.\n4. Offline evaluation is conducted via historical log replay, without any counterfactual correction.\n\nYour tasks:\n1. Can we directly conclude, based on the above evidence, that Model B is inferior to Model A?\n2. Systematically enumerate at least four classes of mechanisms that could lead to the phenomenon of 'good offline performance but poor online performance'. Your analysis must cover statistical, system-level, and causal perspectives.\n3. Identify at least three non-negligible flaws in the current A/B experiment design.\n4. Propose one practical and implementable improvement to the experimental or analytical setup.\n5. Clearly specify under what evidential conditions you would support taking Model B offline.", "session_data": {"scope_id": "8a0979c6a7a846e35ed656ef1d2927405e82b91f", "session_id": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "info": {"id": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "version": "local", "scopeID": "8a0979c6a7a846e35ed656ef1d2927405e82b91f", "directory": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__4164__global/omb-batch", "title": "onemillion:industry/4164/global", "time": {"created": 1773730263653, "updated": 1773730263780}, "summary": {"additions": 0, "deletions": 0, "files": 0}}, "messages": [{"id": "msg_cfa8ff27a001oy21RYRGQTj1hc", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa8ff27a001oy21RYRGQTj1hc", "sessionID": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "role": "user", "time": {"created": 1773730263674}, "summary": {"title": "Deep Learning CTR Model Upgrade with A/B Test Challenges", "diffs": []}, "agent": "synergy", "model": {"providerID": "sii-holos", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B"}}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa8ff27b001roTdYrAtOu1uWL", "sessionID": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff27a001oy21RYRGQTj1hc", "type": "text", "text": "\"I am the owner of a recommendation system at an internet company. My team is responsible for a large-scale recommender system, whose core model is a deep-learning-based CTR prediction model used to rank items in the home feed.\n\nIn the first quarter of 2025, we upgraded the model from the legacy Model A to Model B. Model B incorporates richer user behavior sequence features and a more complex network architecture. On the same offline validation set, Model B outperforms Model A with an AUC improvement of 0.015 and a significantly lower log loss. However, after a two-week A/B test, Model B shows a 2.1% decrease in online CTR compared to Model A, along with a decline in average user dwell time and an increase in user complaints.\n\nAdditional information:\n1. User assignment in the A/B test is done via user_id hash bucketing, but the new model introduces real-time user features that depend on user behavior within the last 5 minutes.\n2. During the online experiment, there was a change in the push-notification strategy, which the PM believes should have minimal impact on CTR.\n3. According to the logging system, the request failure rate of the new model is 0.3% higher than that of the old model. Failed requests fall back to a rule-based ranking strategy.\n4. Offline evaluation is conducted via historical log replay, without any counterfactual correction.\n\nYour tasks:\n1. Can we directly conclude, based on the above evidence, that Model B is inferior to Model A?\n2. Systematically enumerate at least four classes of mechanisms that could lead to the phenomenon of 'good offline performance but poor online performance'. Your analysis must cover statistical, system-level, and causal perspectives.\n3. Identify at least three non-negligible flaws in the current A/B experiment design.\n4. Propose one practical and implementable improvement to the experimental or analytical setup.\n5. Clearly specify under what evidential conditions you would support taking Model B offline.\"\n"}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa8ff2b7001bhS9enZX3GUyn2", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa8ff2b7001bhS9enZX3GUyn2", "sessionID": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730263735, "completed": 1773730359316}, "parentID": "msg_cfa8ff27a001oy21RYRGQTj1hc", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__4164__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/industry__4164__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26089, "output": 1826, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa8ffc79001OPi0vwe8Um5wf6", "sessionID": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff2b7001bhS9enZX3GUyn2", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa8ffc79002onpxFt9NssnjWw", "sessionID": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff2b7001bhS9enZX3GUyn2", "type": "reasoning", "text": "This is a sophisticated question about recommendation system evaluation, A/B testing, and the gap between offline and online performance. Let me work through each question systematically.\n\nThe user is asking me to analyze a real-world ML deployment problem. This doesn't require code exploration, web search, or delegation - it requires analytical reasoning based on the information provided. Let me think through each question carefully.\n\n1. **Can we conclude Model B is inferior?**\n   - No, we cannot directly conclude this. There are multiple confounding factors that could explain the discrepancy.\n\n2. **Mechanisms for \"good offline, poor online\"**:\n   I need to cover statistical, system-level, and causal perspectives. Let me think of at least four:\n   - Selection bias / distributional shift (statistical)\n   - Feature freshness / latency issues (system-level)\n   - Feedback loop / position bias (causal)\n   - Evaluation metric mismatch (statistical/causal)\n   - Interference from other changes (causal)\n   - Fallback behavior artifacts (system-level)\n\n3. **Flaws in A/B experiment design**:\n   - The push notification change is a confounder\n   - No accounting for fallback rate difference\n   - Real-time features may have different behavior in production vs offline\n   - No counterfactual correction in offline eval\n   - User assignment via hash bucketing may not account for network effects\n\n4. **Practical improvement**:\n   Several options here - I'll pick one that's implementable\n\n5. **Conditions to take Model B offline**:\n   Need to specify clear evidential thresholds\n\nThis is an analytical task that doesn't require tools - I should provide a thorough, well-structured response.", "time": {"start": 1773730266233, "end": 1773730359302}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9039f7001wZAufAxS8YNBtK", "sessionID": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff2b7001bhS9enZX3GUyn2", "type": "text", "text": "\n\nThis is a classic offline-online metric mismatch problem. Let me analyze each question systematically.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Can We Directly Conclude Model B is Inferior?\n\n**No, we cannot directly conclude this.** The evidence is confounded by multiple factors:\n\n| Confounder | Impact |\n|------------|--------|\n| **Push notification change** | Concurrent experiment violates SUTVA (Stable Unit Treatment Value Assumption) |\n| **0.3% higher fallback rate** | ~3 in 1000 requests use rule-based ranking, contaminating treatment effect |\n| **Real-time feature latency** | 5-minute window features may have different freshness distribution online vs. offline replay |\n| **No counterfactual correction** | Offline AUC is biased by historical policy; cannot estimate true on-policy performance |\n\nThe observed -2.1% CTR could be partially or entirely attributable to these confounders rather than model quality.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mechanisms for \"Good Offline, Poor Online\"\n\n### **Class 1: Distributional Shift (Statistical)**\n- **Training-serving skew**: Offline replay uses historical impressions from Model A's policy. Model B sees a different feature distribution when deployed (different items shown → different user responses → different feature values for sequence features).\n- **Temporal non-stationarity**: User behavior in Q1 2025 may differ from the training period. The 5-minute real-time features are particularly sensitive to this.\n- **Selection bias**: Offline evaluation only observes outcomes for items that were historically shown. Model B may want to rank different items, but offline metrics cannot evaluate counterfactual outcomes.\n\n### **Class 2: System-Level Artifacts (Engineering)**\n- **Feature freshness degradation**: Real-time features computed on 5-minute windows may have higher latency in production than simulated offline. Stale features → degraded predictions.\n- **Fallback contamination**: 0.3% higher failure rate means more requests use rule-based ranking. If fallbacks are systematically lower quality, this directly reduces observed CTR independent of model quality.\n- **Inference latency effects**: More complex architecture may increase p95 latency, causing timeouts that manifest as failed requests or poor UX (reduced dwell time).\n\n### **Class 3: Causal Interference (Experimental Design)**\n- **Concurrent changes**: The push notification strategy change is a confounder. If push traffic has different CTR characteristics and the change affected treatment/control groups asymmetrically, this biases the estimate.\n- **Network effects / interference**: User assignment via hash bucketing assumes no interference between units. But recommendation systems have network effects (e.g., content creators respond to engagement patterns, affecting item supply).\n- **Feedback loops**: Model B's different ranking → different user behavior → different real-time features → different predictions. This dynamic feedback cannot be captured in static offline evaluation.\n\n### **Class 4: Metric Mismatch (Evaluation)**\n- **AUC vs. CTR optimization**: AUC measures ranking quality across all thresholds; CTR measures actual clicks at the deployed threshold. A model can improve AUC while harming CTR if it miscalibrates scores at the operating point.\n- **Position bias uncorrected**: Offline replay doesn't account for position bias in historical logs. Model B may be more sensitive to position effects.\n- **Dwell time not optimized**: Model B improved AUC/logloss but decreased dwell time—suggesting it may optimize for \"clicky\" but low-quality engagement.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Flaws in Current A/B Experiment Design\n\n| Flaw | Severity | Why It Matters |\n|------|----------|----------------|\n| **Concurrent push notification change** | High | Violates isolation assumption; cannot attribute effect to model alone |\n| **No fallback rate accounting** | Medium-High | 0.3% differential fallback is ~15% of the observed -2.1% effect; should be measured and corrected |\n| **No counterfactual correction in offline eval** | Medium | Offline AUC is biased; cannot reliably predict online performance |\n| **Real-time feature validation gap** | Medium | No verification that 5-minute features have same distribution/freshness online vs. offline |\n| **Single metric focus (CTR)** | Medium | Dwell time decreased and complaints increased—multi-metric dashboard needed before launch decision |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practical Improvement Proposal\n\n**Implement inverse propensity scoring (IPS) or doubly robust estimation for offline evaluation.**\n\n**Why this helps:**\n- Corrects for selection bias in historical logs\n- Provides unbiased estimate of what Model B would achieve under its own policy\n- Can be computed retroactively on existing data\n\n**Implementation:**\n```python\n# Pseudocode for IPS-weighted offline evaluation\nfor impression in historical_logs:\n    propensity = P(item_was_shown | context, Model_A)  # logging policy\n    weight = 1 / propensity\n    ips_auc += weight * indicator(model_B_would_be_correct)\n```\n\n**Complementary action:** Re-run the A/B test with the push notification change held constant, and instrument fallback events to measure their CTR separately.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Conditions to Take Model B Offline\n\nI would support taking Model B offline if **any** of the following evidential conditions are met:\n\n| Condition | Evidence Required |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| **Clean A/B confirms degradation** | Re-run experiment with no concurrent changes; 95% CI for CTR lift excludes 0 and is < -1.0% |\n| **Fallback rate is the culprit** | After correcting for fallback contamination, adjusted CTR shows no significant difference, but engineering cannot reduce failure rate to parity within 2 weeks |\n| **User harm is significant** | Complaint rate increase is statistically significant (p < 0.01) AND dwell time decrease exceeds -3% with narrow confidence intervals |\n| **Offline-online correlation is broken** | IPS-corrected offline metrics still predict improvement, but online degradation persists across 3+ independent experiments (suggests fundamental model mismatch) |\n| **Feature freshness is unfixable** | Audit shows >20% of real-time features are stale (>5 min old) in production, and infrastructure cannot improve this within reasonable timeline |\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation\n\n**Do not conclude Model B is inferior yet.** The evidence is confounded. Priority actions:\n\n1. **Immediate**: Instrument fallback events to measure their CTR; quantify how much of the -2.1% is attributable to the 0.3% higher fallback rate.\n2. **Short-term**: Re-run A/B test with push notification strategy held constant.\n3. **Medium-term**: Implement IPS/doubly robust offline evaluation to improve offline-online correlation.\n4. **Parallel**: Audit real-time feature freshness distribution in production vs. offline simulation.\n\nOnly after these steps should a launch decision be made.", "time": {"start": 1773730359303, "end": 1773730359303}}, {"id": "prt_cfa916809001iCmJnioIUwSeU8", "sessionID": "ses_305700d9affeaSeECQwBDhI1JX", "messageID": "msg_cfa8ff2b7001bhS9enZX3GUyn2", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26089, "output": 1826, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "This is a classic offline-online metric mismatch problem. Let me analyze each question systematically.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Can We Directly Conclude Model B is Inferior?\n\n**No, we cannot directly conclude this.** The evidence is confounded by multiple factors:\n\n| Confounder | Impact |\n|------------|--------|\n| **Push notification change** | Concurrent experiment violates SUTVA (Stable Unit Treatment Value Assumption) |\n| **0.3% higher fallback rate** | ~3 in 1000 requests use rule-based ranking, contaminating treatment effect |\n| **Real-time feature latency** | 5-minute window features may have different freshness distribution online vs. offline replay |\n| **No counterfactual correction** | Offline AUC is biased by historical policy; cannot estimate true on-policy performance |\n\nThe observed -2.1% CTR could be partially or entirely attributable to these confounders rather than model quality.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mechanisms for \"Good Offline, Poor Online\"\n\n### **Class 1: Distributional Shift (Statistical)**\n- **Training-serving skew**: Offline replay uses historical impressions from Model A's policy. Model B sees a different feature distribution when deployed (different items shown → different user responses → different feature values for sequence features).\n- **Temporal non-stationarity**: User behavior in Q1 2025 may differ from the training period. The 5-minute real-time features are particularly sensitive to this.\n- **Selection bias**: Offline evaluation only observes outcomes for items that were historically shown. Model B may want to rank different items, but offline metrics cannot evaluate counterfactual outcomes.\n\n### **Class 2: System-Level Artifacts (Engineering)**\n- **Feature freshness degradation**: Real-time features computed on 5-minute windows may have higher latency in production than simulated offline. Stale features → degraded predictions.\n- **Fallback contamination**: 0.3% higher failure rate means more requests use rule-based ranking. If fallbacks are systematically lower quality, this directly reduces observed CTR independent of model quality.\n- **Inference latency effects**: More complex architecture may increase p95 latency, causing timeouts that manifest as failed requests or poor UX (reduced dwell time).\n\n### **Class 3: Causal Interference (Experimental Design)**\n- **Concurrent changes**: The push notification strategy change is a confounder. If push traffic has different CTR characteristics and the change affected treatment/control groups asymmetrically, this biases the estimate.\n- **Network effects / interference**: User assignment via hash bucketing assumes no interference between units. But recommendation systems have network effects (e.g., content creators respond to engagement patterns, affecting item supply).\n- **Feedback loops**: Model B's different ranking → different user behavior → different real-time features → different predictions. This dynamic feedback cannot be captured in static offline evaluation.\n\n### **Class 4: Metric Mismatch (Evaluation)**\n- **AUC vs. CTR optimization**: AUC measures ranking quality across all thresholds; CTR measures actual clicks at the deployed threshold. A model can improve AUC while harming CTR if it miscalibrates scores at the operating point.\n- **Position bias uncorrected**: Offline replay doesn't account for position bias in historical logs. Model B may be more sensitive to position effects.\n- **Dwell time not optimized**: Model B improved AUC/logloss but decreased dwell time—suggesting it may optimize for \"clicky\" but low-quality engagement.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Flaws in Current A/B Experiment Design\n\n| Flaw | Severity | Why It Matters |\n|------|----------|----------------|\n| **Concurrent push notification change** | High | Violates isolation assumption; cannot attribute effect to model alone |\n| **No fallback rate accounting** | Medium-High | 0.3% differential fallback is ~15% of the observed -2.1% effect; should be measured and corrected |\n| **No counterfactual correction in offline eval** | Medium | Offline AUC is biased; cannot reliably predict online performance |\n| **Real-time feature validation gap** | Medium | No verification that 5-minute features have same distribution/freshness online vs. offline |\n| **Single metric focus (CTR)** | Medium | Dwell time decreased and complaints increased—multi-metric dashboard needed before launch decision |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practical Improvement Proposal\n\n**Implement inverse propensity scoring (IPS) or doubly robust estimation for offline evaluation.**\n\n**Why this helps:**\n- Corrects for selection bias in historical logs\n- Provides unbiased estimate of what Model B would achieve under its own policy\n- Can be computed retroactively on existing data\n\n**Implementation:**\n```python\n# Pseudocode for IPS-weighted offline evaluation\nfor impression in historical_logs:\n    propensity = P(item_was_shown | context, Model_A)  # logging policy\n    weight = 1 / propensity\n    ips_auc += weight * indicator(model_B_would_be_correct)\n```\n\n**Complementary action:** Re-run the A/B test with the push notification change held constant, and instrument fallback events to measure their CTR separately.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Conditions to Take Model B Offline\n\nI would support taking Model B offline if **any** of the following evidential conditions are met:\n\n| Condition | Evidence Required |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| **Clean A/B confirms degradation** | Re-run experiment with no concurrent changes; 95% CI for CTR lift excludes 0 and is < -1.0% |\n| **Fallback rate is the culprit** | After correcting for fallback contamination, adjusted CTR shows no significant difference, but engineering cannot reduce failure rate to parity within 2 weeks |\n| **User harm is significant** | Complaint rate increase is statistically significant (p < 0.01) AND dwell time decrease exceeds -3% with narrow confidence intervals |\n| **Offline-online correlation is broken** | IPS-corrected offline metrics still predict improvement, but online degradation persists across 3+ independent experiments (suggests fundamental model mismatch) |\n| **Feature freshness is unfixable** | Audit shows >20% of real-time features are stale (>5 min old) in production, and infrastructure cannot improve this within reasonable timeline |\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation\n\n**Do not conclude Model B is inferior yet.** The evidence is confounded. Priority actions:\n\n1. **Immediate**: Instrument fallback events to measure their CTR; quantify how much of the -2.1% is attributable to the 0.3% higher fallback rate.\n2. **Short-term**: Re-run A/B test with push notification strategy held constant.\n3. **Medium-term**: Implement IPS/doubly robust offline evaluation to improve offline-online correlation.\n4. **Parallel**: Audit real-time feature freshness distribution in production vs. offline simulation.\n\nOnly after these steps should a launch decision be made.", "case_id": 4164, "language": "global", "tags": {"topics": ["Industry", "Machine Learning", "Machine Learning"], "time_sensitivity": {"time_sensitivity": "Time-agnostic", "year_month": "NA", "day": "NA"}}, "rubric_count": 18}, "metrics": {"solve_time_seconds": 98.2, "tokens": {"input": 26089, "output": 1826, "total": 27915}, "cost": 0.0, "step_count": 1}, "error": ""}}
{"task_id": "healthcare_and_medicine/3596/global", "answer": "This is a complex surgical case involving an immunocompromised elderly patient with severe intra-abdominal sepsis. Let me address each question systematically.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CT Radiographic Features and Severity Assessment\n\n| Feature | Finding | Clinical Significance |\n|---------|---------|----------------------|\n| **Bowel wall changes** | Significant thickening, loss of stratification | Indicates transmural inflammation/necrosis; loss of normal layered appearance suggests ischemia or advanced infection |\n| **Appendix** | Not clearly visualized | Suggests appendiceal perforation with collapse or destruction by inflammatory process |\n| **Fluid collection** | 6.5 × 5.0 cm loculated abscess with air-fluid level | Confirms perforation with contained leakage; size exceeds threshold for percutaneous drainage consideration |\n| **Mesenteric changes** | Fat stranding | Active inflammatory spread into mesentery |\n| **Free air** | Small amount present | **Critical finding** — indicates ongoing perforation/leak; suggests containment is incomplete |\n| **No tumor mass** | No definite mass seen | Makes malignancy less likely as primary etiology |\n\n**Severity indicators:** The combination of free intraperitoneal air + large abscess + loss of bowel wall stratification indicates **perforated viscus with inadequate containment** — this is Hinchey III/IV equivalent pathology requiring urgent source control.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Impact of Immunosuppression on Clinical Interpretation\n\n### Diagnostic Challenges:\n- **Blunted inflammatory response**: Despite WBC 18.6 and CRP 186, the true severity may be *underestimated* — immunosuppressed patients often present with less dramatic signs until very advanced\n- **Delayed presentation**: 5-day history with only 2 days of fever suggests the infection progressed significantly before systemic signs emerged\n- **Atypical course**: Normal immune responders typically declare themselves earlier; this patient's immunosuppression allowed silent progression to perforation\n\n### Therapeutic Implications:\n\n| Consideration | Impact |\n|--------------|--------|\n| **Infection control** | Higher risk of treatment failure with antibiotics alone; broader spectrum coverage needed |\n| **Wound healing** | Prednisone 7.5 mg/d + methotrexate → impaired healing, higher dehiscence risk |\n| **Medication management** | Immunosuppressants should be held perioperatively; consider stress-dose steroids |\n| **Opportunistic pathogens** | Consider atypical organisms beyond standard GI flora |\n| **Postoperative course** | Higher risk of anastomotic leak, wound infection, sepsis progression |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Antibiotics-Only vs. Surgical Intervention\n\n### **Recommendation: Surgery cannot be deferred**\n\n| Factor | Assessment |\n|--------|------------|\n| **Free intraperitoneal air** | Indicates *ongoing* perforation — antibiotics cannot seal a hole |\n| **Hemodynamic instability** | BP 96/58, HR 112, lactate 3.8 = septic shock |\n| **PCT 8.2 ng/mL** | Strongly suggests bacterial sepsis with poor source control |\n| **Abscess size** | 6.5 cm is drainable, BUT free air indicates drainage alone is insufficient |\n| **Immunocompromised state** | Significantly reduced likelihood of medical management success |\n| **Bowel obstruction** | Cessation of flatus/defecation indicates mechanical component |\n\n### Rationale Against Antibiotics-Only:\n1. **Source not controlled**: Perforation with free air = continuous contamination\n2. **Failed conservative window**: Patient already had 5 days of progression despite (presumably) some immune function\n3. **Sepsis trajectory**: Lactate 3.8 + hypotension indicates organ hypoperfusion — mortality increases hourly without source control\n4. **Immunosuppression**: Mathematical probability of medical management success is markedly reduced\n\n### **Priority Intervention: Emergency Surgical Exploration**\n\n**Preferred approach:**\n- **Damage control surgery** given physiological derangement\n- **Right hemicolectomy** vs. **ileocecal resection** with primary anastomosis *only if* patient stabilizes intraoperatively\n- **Stoma consideration**: Given immunosuppression, malnutrition (ALB 28), and sepsis — ileostomy may be safer than anastomosis\n- **Percutaneous drainage alone** is insufficient due to free air and ongoing leak\n\n---\n\n## 4. Expected Pathological Findings at Exploration\n\n| Likely Diagnosis | Expected Findings |\n|-----------------|-------------------|\n| **Perforated appendicitis** (most likely) | Gangrenous/perforated appendix with abscess; possible appendiceal stump blowout |\n| **Perforated cecal diverticulitis** | Inflamed diverticulum with localized perforation; more common in elderly |\n| **Typhlitis (neutropenic enterocolitis)** | Cecal wall necrosis; though typically in profound neutropenia |\n| **Crohn's disease perforation** | Terminal ileum thickening, creeping fat, skip lesions (less likely given no prior history) |\n| **Tuberculosis** | Ileocecal predilection; caseating granulomas; consider given immunosuppression |\n| **Malignancy with perforation** | Despite no mass on CT, infiltrative carcinoma possible |\n| **Actinomycosis** | Chronic granulomatous infection; \"wooden\" induration; rare but seen in immunosuppressed |\n\n### Most Probable:\n**Perforated appendicitis with abscess formation** — the ileocecal location, clinical timeline (5 days), and CT findings (abscess with air-fluid level, non-visualized appendix) are classic for this diagnosis. The immunosuppression allowed progression to perforation without earlier declaration.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Management Priorities\n\n1. **Immediate**: Fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics (piperacillin-tazobactam or carbapenem), vasopressors if needed\n2. **Hold**: Methotrexate and prednisone (give stress-dose hydrocortisone)\n3. **Urgent**: Operating room for source control — do not delay for additional imaging or antibiotic trial\n4. **Intraoperative decision**: Anastomosis vs. stoma based on contamination, hemodynamics, and tissue quality\n5. **Postoperative**: ICU care, glycemic control, nutritional support, DVT prophylaxis\n\nThis patient's mortality risk is substantial given age, comorbidities (CAD stent, CKD, diabetes), immunosuppression, and septic physiology — **expeditious source control is the single most important modifiable factor.**", "score": {"rubric_results": [{"rubric_number": 1, "weight": 7, "met": false, "reason": "模型明确反对推迟手术并指出抗生素无法封闭穿孔，但未提及抗生素在脓肿腔内渗透受限这一具体药理学理由。"}, {"rubric_number": 2, "weight": 6, "met": true, "reason": "模型在 CT 特征表中明确指出肠壁分层消失提示缺血或坏死，符合评分标准。"}, {"rubric_number": 3, "weight": 6, "met": true, "reason": "模型在 CT 特征及手术理由中均明确指出游离气体意味着穿孔或肠壁完整性受损。"}, {"rubric_number": 4, "weight": 7, "met": false, "reason": "模型列出了多种病理诊断（如阑尾炎、憩室炎等），但未将“局限性腹膜炎”明确列为诊断之一。"}, {"rubric_number": 5, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型详细阐述了免疫抑制状态会导致炎症反应迟钝、表现延迟及非典型病程，符合评分要求。"}, {"rubric_number": 6, "weight": -6, "met": false, "reason": "模型在探索预期发现中列出了阑尾炎、恶性肿瘤（肿瘤）和憩室炎，未出现遗漏，故不扣分。"}, {"rubric_number": 7, "weight": -15, "met": false, "reason": "模型使用“最可能（Most Probable）”的表述并保留了鉴别诊断，未做出绝对的确定性诊断，故不扣分。"}, {"rubric_number": 8, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型内容紧扣诊断与治疗决策，关于病原体的讨论与免疫抑制患者的抗生素选择相关，无明显冗余。"}, {"rubric_number": 9, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型全程使用专业医学术语，未发现非专业、口语化或不确定的表达，故不扣分。"}, {"rubric_number": 10, "weight": 10, "met": false, "reason": "模型在术前干预建议中未明确提及针对长期服用阿司匹林导致的出血风险的管理措施。"}, {"rubric_number": 11, "weight": 7, "met": true, "reason": "模型在药物管理及总结中明确提到了给予“应激剂量氢化可的松（stress-dose hydrocortisone）”。"}, {"rubric_number": 12, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型虽提及相关病史，但未明确建议在围手术期咨询肾内科和风湿免疫科医师进行 MDT 管理。"}], "score": 24, "max_score": 53, "raw_response": "[\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 1,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型明确反对推迟手术并指出抗生素无法封闭穿孔，但未提及抗生素在脓肿腔内渗透受限这一具体药理学理由。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 2,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在 CT 特征表中明确指出肠壁分层消失提示缺血或坏死，符合评分标准。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 3,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在 CT 特征及手术理由中均明确指出游离气体意味着穿孔或肠壁完整性受损。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 4,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型列出了多种病理诊断（如阑尾炎、憩室炎等），但未将“局限性腹膜炎”明确列为诊断之一。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 5,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型详细阐述了免疫抑制状态会导致炎症反应迟钝、表现延迟及非典型病程，符合评分要求。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 6,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在探索预期发现中列出了阑尾炎、恶性肿瘤（肿瘤）和憩室炎，未出现遗漏，故不扣分。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 7,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型使用“最可能（Most Probable）”的表述并保留了鉴别诊断，未做出绝对的确定性诊断，故不扣分。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 8,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型内容紧扣诊断与治疗决策，关于病原体的讨论与免疫抑制患者的抗生素选择相关，无明显冗余。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 9,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型全程使用专业医学术语，未发现非专业、口语化或不确定的表达，故不扣分。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 10,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在术前干预建议中未明确提及针对长期服用阿司匹林导致的出血风险的管理措施。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 11,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在药物管理及总结中明确提到了给予“应激剂量氢化可的松（stress-dose hydrocortisone）”。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 12,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型虽提及相关病史，但未明确建议在围手术期咨询肾内科和风湿免疫科医师进行 MDT 管理。\"\n  }\n]"}, "error": null, "stage": null, "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "user_prompt": "Case:\nPatient, female, 72 years old. Chief Complaint: \"Abdominal pain and fever accompanied by vomiting for 5 days, aggravated for 1 day.\" 5 days ago, persistent right lower quadrant pain appeared, accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and poor appetite. 2 days ago, fever ensued (maximum 38.9℃), and self-administered oral antibiotics were ineffective. In the last 24 hours, abdominal pain significantly worsened, with abdominal distension and cessation of flatus and defecation. Past History: Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus for 20 years, insulin therapy, HbA1c 8.9%. Rheumatoid Arthritis for 18 years, long-term oral methotrexate 12.5 mg/week + prednisone 7.5 mg/d. Coronary Artery Disease (CAD), stent placement 3 years ago, long-term aspirin use. Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD stage 3a). Physical Examination:\nT 38.5℃, HR 112 bpm, BP 96/58 mmHg. Abdominal distension, obvious tenderness in the right lower quadrant, rebound tenderness (+), muscle guarding (+), hypoactive bowel sounds.\nLaboratory Findings: WBC 18.6 ×10⁹/L (Neutrophils 89%), CRP 186 mg/L, PCT 8.2 ng/mL, Hb 102 g/L, ALB 28 g/L, Cr 136 μmol/L, Lactate 3.8 mmol/L. Imaging (Contrast-enhanced CT): Significant thickening of the intestinal wall in the ileocecal region, loss of local bowel wall stratification, appendix not clearly visualized. A 6.5 × 5.0 cm loculated fluid collection with an air-fluid level is seen in the right lower quadrant, with surrounding mesenteric fat stranding and a small amount of free intraperitoneal air; no definite tumor mass observed.\n\nPlease answer the following questions:\n1. Based on the contrast-enhanced CT results, summarize the main radiographic features of the ileocecal lesion in this case and explain their significance in judging the severity of the condition.\n2. The patient has long-standing rheumatoid arthritis and uses immunosuppressive drugs; how does this background influence the interpretation of clinical presentation and therapeutic decision-making?\n3. Given the current condition, is there room to continue intensifying antibiotics and defer surgery? Please explain the rationale; if intervention is required, what mode should be prioritized?\n4. If emergency surgical exploration is performed, what pathological conditions might be encountered in the ileocecal region?", "response": {"request_id": "omb-batch", "status": "completed", "session_id": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "workspace": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__3596__global/omb-batch", "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the 