/mL, Hb 102 g/L, ALB 28 g/L, Cr 136 μmol/L, Lactate 3.8 mmol/L. Imaging (Contrast-enhanced CT): Significant thickening of the intestinal wall in the ileocecal region, loss of local bowel wall stratification, appendix not clearly visualized. A 6.5 × 5.0 cm loculated fluid collection with an air-fluid level is seen in the right lower quadrant, with surrounding mesenteric fat stranding and a small amount of free intraperitoneal air; no definite tumor mass observed.\n\nPlease answer the following questions:\n1. Based on the contrast-enhanced CT results, summarize the main radiographic features of the ileocecal lesion in this case and explain their significance in judging the severity of the condition.\n2. The patient has long-standing rheumatoid arthritis and uses immunosuppressive drugs; how does this background influence the interpretation of clinical presentation and therapeutic decision-making?\n3. Given the current condition, is there room to continue intensifying antibiotics and defer surgery? Please explain the rationale; if intervention is required, what mode should be prioritized?\n4. If emergency surgical exploration is performed, what pathological conditions might be encountered in the ileocecal region?", "session_data": {"scope_id": "8ee9b3c77ecc318a5536e408e362ab71c9ea0001", "session_id": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "info": {"id": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "version": "local", "scopeID": "8ee9b3c77ecc318a5536e408e362ab71c9ea0001", "directory": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__3596__global/omb-batch", "title": "onemillion:healthcare_and_medicine/3596/global", "time": {"created": 1773730468288, "updated": 1773730468403}, "summary": {"additions": 0, "deletions": 0, "files": 0}}, "messages": [{"id": "msg_cfa9311d6001FLCwUV2GXN4OJg", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa9311d6001FLCwUV2GXN4OJg", "sessionID": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "role": "user", "time": {"created": 1773730468310}, "summary": {"title": "Ileocecal lesion features, immunosuppression impact, treatment options", "diffs": []}, "agent": "synergy", "model": {"providerID": "sii-holos", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B"}}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa9311d700102ng4zY6KMNOBO", "sessionID": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "messageID": "msg_cfa9311d6001FLCwUV2GXN4OJg", "type": "text", "text": "\"Case:\nPatient, female, 72 years old. Chief Complaint: \\\"Abdominal pain and fever accompanied by vomiting for 5 days, aggravated for 1 day.\\\" 5 days ago, persistent right lower quadrant pain appeared, accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and poor appetite. 2 days ago, fever ensued (maximum 38.9℃), and self-administered oral antibiotics were ineffective. In the last 24 hours, abdominal pain significantly worsened, with abdominal distension and cessation of flatus and defecation. Past History: Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus for 20 years, insulin therapy, HbA1c 8.9%. Rheumatoid Arthritis for 18 years, long-term oral methotrexate 12.5 mg/week + prednisone 7.5 mg/d. Coronary Artery Disease (CAD), stent placement 3 years ago, long-term aspirin use. Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD stage 3a). Physical Examination:\nT 38.5℃, HR 112 bpm, BP 96/58 mmHg. Abdominal distension, obvious tenderness in the right lower quadrant, rebound tenderness (+), muscle guarding (+), hypoactive bowel sounds.\nLaboratory Findings: WBC 18.6 ×10⁹/L (Neutrophils 89%), CRP 186 mg/L, PCT 8.2 ng/mL, Hb 102 g/L, ALB 28 g/L, Cr 136 μmol/L, Lactate 3.8 mmol/L. Imaging (Contrast-enhanced CT): Significant thickening of the intestinal wall in the ileocecal region, loss of local bowel wall stratification, appendix not clearly visualized. A 6.5 × 5.0 cm loculated fluid collection with an air-fluid level is seen in the right lower quadrant, with surrounding mesenteric fat stranding and a small amount of free intraperitoneal air; no definite tumor mass observed.\n\nPlease answer the following questions:\n1. Based on the contrast-enhanced CT results, summarize the main radiographic features of the ileocecal lesion in this case and explain their significance in judging the severity of the condition.\n2. The patient has long-standing rheumatoid arthritis and uses immunosuppressive drugs; how does this background influence the interpretation of clinical presentation and therapeutic decision-making?\n3. Given the current condition, is there room to continue intensifying antibiotics and defer surgery? Please explain the rationale; if intervention is required, what mode should be prioritized?\n4. If emergency surgical exploration is performed, what pathological conditions might be encountered in the ileocecal region?\"\n"}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa93120a0017w6yWA1j8fBH6V", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa93120a0017w6yWA1j8fBH6V", "sessionID": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730468362, "completed": 1773730596372}, "parentID": "msg_cfa9311d6001FLCwUV2GXN4OJg", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__3596__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__3596__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26258, "output": 1713, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa93382e001JD0Nt15fZshkga", "sessionID": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "messageID": "msg_cfa93120a0017w6yWA1j8fBH6V", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa93382f001u94HpMiP1RDfaJ", "sessionID": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "messageID": "msg_cfa93120a0017w6yWA1j8fBH6V", "type": "reasoning", "text": "This is a complex clinical case involving an elderly patient with multiple comorbidities presenting with what appears to be a severe intra-abdominal infection. Let me analyze each question systematically.\n\nLet me work through this case carefully:\n\n**Key Clinical Features:**\n- 72-year-old female\n- 5 days of RLQ pain, fever, vomiting\n- Immunocompromised (RA on methotrexate + prednisone, diabetes, CKD)\n- Septic physiology (T 38.5℃, HR 112, BP 96/58, lactate 3.8)\n- CT shows: ileocecal wall thickening, loss of stratification, 6.5cm abscess with air-fluid level, free intraperitoneal air\n- Laboratory markers of severe infection (WBC 18.6, CRP 186, PCT 8.2)\n\nThis appears to be a perforated appendiceal/ileocecal abscess with sepsis in an immunocompromised host.\n\nLet me answer each question in detail based on surgical and medical principles.", "time": {"start": 1773730478127, "end": 1773730596336}}, {"id": "prt_cfa93778100199wu0s1BVrSEng", "sessionID": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "messageID": "msg_cfa93120a0017w6yWA1j8fBH6V", "type": "text", "text": "\n\nThis is a complex surgical case involving an immunocompromised elderly patient with severe intra-abdominal sepsis. Let me address each question systematically.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CT Radiographic Features and Severity Assessment\n\n| Feature | Finding | Clinical Significance |\n|---------|---------|----------------------|\n| **Bowel wall changes** | Significant thickening, loss of stratification | Indicates transmural inflammation/necrosis; loss of normal layered appearance suggests ischemia or advanced infection |\n| **Appendix** | Not clearly visualized | Suggests appendiceal perforation with collapse or destruction by inflammatory process |\n| **Fluid collection** | 6.5 × 5.0 cm loculated abscess with air-fluid level | Confirms perforation with contained leakage; size exceeds threshold for percutaneous drainage consideration |\n| **Mesenteric changes** | Fat stranding | Active inflammatory spread into mesentery |\n| **Free air** | Small amount present | **Critical finding** — indicates ongoing perforation/leak; suggests containment is incomplete |\n| **No tumor mass** | No definite mass seen | Makes malignancy less likely as primary etiology |\n\n**Severity indicators:** The combination of free intraperitoneal air + large abscess + loss of bowel wall stratification indicates **perforated viscus with inadequate containment** — this is Hinchey III/IV equivalent pathology requiring urgent source control.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Impact of Immunosuppression on Clinical Interpretation\n\n### Diagnostic Challenges:\n- **Blunted inflammatory response**: Despite WBC 18.6 and CRP 186, the true severity may be *underestimated* — immunosuppressed patients often present with less dramatic signs until very advanced\n- **Delayed presentation**: 5-day history with only 2 days of fever suggests the infection progressed significantly before systemic signs emerged\n- **Atypical course**: Normal immune responders typically declare themselves earlier; this patient's immunosuppression allowed silent progression to perforation\n\n### Therapeutic Implications:\n\n| Consideration | Impact |\n|--------------|--------|\n| **Infection control** | Higher risk of treatment failure with antibiotics alone; broader spectrum coverage needed |\n| **Wound healing** | Prednisone 7.5 mg/d + methotrexate → impaired healing, higher dehiscence risk |\n| **Medication management** | Immunosuppressants should be held perioperatively; consider stress-dose steroids |\n| **Opportunistic pathogens** | Consider atypical organisms beyond standard GI flora |\n| **Postoperative course** | Higher risk of anastomotic leak, wound infection, sepsis progression |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Antibiotics-Only vs. Surgical Intervention\n\n### **Recommendation: Surgery cannot be deferred**\n\n| Factor | Assessment |\n|--------|------------|\n| **Free intraperitoneal air** | Indicates *ongoing* perforation — antibiotics cannot seal a hole |\n| **Hemodynamic instability** | BP 96/58, HR 112, lactate 3.8 = septic shock |\n| **PCT 8.2 ng/mL** | Strongly suggests bacterial sepsis with poor source control |\n| **Abscess size** | 6.5 cm is drainable, BUT free air indicates drainage alone is insufficient |\n| **Immunocompromised state** | Significantly reduced likelihood of medical management success |\n| **Bowel obstruction** | Cessation of flatus/defecation indicates mechanical component |\n\n### Rationale Against Antibiotics-Only:\n1. **Source not controlled**: Perforation with free air = continuous contamination\n2. **Failed conservative window**: Patient already had 5 days of progression despite (presumably) some immune function\n3. **Sepsis trajectory**: Lactate 3.8 + hypotension indicates organ hypoperfusion — mortality increases hourly without source control\n4. **Immunosuppression**: Mathematical probability of medical management success is markedly reduced\n\n### **Priority Intervention: Emergency Surgical Exploration**\n\n**Preferred approach:**\n- **Damage control surgery** given physiological derangement\n- **Right hemicolectomy** vs. **ileocecal resection** with primary anastomosis *only if* patient stabilizes intraoperatively\n- **Stoma consideration**: Given immunosuppression, malnutrition (ALB 28), and sepsis — ileostomy may be safer than anastomosis\n- **Percutaneous drainage alone** is insufficient due to free air and ongoing leak\n\n---\n\n## 4. Expected Pathological Findings at Exploration\n\n| Likely Diagnosis | Expected Findings |\n|-----------------|-------------------|\n| **Perforated appendicitis** (most likely) | Gangrenous/perforated appendix with abscess; possible appendiceal stump blowout |\n| **Perforated cecal diverticulitis** | Inflamed diverticulum with localized perforation; more common in elderly |\n| **Typhlitis (neutropenic enterocolitis)** | Cecal wall necrosis; though typically in profound neutropenia |\n| **Crohn's disease perforation** | Terminal ileum thickening, creeping fat, skip lesions (less likely given no prior history) |\n| **Tuberculosis** | Ileocecal predilection; caseating granulomas; consider given immunosuppression |\n| **Malignancy with perforation** | Despite no mass on CT, infiltrative carcinoma possible |\n| **Actinomycosis** | Chronic granulomatous infection; \"wooden\" induration; rare but seen in immunosuppressed |\n\n### Most Probable:\n**Perforated appendicitis with abscess formation** — the ileocecal location, clinical timeline (5 days), and CT findings (abscess with air-fluid level, non-visualized appendix) are classic for this diagnosis. The immunosuppression allowed progression to perforation without earlier declaration.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Management Priorities\n\n1. **Immediate**: Fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics (piperacillin-tazobactam or carbapenem), vasopressors if needed\n2. **Hold**: Methotrexate and prednisone (give stress-dose hydrocortisone)\n3. **Urgent**: Operating room for source control — do not delay for additional imaging or antibiotic trial\n4. **Intraoperative decision**: Anastomosis vs. stoma based on contamination, hemodynamics, and tissue quality\n5. **Postoperative**: ICU care, glycemic control, nutritional support, DVT prophylaxis\n\nThis patient's mortality risk is substantial given age, comorbidities (CAD stent, CKD, diabetes), immunosuppression, and septic physiology — **expeditious source control is the single most important modifiable factor.**", "time": {"start": 1773730596336, "end": 1773730596336}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9505f3001q80mFgaowNf2BC", "sessionID": "ses_3056cee3fffeodT6eUP3jqv18z", "messageID": "msg_cfa93120a0017w6yWA1j8fBH6V", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26258, "output": 1713, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "This is a complex surgical case involving an immunocompromised elderly patient with severe intra-abdominal sepsis. Let me address each question systematically.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CT Radiographic Features 