, "type": "step-finish", "reason": "tool-calls", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 25844, "output": 317, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa9463eb001tvuQjoWR16ID4v", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa9463eb001tvuQjoWR16ID4v", "sessionID": "ses_3056be963ffeccFb5xfI1q42n3", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730554859, "completed": 1773730633581}, "parentID": "msg_cfa9416b0001h3a7u25hf0t1om", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8479__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8479__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26106, "output": 1614, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa947891001hdyV8Igxm1Bvho", "sessionID": "ses_3056be963ffeccFb5xfI1q42n3", "messageID": "msg_cfa9463eb001tvuQjoWR16ID4v", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa94797100183cYfchUoeQUSh", "sessionID": "ses_3056be963ffeccFb5xfI1q42n3", "messageID": "msg_cfa9463eb001tvuQjoWR16ID4v", "type": "text", "text": "\n\nI cannot access external sources at the moment, but I can provide you with comprehensive recommendations based on established best practices for methyltransferase assays using the MTase-Glo system and SETDB1 enzymology. Here are the key protocol optimization strategies:\n\n## **Critical Protocol Recommendations for SETDB1 MTase-Glo Assay Stability**\n\n### **1. S-Adenosylmethionine (SAM) Cofactor Handling**\n\nSAM is highly labile and a major source of variability:\n- **Aliquot SAM immediately upon receipt** and store at -80°C; avoid freeze-thaw cycles\n- **Prepare fresh SAM working solutions** for each assay plate; do not reuse\n- **Keep SAM on ice** during assay setup; it degrades rapidly at room temperature\n- **Verify SAM concentration** spectrophotometrically if variability persists (ε260 = 15,400 M⁻¹cm⁻¹)\n- **Optimize SAM concentration** for SETDB1 specifically (typical range: 10-40 μM; titrate to find saturating concentration)\n\n### **2. Reaction Buffer Optimization**\n\nSETDB1 has specific buffer requirements:\n- **Include 5-10 mM DTT or TCEP** in all buffers; SETDB1 contains zinc-binding domains sensitive to oxidation\n- **Add 0.01% BSA or 0.1 mg/mL acetylated BSA** to reduce protein adsorption to plate surfaces\n- **Maintain consistent ionic strength**: KCl/NaCl concentration significantly affects SETDB1 activity (typically 50-150 mM)\n- **Include 5-10% glycerol** to stabilize enzyme during setup\n- **Use HEPES (pH 7.5-8.0) or Tris-HCl (pH 8.0-8.5)**; avoid phosphate buffers which can chelate divalent cations\n- **Add 1-5 mM MgCl₂** if required for SETDB1 construct (verify for your specific construct)\n\n### **3. Temperature and Timing Control**\n\n- **Pre-equilibrate all reagents** to reaction temperature before mixing (typically 30-37°C for SETDB1)\n- **Use a thermocycler or temperature-controlled plate reader** for reaction incubation (±0.5°C stability)\n- **Standardize reaction timing precisely**: Use a multichannel pipette and work quickly; consider staggered starts if processing multiple plates\n- **Quench reactions consistently**: Add MTase-Glo Detection Reagent at exactly the same time point for all wells\n- **Avoid edge effects**: Use plate sealers; avoid using outer wells or fill them with buffer only\n\n### **4. Enzyme Handling**\n\nEven from the same batch, enzyme handling introduces variability:\n- **Aliquot SETDB1 protein** upon purification; avoid freeze-thaw cycles\n- **Keep enzyme on ice** during assay setup; return to -80°C immediately after use\n- **Prepare enzyme dilution fresh** in cold buffer with 0.1 mg/mL BSA immediately before assay\n- **Optimize enzyme concentration** to work in the linear range (typically 10-100 nM for SETDB1; perform titration)\n- **Verify enzyme stability**: Test activity immediately after thawing vs. after 2 hours on ice to assess degradation\n\n### **5. Substrate (Histone H3) Considerations**\n\n- **Verify histone H3 is reduced**: Free cysteines should be available; add 1 mM DTT during substrate preparation\n- **Avoid histone aggregation**: Centrifuge histone stock at 14,000 × g for 5 min before use to remove aggregates\n- **Standardize substrate concentration**: Work at or above Km for H3 (typically 1-10 μM for SETDB1)\n- **Check substrate quality**: Run SDS-PAGE to verify full-length H3 integrity; degradation causes variability\n\n### **6. MTase-Glo Detection System Optimization**\n\n- **Bring Detection Reagent to room temperature** before use (30 min equilibration)\n- **Mix Detection Reagent thoroughly** but gently; avoid foam formation\n- **Use consistent luminescence integration time** (typically 0.5-1 second per well)\n- **Allow plates to equilibrate to room temperature** after adding detection reagent (15-30 min)\n- **Read plates immediately** after equilibration; signal decays over time\n- **Include no-enzyme controls** on every plate for background subtraction\n- **Include SAM-only controls** to detect non-enzymatic methylation or contamination\n\n### **7. Plate and Pipetting Technique**\n\n- **Use low-binding white plates** (Promega recommends 96-well or 384-well white plates)\n- **Pre-wet pipette tips** with enzyme/substrate solutions to reduce adsorption losses\n- **Use calibrated pipettes** and verify volumes with gravimetric testing\n- **Mix reactions gently** by pipetting up/down 3-5 times; avoid vortexing which can denature enzyme\n- **Centrifuge plates briefly** (200 × g, 1 min) before incubation to ensure all liquid is at well bottom\n\n### **8. Experimental Design for Stability**\n\n- **Include technical triplicates minimum**; quadruplicates preferred for critical experiments\n- **Randomize sample positions** across plates to avoid positional bias\n- **Include a positive control** on every plate (e.g., a high-activity SETDB1 aliquot stored as reference)\n- **Use inter-plate calibrators** if comparing across multiple plates\n- **Process all samples in the same session** when possible; avoid comparing assays run on different days\n\n### **9. Data Quality Controls**\n\n- **Calculate Z'-factor** for each plate: Z' > 0.5 indicates acceptable assay quality\n- **Monitor coefficient of variation (CV)**: Technical replicates should have CV < 10%\n- **Verify signal-to-background ratio**: Should be > 3:1 for reliable detection\n- **Plot raw luminescence values** to identify outlier wells or plate patterns\n\n### **10. Common SETDB1-Specific Issues**\n\n- **SETDB1 requires proper folding**: Verify protein is monodisperse by size-exclusion chromatography\n- **Check for autoinhibition**: Full-length SETDB1 may be autoinhibited; consider using catalytic domain construct (SET domain alone) for more consistent activity\n- **Verify oligomeric state**: SETDB1 can form oligomers; ensure consistent preparation method\n- **Consider post-translational modifications**: Phosphorylation or other PTMs may affect activity; verify protein modification state is consistent\n\n---\n\n## **Recommended Protocol Optimization Workflow**\n\n1. **Week 1**: Perform enzyme titration (5 concentrations) × SAM titration (5 concentrations) matrix to identify optimal linear range\n2. **Week 2**: Test buffer conditions (DTT concentration, salt concentration, pH) using optimal enzyme/SAM from Week 1\n3. **Week 3**: Validate timing and temperature parameters; establish standard operating procedure\n4. **Week 4**: Run pilot experiments with full controls to establish baseline variability\n\nBy systematically addressing each of these factors, you should achieve CV < 10% across technical replicates and stable results across experiments. The most common culprits are **SAM degradation**, **inconsistent temperature control**, and **enzyme handling variability** — prioritize these first.", "time": {"start": 1773730633572, "end": 1773730633572}}, {"id": "prt_cfa959766001XTcHc25t7YQuLe", "sessionID": "ses_3056be963ffeccFb5xfI1q42n3", "messageID": "msg_cfa9463eb001tvuQjoWR16ID4v", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26106, "output": 1614, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "I cannot access external sources at the moment, but I can provide you with comprehensive recommendations based on established best practices for methyltransferase assays using the MTase-Glo system and SETDB1 enzymology. Here are the key protocol optimization strategies:\n\n## **Critical Protocol Recommendations for SETDB1 MTase-Glo Assay Stability**\n\n### **1. S-Adenosylmethionine (SAM) Cofactor Handling**\n\nSAM is highly labile and a major source of variability:\n- **Aliquot SAM immediately upon receipt** and store at -80°C; avoid freeze-thaw cycles\n- **Prepare fresh SAM working solutions** for each assay plate; do not reuse\n- **Keep SAM on ice** during assay setup; it degrades rapidly at room temperature\n- **Verify SAM concentration** spectrophotometrically if variability persists (ε260 = 15,400 M⁻¹cm⁻¹)\n- **Optimize SAM concentration** for SETDB1 specifically (typical range: 10-40 μM; titrate to find saturating concentration)\n\n### **2. Reaction Buffer Optimization**\n\nSETDB1 has specific buffer requirements:\n- **Include 5-10 mM DTT or TCEP** in all buffers; SETDB1 contains zinc-binding domains sensitive to oxidation\n- **Add 0.01% BSA or 0.1 mg/mL acetylated BSA** to reduce protein adsorption to plate surfaces\n- **Maintain consistent ionic strength**: KCl/NaCl concentration significantly affects SETDB1 activity (typically 50-150 mM)\n- **Include 5-10% glycerol** to stabilize enzyme during setup\n- **Use HEPES (pH 7.5-8.0) or Tris-HCl (pH 8.0-8.5)**; avoid phosphate buffers which can chelate divalent cations\n- **Add 1-5 mM MgCl₂** if required for SETDB1 construct (verify for your specific construct)\n\n### **3. Temperature and Timing Control**\n\n- **Pre-equilibrate all reagents** to reaction temperature before mixing (typically 30-37°C for SETDB1)\n- **Use a thermocycler or temperature-controlled plate reader** for reaction incubation (±0.5°C stability)\n- **Standardize reaction timing precisely**: Use a multichannel pipette and work quickly; consider staggered starts if processing multiple plates\n- **Quench reactions consistently**: Add MTase-Glo Detection Reagent at exactly the same time point for all wells\n- **Avoid edge effects**: Use plate sealers; avoid using outer wells or fill them with buffer only\n\n### **4. Enzyme Handling**\n\nEven from the same batch, enzyme handling introduces variability:\n- **Aliquot SETDB1 protein** upon purification; avoid freeze-thaw cycles\n- **Keep enzyme on ice** during assay setup; return to -80°C immediately after use\n- **Prepare enzyme dilution fresh** in cold buffer with 0.1 mg/mL BSA immediately before assay\n- **Optimize enzyme concentration** to work in the linear range (typically 10-100 nM for SETDB1; perform titration)\n- **Verify enzyme stability**: Test activity immediately after thawing vs. after 2 hours on ice to assess degradation\n\n### **5. Substrate (Histone H3) Considerations**\n\n- **Verify histone H3 is reduced**: Free cysteines should be available; add 1 mM DTT during substrate preparation\n- **Avoid histone aggregation**: Centrifuge histone stock at 14,000 × g for 5 min before use to remove aggregates\n- **Standardize substrate concentration**: Work at or above Km for H3 (typically 1-10 μM for SETDB1)\n- **Check substrate quality**: Run SDS-PAGE to verify full-length H3 integrity; degradation causes variability\n\n### **6. MTase-Glo Detection System Optimization**\n\n- **Bring Detection Reagent to room temperature** before use (30 min equilibration)\n- **Mix Detection Reagent thoroughly** but gently; avoid foam formation\n- **Use consistent luminescence integration time** (typically 0.5-1 second per well)\n- **Allow plates to equilibrate to room temperature** after adding detection reagent (15-30 min)\n- **Read plates immediately** after equilibration; signal decays over time\n- **Include no-enzyme controls** on every plate for background subtraction\n- **Include SAM-only controls** to detect non-enzymatic methylation or contamination\n\n### **7. Plate and Pipetting Technique**\n\n- **Use low-binding white plates** (Promega recommends 96-well or 384-well white plates)\n- **Pre-wet pipette tips** with enzyme/substrate solutions to reduce adsorption losses\n- **Use calibrated pipettes** and verify volumes with gravimetric testing\n- **Mix reactions gently** by pipetting up/down 3-5 times; avoid vortexing which can denature enzyme\n- **Centrifuge plates briefly** (200 × g, 1 min) before incubation to ensure all liquid is at well bottom\n\n### **8. Experimental Design for Stability**\n\n- **Include technical triplicates minimum**; quadruplicates preferred for critical experiments\n- **Randomize sample positions** across plates to avoid positional bias\n- **Include a positive control** on every plate (e.g., a high-activity SETDB1 aliquot stored as reference)\n- **Use inter-plate calibrators** if comparing across multiple plates\n- **Process all samples in the same session** when possible; avoid comparing assays run on different days\n\n### **9. Data Quality Controls**\n\n- **Calculate Z'-factor** for each plate: Z' > 0.5 indicates acceptable assay quality\n- **Monitor coefficient of variation (CV)**: Technical replicates should have CV < 10%\n- **Verify signal-to-background ratio**: Should be > 3:1 for reliable detection\n- **Plot raw luminescence values** to identify outlier wells or plate patterns\n\n### **10. Common SETDB1-Specific Issues**\n\n- **SETDB1 requires proper folding**: Verify protein is monodisperse by size-exclusion chromatography\n- **Check for autoinhibition**: Full-length SETDB1 may be autoinhibited; consider using catalytic domain construct (SET domain alone) for more consistent activity\n- **Verify oligomeric state**: SETDB1 can form oligomers; ensure consistent preparation method\n- **Consider post-translational modifications**: Phosphorylation or other PTMs may affect activity; verify protein modification state is consistent\n\n---\n\n## **Recommended Protocol Optimization Workflow**\n\n1. **Week 1**: Perform enzyme titration (5 concentrations) × SAM titration (5 concentrations) matrix to identify optimal linear range\n2. **Week 2**: Test buffer conditions (DTT concentration, salt concentration, pH) using optimal enzyme/SAM from Week 1\n3. **Week 3**: Validate timing and temperature parameters; establish standard operating procedure\n4. **Week 4**: Run pilot experiments with full controls to establish baseline variability\n\nBy systematically addressing each of these factors, you should achieve CV < 10% across technical replicates and stable results across experiments. The most common culprits are **SAM degradation**, **inconsistent temperature control**, and **enzyme handling variability** — prioritize these first.", "case_id": 8479, "language": "global", "tags": {"topics": ["Natural Sciences", "Biology", "Biochemistry"], "time_sensitivity": {"time_sensitivity": "Time-agnostic", "year_month": "NA", "day": "NA"}}, "rubric_count": 18}, "metrics": {"solve_time_seconds": 100.8, "tokens": {"input": 51950, "output": 1931, "total": 53881}, "cost": 0.0, "step_count": 2}, "error": ""}}
{"task_id": "natural_science/8583/global", "answer": "# Mutational Analysis of ENSX and NXF1 Gene\n\n## 1. Mutation Type Identification\n\n### Sequence Comparison\n\n| Position | Wild-type | Patient | Change |\n|----------|-----------|---------|--------|\n| Codon 3 | `AAG` | `UAG` | A → U (single base substitution) |\n\n**Mutation Type:** **Point mutation (single base substitution)**\n- Specifically: **Transition mutation** (purine A → pyrimidine U... actually this is a transversion)\n- Correction: A (purine) → U (pyrimidine) = **Transversion mutation**\n\n### DNA Level Change\n\nSince the cDNA sequences represent mRNA:\n- **Wild-type DNA (coding strand):** `5'-...AAG...-3'`\n- **Patient DNA (coding strand):** `5'-...TAG...-3'`\n- **Mutation:** **A → T substitution** on the coding strand\n\n---\n\n## 2. Amino Acid Analysis\n\n| Codon | Encodes | Classification |\n|-------|---------|----------------|\n| `AAG` | **Lysine (Lys/K)** | Basic, positively charged amino acid |\n| `UAG` | **STOP codon** | Amber termination codon |\n\n### Direct Impact on Protein\n\nThis is a **nonsense mutation** creating a **premature termination codon (PTC)**:\n\n```\nWild-type:  Met---[Exon1-6]---Lys---[Exon7-12]---Stop\n                    (AAG)\n                    \nPatient:    Met---[Exon1-6]---STOP\n                    (UAG)\n                    ↑\n              Truncated protein (~50% length)\n```\n\n**Consequences:**\n1. **Truncated protein** - Only exons 1-6 translated (~50% of normal protein)\n2. **Loss of critical domains** - NXF1's C-terminal domains essential for function\n3. **Possible NMD** - mRNA may be degraded via Nonsense-Mediated Decay\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cell Type Vulnerability Hypothesis\n\n### Most Severely Affected Cell Types\n\n| Cell Type | Reason for Vulnerability |\n|-----------|-------------------------|\n| **Neurons** (especially CNS) | Post-mitotic, high protein turnover, long axons require efficient mRNA transport |\n| **Motor neurons** | Extended cellular processes demand robust mRNA export |\n| **Cells with high secretory demand** | Require continuous high-level protein synthesis |\n\n### Why Neurons Are Particularly Vulnerable\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Neuronal Vulnerability to NXF1 Deficiency              │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  1. Post-mitotic state → Cannot dilute defect via       │\n│     cell division; damage accumulates                   │\n│                                                         │\n│  2. Polarized structure → mRNA must be exported and     │\n│     transported to dendrites/axons for local translation│\n│                                                         │\n│  3. High metabolic demand → Continuous protein synthesis│\n│     required for synaptic function                      │\n│                                                         │\n│  4. Long-lived cells → Cumulative export defects lead   │\n│     to neurodegeneration                                │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Global Gene Expression Effects\n\n### Molecular Cascade\n\n```\nNXF1 haploinsufficiency/loss\n        ↓\nImpaired nuclear mRNA export\n        ↓\n┌───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐\n│   Nuclear retention   │   Cytoplasmic mRNA    │\n│   of poly(A)+ RNA     │   depletion           │\n│                       │                       │\n│   • mRNA accumulates  │   • Reduced translation│\n│     in nucleus        │   • Protein synthesis  │\n│   • Nuclear foci      │     decline           │\n│     formation         │   • Cellular stress   │\n└───────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘\n        ↓\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Cellular Consequences                        │\n│  • Unfolded Protein Response (UPR) activation │\n│  • Integrated Stress Response (ISR)           │\n│  • Apoptosis pathway activation               │\n│  • Eventual cell death                        │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Specific Effects on Gene Expression\n\n1. **Global mRNA export defect** - All mRNAs dependent on NXF1 affected\n2. **Nuclear accumulation** - Visible as nuclear poly(A)+ RNA foci\n3. **Reduced protein synthesis** - Global translation decline\n4. **Stress response activation** - UPR, ISR pathways triggered\n5. **Cell-type specific sensitivity** - Neurons most vulnerable\n\n---\n\n## 5. Experimental Design to Verify Hypothesis\n\n### Experimental Framework\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    EXPERIMENTAL VERIFICATION PLAN                │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nLevel 1: Molecular Confirmation\nLevel 2: Cellular Phenotype  \nLevel 3: Functional Assays\nLevel 4: Patient Sample Validation\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 1: Molecular Confirmation**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Expected Result (Patient) |\n|------------|--------|--------------------------|\n| **Genotyping** | Sanger sequencing of Exon 7 | Confirm A→T mutation |\n| **mRNA analysis** | RT-PCR + sequencing | Detect mutant transcript; assess NMD |\n| **Protein analysis** | Western blot (N-term vs C-term antibodies) | Truncated protein or reduced full-length |\n| **NMD assessment** | qPCR ± cycloheximide treatment | Increased mutant mRNA with NMD inhibition |\n\n**Key reagents needed:**\n- Antibodies against NXF1 N-terminus (exons 1-6)\n- Antibodies against NXF1 C-terminus (exons 8-12)\n- Patient and control cell lines\n\n---\n\n### **Level 2: Cellular Phenotype**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Expected Result |\n|------------|--------|-----------------|\n| **mRNA localization** | FISH with oligo(dT) probe | Nuclear poly(A)+ RNA accumulation in patient cells |\n| **NXF1 localization** | Immunofluorescence | Altered NXF1 distribution (if protein stable) |\n| **Nuclear morphology** | DAPI + nuclear envelope markers | Check for nuclear stress indicators |\n| **Cell viability** | Apoptosis assays (TUNEL, Annexin V) | Increased cell death in patient neurons |\n\n**FISH Protocol Outline:**\n```\n1. Fix patient and control fibroblasts\n2. Hybridize with fluorescent oligo(dT) probe\n3. Counterstain with DAPI\n4. Image by confocal microscopy\n5. Quantify nuclear vs cytoplasmic signal ratio\n\nExpected: Patient cells show increased nuclear poly(A)+ signal\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 3: Functional Assays**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Purpose |\n|------------|--------|---------|\n| **mRNA export assay** | Microinjection of fluorescent mRNA reporter | Direct measurement of export kinetics |\n| **Polysome profiling** | Sucrose gradient centrifugation | Assess global translation efficiency |\n| **RNA-seq** | Nuclear vs cytoplasmic fractionation | Identify affected transcripts |\n| **Rescue experiment** | Express wild-type NXF1 in patient cells | Confirm causality |\n\n**Rescue Experiment Design:**\n```\nPatient fibroblasts → Transfect with WT NXF1 expression vector\n                    → Assess:\n                      • mRNA export (FISH)\n                      • Cell viability\n                      • Protein synthesis rates\n                    \nControl: Empty vector transfection\n\nExpected: WT NXF1 restores normal phenotype\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 4: Patient Sample Validation**\n\n| Sample Type | Experiments |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Skin fibroblasts** | Primary cells for molecular/cellular assays |\n| **Blood samples** | Lymphoblastoid cell lines; RNA for transcript analysis |\n| **iPSC-derived neurons** | Differentiate patient fibroblasts → neurons for disease-relevant model |\n\n**iPSC Disease Model:**\n```\nPatient fibroblasts → Reprogram to iPSCs → Differentiate to neurons\n                                                    ↓\n                                    Most relevant cell type for ENSX\n                                                    ↓\n                                    Assess: mRNA export, viability, \n                                            neurodegeneration markers\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Summary of Key Predictions to Test**\n\n| Prediction | Assay | Confirmation Criteria |\n|------------|-------|----------------------|\n| PTC present | Sequencing | A→T mutation in Exon 7 |\n| Truncated protein | Western blot | C-terminal epitope loss |\n| Nuclear mRNA retention | FISH | Increased nuclear poly(A)+ signal |\n| Reduced protein synthesis | Polysome profiling | Decreased polysome fraction |\n| Neuronal vulnerability | iPSC-neurons | Greater defect vs fibroblasts |\n| Causality | Rescue experiment | WT NXF1 restores phenotype |\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe ENSX mutation is a **nonsense mutation (A→T transversion)** in Exon 7 of NXF1, converting a Lysine codon (AAG) to a premature STOP codon (UAG). This produces a truncated, non-functional NXF1 protein that cannot properly export mRNA from nucleus to cytoplasm.\n\n**Neurons are most severely affected** due to their post-mitotic nature, high protein synthesis demands, and polarized structure requiring efficient mRNA transport. The global consequence is **nuclear mRNA accumulation with cytoplasmic depletion**, leading to cellular stress and eventual neurodegeneration.\n\nThe experimental framework above provides a systematic approach to validate this molecular diagnosis and establish the pathogenic mechanism of ENSX.", "score": {"rubric_results": [{"rubric_number": 1, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型明确指出了突变类型为单碱基替换（点突变），并准确说明了 mRNA 水平为 A→U，DNA 水平为 A→T。"}, {"rubric_number": 2, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型在 DNA Level Change 部分准确陈述了编码链上的变化为 Adenine (A) 到 Thymine (T)。"}, {"rubric_number": 3, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型在表格中明确指出野生型密码子 AAG 编码 Lysine，并提供了三字母缩写 Lys 和单字母缩写 K。"}, {"rubric_number": 4, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型准确识别 UAG 为 STOP codon（终止密码子）或 nonsense codon（无义密码子）。"}, {"rubric_number": 5, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型通过序列分析得出结论，AAG→UAG 导致翻译提前终止，生成截短的、结构不完整的 NXF1 蛋白。"}, {"rubric_number": 6, "weight": 10, "met": true, "reason": "模型解释了 NXF1 失活阻断了 mRNA 从核到质的运输，导致细胞质中翻译模板减少，从而抑制蛋白质合成，符合中心法则关键步骤中断的逻辑。"}, {"rubric_number": 7, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型不仅指出了 mRNA 核滞留的表型，还明确将其机制链接到了 NXF1 核输出功能的受损，未出现评分项描述的‘未能指定机制’的错误。"}, {"rubric_number": 8, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型陈述了 NXF1 功能失活阻断了 mRNA 运输核心通路，导致细胞质中成熟 mRNA 全面减少，进而引起细胞内蛋白质合成的广泛抑制。"}, {"rubric_number": 9, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "实验设计 Level 1 中明确包含了使用 Sanger 测序在基因组 DNA 水平验证突变的步骤。"}, {"rubric_number": 10, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "实验设计包含了 Western Blot 分析，并说明其目的是检测截短蛋白或全长蛋白减少（即表达丰度和分子量）。"}, {"rubric_number": 11, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型虽然提到了 Western Blot 检测截短蛋白，但未明确陈述观察到的条带作为直接分子证据的逻辑语句，也未按要求将该证据陈述加粗。"}, {"rubric_number": 12, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型包含了 RNA-FISH 和 Western Blot，但未明确文字说明它们相互佐证以形成完整的证据链。"}, {"rubric_number": 13, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型预测的 RNA-FISH 结果是患者细胞核内 poly(A)+ 信号增加（异常滞留），而非评分项描述的‘正常、高效的 mRNA 输出’，未命中扣分条件。"}, {"rubric_number": 14, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "实验设计仅包含一种功能回复验证步骤（表达野生型 NXF1），未包含至少两种（如 CRISPR 或通读药物）。"}, {"rubric_number": 15, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型提到了荧光 mRNA 报告基因，但未明确说明使用‘含有内含子’的报告基因来分析核输出效率。"}, {"rubric_number": 16, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型明确指出了神经元因依赖蛋白质合成维持轴突等结构而最易受影响，未出现评分项描述的‘未能指出’的错误。"}, {"rubric_number": 17, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型清晰地将理论分析（第 1-4 节）与实验验证方案（第 5 节）分节呈现，逻辑清晰，未命中扣分条件。"}, {"rubric_number": 18, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型在第 1 节中通过表格和文字明确对比了野生型和患者序列，未命中扣分条件。"}, {"rubric_number": 19, "weight": -3, "met": false, "reason": "模型回复紧扣题目要求的推理，未包含无关的教科书定义、发现历史或脱离背景的复杂机制细节。"}, {"rubric_number": 20, "weight": -3, "met": false, "reason": "实验设计部分使用了层级标题、表格和列表，组织良好，易于阅读，未命中扣分条件。"}], "score": 60, "max_score": 80, "raw_response": "[\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 1,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型明确指出了突变类型为单碱基替换（点突变），并准确说明了 mRNA 水平为 A→U，DNA 水平为 A→T。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 2,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在 DNA Level Change 部分准确陈述了编码链上的变化为 Adenine (A) 到 Thymine (T)。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 3,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在表格中明确指出野生型密码子 AAG 编码 Lysine，并提供了三字母缩写 Lys 和单字母缩写 K。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 4,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型准确识别 UAG 为 STOP codon（终止密码子）或 nonsense codon（无义密码子）。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 5,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型通过序列分析得出结论，AAG→UAG 导致翻译提前终止，生成截短的、结构不完整的 NXF1 蛋白。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 6,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型解释了 NXF1 失活阻断了 mRNA 从核到质的运输，导致细胞质中翻译模板减少，从而抑制蛋白质合成，符合中心法则关键步骤中断的逻辑。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 7,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型不仅指出了 mRNA 核滞留的表型，还明确将其机制链接到了 NXF1 核输出功能的受损，未出现评分项描述的‘未能指定机制’的错误。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 8,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型陈述了 NXF1 功能失活阻断了 mRNA 运输核心通路，导致细胞质中成熟 mRNA 全面减少，进而引起细胞内蛋白质合成的广泛抑制。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 9,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"实验设计 Level 1 中明确包含了使用 Sanger 测序在基因组 DNA 水平验证突变的步骤。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 10,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"实验设计包含了 Western Blot 分析，并说明其目的是检测截短蛋白或全长蛋白减少（即表达丰度和分子量）。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 11,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型虽然提到了 Western Blot 检测截短蛋白，但未明确陈述观察到的条带作为直接分子证据的逻辑语句，也未按要求将该证据陈述加粗。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 12,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型包含了 RNA-FISH 和 Western Blot，但未明确文字说明它们相互佐证以形成完整的证据链。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 13,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型预测的 RNA-FISH 结果是患者细胞核内 poly(A)+ 信号增加（异常滞留），而非评分项描述的‘正常、高效的 mRNA 输出’，未命中扣分条件。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 14,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"实验设计仅包含一种功能回复验证步骤（表达野生型 NXF1），未包含至少两种（如 CRISPR 或通读药物）。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 15,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型提到了荧光 mRNA 报告基因，但未明确说明使用‘含有内含子’的报告基因来分析核输出效率。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 16,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型明确指出了神经元因依赖蛋白质合成维持轴突等结构而最易受影响，未出现评分项描述的‘未能指出’的错误。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 17,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型清晰地将理论分析（第 1-4 节）与实验验证方案（第 5 节）分节呈现，逻辑清晰，未命中扣分条件。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 18,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在第 1 节中通过表格和文字明确对比了野生型和患者序列，未命中扣分条件。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 19,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型回复紧扣题目要求的推理，未包含无关的教科书定义、发现历史或脱离背景的复杂机制细节。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 20,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"实验设计部分使用了层级标题、表格和列表，组织良好，易于阅读，未命中扣分条件。\"\n  }\n]"}, "error": null, "stage": null, "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "user_prompt": "Researchers have identified a novel rare disease: \"Early-onset Neurodegenerative Syndrome X (ENSX).\" Preliminary genetic analysis suggests an association with a gene named NXF1. It is known that the NXF1 gene encodes a critical nuclear mRNA export factor responsible for exporting mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. The genomic structure of the gene, as well as partial regions of its wild-type and mutant cDNA sequences, are known. Known Information: Genomic Structure: The NXF1 gene contains 12 exons, with Exon 7 containing a critical Lysine (Lys) codon (AAG). Sequence Information: Wild-type cDNA fragment (corresponding to part of Exon 7): 5‘- ...AAC GGA AAG CUC UAC... -3’ (Note: Sequence is mRNA, bases shown). Patient cDNA corresponding fragment: 5’- ...AAC GGA UAG CUC UAC... -3‘. Clinical Samples available: Skin fibroblasts and blood samples from patients and healthy controls. Please conduct a mutational analysis and determine molecular consequences (basic analysis) based on the provided information. Compare the wild-type and patient sequences, identifying the specific type of mutation (base substitution, deletion, etc.) and the change at the DNA level. What amino acid does the \"AAG\" in the wild-type sequence encode? What is the \"UAG\" in the patient sequence? What direct impact will this mutation have on the amino acid sequence of the NXF1 protein? Based on the function of the NXF1 protein, hypothesize in which cell types this mutation is most likely to have severe effects. How will this affect the global process of gene expression? Furthermore, how should experiments be designed to verify this hypothesis?", "response": {"request_id": "omb-batch", "status": "completed", "session_id": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "workspace": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8583__global/omb-batch", "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "final_prompt": "Researchers have identified a novel rare disease: \"Early-onset Neurodegenerative Syndrome X (ENSX).\" Preliminary genetic analysis suggests an association with a gene named NXF1. It is known that the NXF1 gene encodes a critical nuclear mRNA export factor responsible for exporting mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. The genomic structure of the gene, as well as partial regions of its wild-type and mutant cDNA sequences, are known. Known Information: Genomic Structure: The NXF1 gene contains 12 exons, with Exon 7 containing a critical Lysine (Lys) codon (AAG). Sequence Information: Wild-type cDNA fragment (corresponding to part of Exon 7): 5‘- ...AAC GGA AAG CUC UAC... -3’ (Note: Sequence is mRNA, bases shown). Patient cDNA corresponding fragment: 5’- ...AAC GGA UAG CUC UAC... -3‘. Clinical Samples available: Skin fibroblasts and blood samples from patients and healthy controls. Please conduct a mutational analysis and determine molecular consequences (basic analysis) based on the provided information. Compare the wild-type and patient sequences, identifying the specific type of mutation (base substitution, deletion, etc.) and the change at the DNA level. What amino acid does the \"AAG\" in the wild-type sequence encode? What is the \"UAG\" in the patient sequence? What direct impact will this mutation have on the amino acid sequence of the NXF1 protein? Based on the function of the NXF1 protein, hypothesize in which cell types this mutation is most likely to have severe effects. How will this affect the global process of gene expression? Furthermore, how should experiments be designed to verify this hypothesis?", "session_data": {"scope_id": "811f69314c036a1cc3469f74e1f1252eeaf1e9ba", "session_id": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "info": {"id": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "version": "local", "scopeID": "811f69314c036a1cc3469f74e1f1252eeaf1e9ba", "directory": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8583__global/omb-batch", "title": "onemillion:natural_science/8583/global", "time": {"created": 1773730476479, "updated": 1773730476592}, "summary": {"additions": 0, "deletions": 0, "files": 0}}, "messages": [{"id": "msg_cfa9331d4001uDYtxz0gQt6Tm6", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa9331d4001uDYtxz0gQt6Tm6", "sessionID": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "role": "user", "time": {"created": 1773730476500}, "summary": {"title": "Analysis of NXF1 mutation in ENSX gene", "diffs": []}, "agent": "synergy", "model": {"providerID": "sii-holos", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B"}}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa9331d6001XH5BQFFxSmznA7", "sessionID": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "messageID": "msg_cfa9331d4001uDYtxz0gQt6Tm6", "type": "text", "text": "\"Researchers have identified a novel rare disease: \\\"Early-onset Neurodegenerative Syndrome X (ENSX).\\\" Preliminary genetic analysis suggests an association with a gene named NXF1. It is known that the NXF1 gene encodes a critical nuclear mRNA export factor responsible for exporting mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. The genomic structure of the gene, as well as partial regions of its wild-type and mutant cDNA sequences, are known. Known Information: Genomic Structure: The NXF1 gene contains 12 exons, with Exon 7 containing a critical Lysine (Lys) codon (AAG). Sequence Information: Wild-type cDNA fragment (corresponding to part of Exon 7): 5‘- ...AAC GGA AAG CUC UAC... -3’ (Note: Sequence is mRNA, bases shown). Patient cDNA corresponding fragment: 5’- ...AAC GGA UAG CUC UAC... -3‘. Clinical Samples available: Skin fibroblasts and blood samples from patients and healthy controls. Please conduct a mutational analysis and determine molecular consequences (basic analysis) based on the provided information. Compare the wild-type and patient sequences, identifying the specific type of mutation (base substitution, deletion, etc.) and the change at the DNA level. What amino acid does the \\\"AAG\\\" in the wild-type sequence encode? What is the \\\"UAG\\\" in the patient sequence? What direct impact will this mutation have on the amino acid sequence of the NXF1 protein? Based on the function of the NXF1 protein, hypothesize in which cell types this mutation is most likely to have severe effects. How will this affect the global process of gene expression? Furthermore, how should experiments be designed to verify this hypothesis?\"\n"}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa933206001iSKfOsKMA2a1J1", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa933206001iSKfOsKMA2a1J1", "sessionID": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730476550, "completed": 1773730638957}, "parentID": "msg_cfa9331d4001uDYtxz0gQt6Tm6", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8583__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8583__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26041, "output": 2667, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa93424a001VsYvgY6saiTQmU", "sessionID": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "messageID": "msg_cfa933206001iSKfOsKMA2a1J1", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa93424b001IwdSB5ZukofzY1", "sessionID": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "messageID": "msg_cfa933206001iSKfOsKMA2a1J1", "type": "reasoning", "text": "This is a comprehensive molecular genetics question about a rare disease mutation. Let me analyze the information provided and work through each question systematically.\n\nLet me break down what I need to analyze:\n\n1. Compare wild-type and patient sequences to identify mutation type\n2. Determine what amino acid AAG encodes and what UAG represents\n3. Analyze the direct impact on the protein sequence\n4. Hypothesize which cell types would be most severely affected\n5. Explain effects on global gene expression\n6. Design experiments to verify the hypothesis\n\nLet me work through this step by step.\n\n**Wild-type sequence:** 5'- ...AAC GGA AAG CUC UAC... -3'\n**Patient sequence:** 5'- ...AAC GGA UAG CUC UAC... -3'\n\nThe difference is: AAG → UAG (a single base change from A to U)\n\n**Amino acid coding:**\n- AAG codes for Lysine (Lys/K)\n- UAG is a STOP codon (amber stop codon)\n\nThis is a **nonsense mutation** - a single base substitution that creates a premature stop codon.\n\n**Impact on protein:**\n- Translation will terminate prematurely at this position\n- This creates a truncated protein (if any protein is produced at all)\n- The mutation is in Exon 7 of 12 exons, so roughly half the protein would be lost\n\n**NXF1 function:**\n- Nuclear mRNA export factor\n- Exports mRNA from nucleus to cytoplasm\n- Essential for gene expression\n\n**Cell types most affected:**\n- Neurons (neurodegenerative disease phenotype)\n- Cells with high protein synthesis demands\n- Post-mitotic cells that can't dilute the defect through cell division\n\n**Global gene expression effects:**\n- mRNA accumulates in nucleus\n- Reduced protein synthesis\n- Cellular stress responses activated\n- Eventually cell death\n\n**Experimental verification:**\nI should design experiments at multiple levels:\n\n\n- Molecular level (RT-PCR, Western blot)\n- Cellular level (FISH, immunofluorescence)\n- Functional level (mRNA export assays)\n- Patient samples (fibroblasts, blood cells)\n\nNow I'll structure this analysis comprehensively.", "time": {"start": 1773730480715, "end": 1773730638942}}, {"id": "prt_cfa93a2be001569SkX2tuYZEoX", "sessionID": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "messageID": "msg_cfa933206001iSKfOsKMA2a1J1", "type": "text", "text": "\n\n# Mutational Analysis of ENSX and NXF1 Gene\n\n## 1. Mutation Type Identification\n\n### Sequence Comparison\n\n| Position | Wild-type | Patient | Change |\n|----------|-----------|---------|--------|\n| Codon 3 | `AAG` | `UAG` | A → U (single base substitution) |\n\n**Mutation Type:** **Point mutation (single base substitution)**\n- Specifically: **Transition mutation** (purine A → pyrimidine U... actually this is a transversion)\n- Correction: A (purine) → U (pyrimidine) = **Transversion mutation**\n\n### DNA Level Change\n\nSince the cDNA sequences represent mRNA:\n- **Wild-type DNA (coding strand):** `5'-...AAG...-3'`\n- **Patient DNA (coding strand):** `5'-...TAG...-3'`\n- **Mutation:** **A → T substitution** on the coding strand\n\n---\n\n## 2. Amino Acid Analysis\n\n| Codon | Encodes | Classification |\n|-------|---------|----------------|\n| `AAG` | **Lysine (Lys/K)** | Basic, positively charged amino acid |\n| `UAG` | **STOP codon** | Amber termination codon |\n\n### Direct Impact on Protein\n\nThis is a **nonsense mutation** creating a **premature termination codon (PTC)**:\n\n```\nWild-type:  Met---[Exon1-6]---Lys---[Exon7-12]---Stop\n                    (AAG)\n                    \nPatient:    Met---[Exon1-6]---STOP\n                    (UAG)\n                    ↑\n              Truncated protein (~50% length)\n```\n\n**Consequences:**\n1. **Truncated protein** - Only exons 1-6 translated (~50% of normal protein)\n2. **Loss of critical domains** - NXF1's C-terminal domains essential for function\n3. **Possible NMD** - mRNA may be degraded via Nonsense-Mediated Decay\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cell Type Vulnerability Hypothesis\n\n### Most Severely Affected Cell Types\n\n| Cell Type | Reason for Vulnerability |\n|-----------|-------------------------|\n| **Neurons** (especially CNS) | Post-mitotic, high protein turnover, long axons require efficient mRNA transport |\n| **Motor neurons** | Extended cellular processes demand robust mRNA export |\n| **Cells with high secretory demand** | Require continuous high-level protein synthesis |\n\n### Why Neurons Are Particularly Vulnerable\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Neuronal Vulnerability to NXF1 Deficiency              │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  1. Post-mitotic state → Cannot dilute defect via       │\n│     cell division; damage accumulates                   │\n│                                                         │\n│  2. Polarized structure → mRNA must be exported and     │\n│     transported to dendrites/axons for local translation│\n│                                                         │\n│  3. High metabolic demand → Continuous protein synthesis│\n│     required for synaptic function                      │\n│                                                         │\n│  4. Long-lived cells → Cumulative export defects lead   │\n│     to neurodegeneration                                │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Global Gene Expression Effects\n\n### Molecular Cascade\n\n```\nNXF1 haploinsufficiency/loss\n        ↓\nImpaired nuclear mRNA export\n        ↓\n┌───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐\n│   Nuclear retention   │   Cytoplasmic mRNA    │\n│   of poly(A)+ RNA     │   depletion           │\n│                       │                       │\n│   • mRNA accumulates  │   • Reduced translation│\n│     in nucleus        │   • Protein synthesis  │\n│   • Nuclear foci      │     decline           │\n│     formation         │   • Cellular stress   │\n└───────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘\n        ↓\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Cellular Consequences                        │\n│  • Unfolded Protein Response (UPR) activation │\n│  • Integrated Stress Response (ISR)           │\n│  • Apoptosis pathway activation               │\n│  • Eventual cell death                        │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Specific Effects on Gene Expression\n\n1. **Global mRNA export defect** - All mRNAs dependent on NXF1 affected\n2. **Nuclear accumulation** - Visible as nuclear poly(A)+ RNA foci\n3. **Reduced protein synthesis** - Global translation decline\n4. **Stress response activation** - UPR, ISR pathways triggered\n5. **Cell-type specific sensitivity** - Neurons most vulnerable\n\n---\n\n## 5. Experimental Design to Verify Hypothesis\n\n### Experimental Framework\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    EXPERIMENTAL VERIFICATION PLAN                │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nLevel 1: Molecular Confirmation\nLevel 2: Cellular Phenotype  \nLevel 3: Functional Assays\nLevel 4: Patient Sample Validation\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 1: Molecular Confirmation**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Expected Result (Patient) |\n|------------|--------|--------------------------|\n| **Genotyping** | Sanger sequencing of Exon 7 | Confirm A→T mutation |\n| **mRNA analysis** | RT-PCR + sequencing | Detect mutant transcript; assess NMD |\n| **Protein analysis** | Western blot (N-term vs C-term antibodies) | Truncated protein or reduced full-length |\n| **NMD assessment** | qPCR ± cycloheximide treatment | Increased mutant mRNA with NMD inhibition |\n\n**Key reagents needed:**\n- Antibodies against NXF1 N-terminus (exons 1-6)\n- Antibodies against NXF1 C-terminus (exons 8-12)\n- Patient and control cell lines\n\n---\n\n### **Level 2: Cellular Phenotype**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Expected Result |\n|------------|--------|-----------------|\n| **mRNA localization** | FISH with oligo(dT) probe | Nuclear poly(A)+ RNA accumulation in patient cells |\n| **NXF1 localization** | Immunofluorescence | Altered NXF1 distribution (if protein stable) |\n| **Nuclear morphology** | DAPI + nuclear envelope markers | Check for nuclear stress indicators |\n| **Cell viability** | Apoptosis assays (TUNEL, Annexin V) | Increased cell death in patient neurons |\n\n**FISH Protocol Outline:**\n```\n1. Fix patient and control fibroblasts\n2. Hybridize with fluorescent oligo(dT) probe\n3. Counterstain with DAPI\n4. Image by confocal microscopy\n5. Quantify nuclear vs cytoplasmic signal ratio\n\nExpected: Patient cells show increased nuclear poly(A)+ signal\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 3: Functional Assays**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Purpose |\n|------------|--------|---------|\n| **mRNA export assay** | Microinjection of fluorescent mRNA reporter | Direct measurement of export kinetics |\n| **Polysome profiling** | Sucrose gradient centrifugation | Assess global translation efficiency |\n| **RNA-seq** | Nuclear vs cytoplasmic fractionation | Identify affected transcripts |\n| **Rescue experiment** | Express wild-type NXF1 in patient cells | Confirm causality |\n\n**Rescue Experiment Design:**\n```\nPatient fibroblasts → Transfect with WT NXF1 expression vector\n                    → Assess:\n                      • mRNA export (FISH)\n                      • Cell viability\n                      • Protein synthesis rates\n                    \nControl: Empty vector transfection\n\nExpected: WT NXF1 restores normal phenotype\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 4: Patient Sample Validation**\n\n| Sample Type | Experiments |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Skin fibroblasts** | Primary cells for molecular/cellular assays |\n| **Blood samples** | Lymphoblastoid cell lines; RNA for transcript analysis |\n| **iPSC-derived neurons** | Differentiate patient fibroblasts → neurons for disease-relevant model |\n\n**iPSC Disease Model:**\n```\nPatient fibroblasts → Reprogram to iPSCs → Differentiate to neurons\n                                                    ↓\n                                    Most relevant cell type for ENSX\n                                                    ↓\n                                    Assess: mRNA export, viability, \n                                            neurodegeneration markers\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Summary of Key Predictions to Test**\n\n| Prediction | Assay | Confirmation Criteria |\n|------------|-------|----------------------|\n| PTC present | Sequencing | A→T mutation in Exon 7 |\n| Truncated protein | Western blot | C-terminal epitope loss |\n| Nuclear mRNA retention | FISH | Increased nuclear poly(A)+ signal |\n| Reduced protein synthesis | Polysome profiling | Decreased polysome fraction |\n| Neuronal vulnerability | iPSC-neurons | Greater defect vs fibroblasts |\n| Causality | Rescue experiment | WT NXF1 restores phenotype |\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe ENSX mutation is a **nonsense mutation (A→T transversion)** in Exon 7 of NXF1, converting a Lysine codon (AAG) to a premature STOP codon (UAG). This produces a truncated, non-functional NXF1 protein that cannot properly export mRNA from nucleus to cytoplasm.\n\n**Neurons are most severely affected** due to their post-mitotic nature, high protein synthesis demands, and polarized structure requiring efficient mRNA transport. The global consequence is **nuclear mRNA accumulation with cytoplasmic depletion**, leading to cellular stress and eventual neurodegeneration.\n\nThe experimental framework above provides a systematic approach to validate this molecular diagnosis and establish the pathogenic mechanism of ENSX.", "time": {"start": 1773730638943, "end": 1773730638943}}, {"id": "prt_cfa95ac62001fz7t8Uag4YgtEX", "sessionID": "ses_3056cce40ffeFN1WQ2AxCuCpV0", "messageID": "msg_cfa933206001iSKfOsKMA2a1J1", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26041, "output": 2667, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "# Mutational Analysis of ENSX and NXF1 Gene\n\n## 1. Mutation Type Identification\n\n### Sequence Comparison\n\n| Position | Wild-type | Patient | Change |\n|----------|-----------|---------|--------|\n| Codon 3 | `AAG` | `UAG` | A → U (single base substitution) |\n\n**Mutation Type:** **Point mutation (single base substitution)**\n- Specifically: **Transition mutation** (purine A → pyrimidine U... actually this is a transversion)\n- Correction: A (purine) → U (pyrimidine) = **Transversion mutation**\n\n### DNA Level Change\n\nSince the cDNA sequences represent mRNA:\n- **Wild-type DNA (coding strand):** `5'-...AAG...-3'`\n- **Patient DNA (coding strand):** `5'-...TAG...-3'`\n- **Mutation:** **A → T substitution** on the coding strand\n\n---\n\n## 2. Amino Acid Analysis\n\n| Codon | Encodes | Classification |\n|-------|---------|----------------|\n| `AAG` | **Lysine (Lys/K)** | Basic, positively charged amino acid |\n| `UAG` | **STOP codon** | Amber termination codon |\n\n### Direct Impact on Protein\n\nThis is a **nonsense mutation** creating a **premature termination codon (PTC)**:\n\n```\nWild-type:  Met---[Exon1-6]---Lys---[Exon7-12]---Stop\n                    (AAG)\n                    \nPatient:    Met---[Exon1-6]---STOP\n                    (UAG)\n                    ↑\n              Truncated protein (~50% length)\n```\n\n**Consequences:**\n1. **Truncated protein** - Only exons 1-6 translated (~50% of normal protein)\n2. **Loss of critical domains** - NXF1's C-terminal domains essential for function\n3. **Possible NMD** - mRNA may be degraded via Nonsense-Mediated Decay\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cell Type Vulnerability Hypothesis\n\n### Most Severely Affected Cell Types\n\n| Cell Type | Reason for Vulnerability |\n|-----------|-------------------------|\n| **Neurons** (especially CNS) | Post-mitotic, high protein turnover, long axons require efficient mRNA transport |\n| **Motor neurons** | Extended cellular processes demand robust mRNA export |\n| **Cells with high secretory demand** | Require continuous high-level protein synthesis |\n\n### Why Neurons Are Particularly Vulnerable\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Neuronal Vulnerability to NXF1 Deficiency              │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  1. Post-mitotic state → Cannot dilute defect via       │\n│     cell division; damage accumulates                   │\n│                                                         │\n│  2. Polarized structure → mRNA must be exported and     │\n│     transported to dendrites/axons for local translation│\n│                                                         │\n│  3. High metabolic demand → Continuous protein synthesis│\n│     required for synaptic function                      │\n│                                                         │\n│  4. Long-lived cells → Cumulative export defects lead   │\n│     to neurodegeneration                                │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Global Gene Expression Effects\n\n### Molecular Cascade\n\n```\nNXF1 haploinsufficiency/loss\n        ↓\nImpaired nuclear mRNA export\n        ↓\n┌───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐\n│   Nuclear retention   │   Cytoplasmic mRNA    │\n│   of poly(A)+ RNA     │   depletion           │\n│                       │                       │\n│   • mRNA accumulates  │   • Reduced translation│\n│     in nucleus        │   • Protein synthesis  │\n│   • Nuclear foci      │     decline           │\n│     formation         │   • Cellular stress   │\n└───────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘\n        ↓\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Cellular Consequences                        │\n│  • Unfolded Protein Response (UPR) activation │\n│  • Integrated Stress Response (ISR)           │\n│  • Apoptosis pathway activation               │\n│  • Eventual cell death                        │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Specific Effects on Gene Expression\n\n1. **Global mRNA export defect** - All mRNAs dependent on NXF1 affected\n2. **Nuclear accumulation** - Visible as nuclear poly(A)+ RNA foci\n3. **Reduced protein synthesis** - Global translation decline\n4. **Stress response activation** - UPR, ISR pathways triggered\n5. **Cell-type specific sensitivity** - Neurons most vulnerable\n\n---\n\n## 5. Experimental Design to Verify Hypothesis\n\n### Experimental Framework\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    EXPERIMENTAL VERIFICATION PLAN                │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nLevel 1: Molecular Confirmation\nLevel 2: Cellular Phenotype  \nLevel 3: Functional Assays\nLevel 4: Patient Sample Validation\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 1: Molecular Confirmation**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Expected Result (Patient) |\n|------------|--------|--------------------------|\n| **Genotyping** | Sanger sequencing of Exon 7 | Confirm A→T mutation |\n| **mRNA analysis** | RT-PCR + sequencing | Detect mutant transcript; assess NMD |\n| **Protein analysis** | Western blot (N-term vs C-term antibodies) | Truncated protein or reduced full-length |\n| **NMD assessment** | qPCR ± cycloheximide treatment | Increased mutant mRNA with NMD inhibition |\n\n**Key reagents needed:**\n- Antibodies against NXF1 N-terminus (exons 1-6)\n- Antibodies against NXF1 C-terminus (exons 8-12)\n- Patient and control cell lines\n\n---\n\n### **Level 2: Cellular Phenotype**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Expected Result |\n|------------|--------|-----------------|\n| **mRNA localization** | FISH with oligo(dT) probe | Nuclear poly(A)+ RNA accumulation in patient cells |\n| **NXF1 localization** | Immunofluorescence | Altered NXF1 distribution (if protein stable) |\n| **Nuclear morphology** | DAPI + nuclear envelope markers | Check for nuclear stress indicators |\n| **Cell viability** | Apoptosis assays (TUNEL, Annexin V) | Increased cell death in patient neurons |\n\n**FISH Protocol Outline:**\n```\n1. Fix patient and control fibroblasts\n2. Hybridize with fluorescent oligo(dT) probe\n3. Counterstain with DAPI\n4. Image by confocal microscopy\n5. Quantify nuclear vs cytoplasmic signal ratio\n\nExpected: Patient cells show increased nuclear poly(A)+ signal\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 3: Functional Assays**\n\n| Experiment | Method | Purpose |\n|------------|--------|---------|\n| **mRNA export assay** | Microinjection of fluorescent mRNA reporter | Direct measurement of export kinetics |\n| **Polysome profiling** | Sucrose gradient centrifugation | Assess global translation efficiency |\n| **RNA-seq** | Nuclear vs cytoplasmic fractionation | Identify affected transcripts |\n| **Rescue experiment** | Express wild-type NXF1 in patient cells | Confirm causality |\n\n**Rescue Experiment Design:**\n```\nPatient fibroblasts → Transfect with WT NXF1 expression vector\n                    → Assess:\n                      • mRNA export (FISH)\n                      • Cell viability\n                      • Protein synthesis rates\n                    \nControl: Empty vector transfection\n\nExpected: WT NXF1 restores normal phenotype\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Level 4: Patient Sample Validation**\n\n| Sample Type | Experiments |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Skin fibroblasts** | Primary cells for molecular/cellular assays |\n| **Blood samples** | Lymphoblastoid cell lines; RNA for transcript analysis |\n| **iPSC-derived neurons** | Differentiate patient fibroblasts → neurons for disease-relevant model |\n\n**iPSC Disease Model:**\n```\nPatient fibroblasts → Reprogram to iPSCs → Differentiate to neurons\n                                                    ↓\n                                    Most relevant cell type for ENSX\n                                                    ↓\n                                    Assess: mRNA export, viability, \n                                            neurodegeneration markers\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Summary of Key Predictions to Test**\n\n| Prediction | Assay | Confirmation Criteria |\n|------------|-------|----------------------|\n| PTC present | Sequencing | A→T mutation in Exon 7 |\n| Truncated protein | Western blot | C-terminal epitope loss |\n| Nuclear mRNA retention | FISH | Increased nuclear poly(A)+ signal |\n| Reduced protein synthesis | Polysome profiling | Decreased polysome fraction |\n| Neuronal vulnerability | iPSC-neurons | Greater defect vs fibroblasts |\n| Causality | Rescue experiment | WT NXF1 restores phenotype |\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe ENSX mutation is a **nonsense mutation (A→T transversion)** in Exon 7 of NXF1, converting a Lysine codon (AAG) to a premature STOP codon (UAG). This produces a truncated, non-functional NXF1 protein that cannot properly export mRNA from nucleus to cytoplasm.\n\n**Neurons are most severely affected** due to their post-mitotic nature, high protein synthesis demands, and polarized structure requiring efficient mRNA transport. The global consequence is **nuclear mRNA accumulation with cytoplasmic depletion**, leading to cellular stress and eventual neurodegeneration.\n\nThe experimental framework above provides a systematic approach to validate this molecular diagnosis and establish the pathogenic mechanism of ENSX.", "case_id": 8583, "language": "global", "tags": {"topics": ["Natural Sciences", "Biology", "Molecular Biology"], "time_sensitivity": {"time_sensitivity": "Time-agnostic", "year_month": "NA", "day": "NA"}}, "rubric_count": 20}, "metrics": {"solve_time_seconds": 164.9, "tokens": {"input": 26041, "output": 2667, "total": 28708}, "cost": 0.0, "step_count": 1}, "error": ""}}
{"task_id": "natural_science/8832/cn", "answer": "# 分析：MYC Signature 假阳性风险的核心混杂因素\n\n## 关键洞察\n\n在评估可能导致假阳性发现的因素时，需要识别同时满足三个条件的混杂变量：\n1. 与MYC活性系统性相关\n2. 与PARPi治疗反应系统性相关\n3. 在PD组与DCR组间分布可能不均衡\n\n## 各因素评估\n\n| 混杂因素 | 与MYC关联 | 与PARPi反应关联 | 小样本不均衡风险 |\n|---------|----------|----------------|-----------------|\n| 样本来源（原发灶vs转移灶） | **强**（转移灶MYC普遍更高） | **强**（转移灶预后更差） | **高**（12例中5例转移） |\n| BRCA1 vs BRCA2突变 | 中（BRCA1增殖更高） | 中（敏感性有差异） | 中 |\n| PARPi种类（olaparib vs talazoparib） | 弱 | 中（效价不同） | 中（8:4分布） |\n| 检测时间点（pre vs post） | — | — | 方法学差异，非混杂 |\n\n## 核心结论\n\n**样本解剖来源的异质性（原发灶 vs 转移灶）在PD组与DCR组间的分布不均衡**是最可能导致假阳性发现的具体因素。\n\n### 理由\n\n1. **生物学机制明确**：转移灶经历上皮-间质转化（EMT）、获得干细胞样特征，MYC活性系统性高于原发灶\n\n2. **预后混淆预测**：转移灶本身对任何系统性治疗反应更差，高MYC可能仅反映\"肿瘤侵袭性\"这一预后因素，而非PARPi特异性的预测因素\n\n3. **小样本极易失衡**：12例中若PD组恰好包含4-5例转移灶样本，而DCR组以原发灶为主，观察到的\"MYC→耐药\"关联实际上是\"转移状态→高MYC+不良预后\"的混杂\n\n4. **无法通过现有设计排除**：GeoMx DSP虽解决空间分辨率问题，但无法校正不同解剖来源肿瘤的内在生物学差异\n\n## 验证建议\n\n向机构论证稳健性时，应：\n- 提供PD组与DCR组间样本来源分布的列联表\n- 进行多变量回归校正样本来源后重新评估MYC signature的独立预测价值\n- 在原发灶亚组内单独验证 associations\n\n---\n\n**简答**：最可能导致假阳性的具体因素是**肿瘤样本的解剖学来源（原发灶 vs 转移灶）在响应组与非响应组间的分布不均衡**——这使得MYC高表达可能仅是转移状态的伴随标志，而非PARPi耐药的真实预测因子。", "score": {"rubric_results": [{"rubric_number": 1, "weight": 10, "met": false, "reason": "模型未将 Bulk RNA-seq 无法区分克隆选择等作为导致假阳性的核心因素，而是归因为样本解剖来源异质性。"}, {"rubric_number": 2, "weight": 6, "met": false, "reason": "模型未明确指出 Bulk 测序信号平均化无法区分克隆/基质贡献是导致假阳性的原因。"}, {"rubric_number": 3, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型未推导 PDX 治疗后 MYC 升高是药物筛选耐药克隆的结果（后果）而非预测因子（原因）。"}, {"rubric_number": 4, "weight": 7, "met": false, "reason": "模型在表格中将检测时间点差异标记为'非混杂'，未指出临床与 PDX 队列的时序错配问题。"}, {"rubric_number": 5, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型指出的具体因素是'解剖学来源'，而非 Rubric 要求的'细胞增殖速率或高肿瘤负荷'。"}, {"rubric_number": 6, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型未提及空间转录组 ROI 选择面积可能引入的采样偏差。"}, {"rubric_number": 7, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型未分析治疗诱导的应激反应导致 MYC 虚假上调的可能性。"}, {"rubric_number": 8, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型未引用流行病学因果推断中的时序性原则来论证 PDX 数据的局限性。"}, {"rubric_number": 9, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型论证异质性导致假阳性，与 Rubric 要求的'异质性更易导致假阴性'的逻辑相反。"}, {"rubric_number": 10, "weight": 2, "met": true, "reason": "模型回复中的学术符号（如 MYC, PARPi, PDX 等）使用上下保持一致。"}, {"rubric_number": 11, "weight": -5, "met": true, "reason": "模型错误地将导致假阳性的首要因素归因为'样本异质性（原发灶 vs 转移灶）'，符合扣分描述。"}, {"rubric_number": 12, "weight": -5, "met": true, "reason": "模型未论证 Bulk RNA-seq 在处理复杂克隆结构时的信号平均化弊端，符合扣分描述。"}, {"rubric_number": 13, "weight": -10, "met": false, "reason": "模型未出现与公认科学事实相悖的明显生物学错误断言。"}, {"rubric_number": 14, "weight": -2, "met": false, "reason": "模型回复精简，未堆砌超过 50% 的非核心背景科普内容。"}], "score": -8, "max_score": 55, "raw_response": "[\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 1,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未将 Bulk RNA-seq 无法区分克隆选择等作为导致假阳性的核心因素，而是归因为样本解剖来源异质性。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 2,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未明确指出 Bulk 测序信号平均化无法区分克隆/基质贡献是导致假阳性的原因。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 3,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未推导 PDX 治疗后 MYC 升高是药物筛选耐药克隆的结果（后果）而非预测因子（原因）。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 4,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在表格中将检测时间点差异标记为'非混杂'，未指出临床与 PDX 队列的时序错配问题。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 5,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型指出的具体因素是'解剖学来源'，而非 Rubric 要求的'细胞增殖速率或高肿瘤负荷'。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 6,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未提及空间转录组 ROI 选择面积可能引入的采样偏差。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 7,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未分析治疗诱导的应激反应导致 MYC 虚假上调的可能性。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 8,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未引用流行病学因果推断中的时序性原则来论证 PDX 数据的局限性。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 9,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型论证异质性导致假阳性，与 Rubric 要求的'异质性更易导致假阴性'的逻辑相反。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 10,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型回复中的学术符号（如 MYC, PARPi, PDX 等）使用上下保持一致。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 11,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型错误地将导致假阳性的首要因素归因为'样本异质性（原发灶 vs 转移灶）'，符合扣分描述。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 12,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未论证 Bulk RNA-seq 在处理复杂克隆结构时的信号平均化弊端，符合扣分描述。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 13,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未出现与公认科学事实相悖的明显生物学错误断言。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 14,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型回复精简，未堆砌超过 50% 的非核心背景科普内容。\"\n  }\n]"}, "error": null, "stage": null, "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "user_prompt": "一家肿瘤学公司正在开发基于MYC转录活性的PARPi耐药预测算法。他们的初步验证基于35个HR缺陷型PDX模型的bulk RNA-seq数据，发现接受olaparib治疗3周后出现疾病进展（PD）的模型，其post-treatment样本的MYC转录活性显著高于达到疾病控制（DCR）的模型。\n基于此，该公司设计了一个临床验证队列：收集12例HR改变型转移性乳腺癌患者的PARPi治疗前活检标本，使用GeoMx数字空间分析平台进行RNA测序。在对PANCK阳性（肿瘤上皮细胞）区域的分析中，他们确实观察到后续治疗出现PD的患者（n=6）相比获得客观缓解（PR+CR，n=6）的患者，pre-treatment时MYC targets V1基因集和DNA repair通路基因集均显著上调（FDR<0.05）。\n但有一位成员却提出异议，他认为虽然两个数据集都支持\"高MYC活性→PARPi耐药\"的关联，但PDX的bulk RNA-seq采集的是治疗后样本（用于分类耐药性），而临床DSP分析的是治疗前样本（用于预测反应），且bulk方法提取的是整个肿瘤组织的总RNA（包括人源肿瘤细胞和小鼠基质，虽然可以分离比对），无法区分肿瘤内部克隆异质性。\n而在后续的质控审查会上，一位专家进一步提出质疑，他认为12例患者的样本来源高度异质（7例原发灶、2例淋巴结转移、2例骨转移、1例脑转移），BRCA突变类型混合（BRCA1和BRCA2均有），使用的PARPi种类也不同（8例olaparib，4例talazoparib）。\n如果要向相关机构论证MYC signature作为预测性生物标志物的稳健性，你作为该公司的负责人，你认为在基于上述条件的前提下，你需要指出哪一项具体的因素最可能导致假阳性发现（即高MYC仅为伴随现象而非真实预测因子），从而解决这个难题。那么该具体因素究竟是什么？", "response": {"request_id": "omb-batch", "status": "completed", "session_id": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "workspace": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8832__cn/omb-batch", "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "final_prompt": "一家肿瘤学公司正在开发基于MYC转录活性的PARPi耐药预测算法。他们的初步验证基于35个HR缺陷型PDX模型的bulk RNA-seq数据，发现接受olaparib治疗3周后出现疾病进展（PD）的模型，其post-treatment样本的MYC转录活性显著高于达到疾病控制（DCR）的模型。\n基于此，该公司设计了一个临床验证队列：收集12例HR改变型转移性乳腺癌患者的PARPi治疗前活检标本，使用GeoMx数字空间分析平台进行RNA测序。在对PANCK阳性（肿瘤上皮细胞）区域的分析中，他们确实观察到后续治疗出现PD的患者（n=6）相比获得客观缓解（PR+CR，n=6）的患者，pre-treatment时MYC targets V1基因集和DNA repair通路基因集均显著上调（FDR<0.05）。\n但有一位成员却提出异议，他认为虽然两个数据集都支持\"高MYC活性→PARPi耐药\"的关联，但PDX的bulk RNA-seq采集的是治疗后样本（用于分类耐药性），而临床DSP分析的是治疗前样本（用于预测反应），且bulk方法提取的是整个肿瘤组织的总RNA（包括人源肿瘤细胞和小鼠基质，虽然可以分离比对），无法区分肿瘤内部克隆异质性。\n而在后续的质控审查会上，一位专家进一步提出质疑，他认为12例患者的样本来源高度异质（7例原发灶、2例淋巴结转移、2例骨转移、1例脑转移），BRCA突变类型混合（BRCA1和BRCA2均有），使用的PARPi种类也不同（8例olaparib，4例talazoparib）。\n如果要向相关机构论证MYC signature作为预测性生物标志物的稳健性，你作为该公司的负责人，你认为在基于上述条件的前提下，你需要指出哪一项具体的因素最可能导致假阳性发现（即高MYC仅为伴随现象而非真实预测因子），从而解决这个难题。那么该具体因素究竟是什么？", "session_data": {"scope_id": "31b78ad3a7cd67c2ad44f14f2f5cc58adf3b1852", "session_id": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "info": {"id": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "version": "local", "scopeID": "31b78ad3a7cd67c2ad44f14f2f5cc58adf3b1852", "directory": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8832__cn/omb-batch", "title": "onemillion:natural_science/8832/cn", "time": {"created": 1773730436158, "updated": 1773730436272}, "summary": {"additions": 0, "deletions": 0, "files": 0}}, "messages": [{"id": "msg_cfa929453001R7luKjH9dPTT6i", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa929453001R7luKjH9dPTT6i", "sessionID": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "role": "user", "time": {"created": 1773730436179}, "summary": {"title": "Factors influencing false positives in MYC as a predictive biomarker", "diffs": []}, "agent": "synergy", "model": {"providerID": "sii-holos", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B"}}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa929455001oHx5vgmA7pcH7X", "sessionID": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "messageID": "msg_cfa929453001R7luKjH9dPTT6i", "type": "text", "text": "\"一家肿瘤学公司正在开发基于MYC转录活性的PARPi耐药预测算法。他们的初步验证基于35个HR缺陷型PDX模型的bulk RNA-seq数据，发现接受olaparib治疗3周后出现疾病进展（PD）的模型，其post-treatment样本的MYC转录活性显著高于达到疾病控制（DCR）的模型。\n基于此，该公司设计了一个临床验证队列：收集12例HR改变型转移性乳腺癌患者的PARPi治疗前活检标本，使用GeoMx数字空间分析平台进行RNA测序。在对PANCK阳性（肿瘤上皮细胞）区域的分析中，他们确实观察到后续治疗出现PD的患者（n=6）相比获得客观缓解（PR+CR，n=6）的患者，pre-treatment时MYC targets V1基因集和DNA repair通路基因集均显著上调（FDR<0.05）。\n但有一位成员却提出异议，他认为虽然两个数据集都支持\\\"高MYC活性→PARPi耐药\\\"的关联，但PDX的bulk RNA-seq采集的是治疗后样本（用于分类耐药性），而临床DSP分析的是治疗前样本（用于预测反应），且bulk方法提取的是整个肿瘤组织的总RNA（包括人源肿瘤细胞和小鼠基质，虽然可以分离比对），无法区分肿瘤内部克隆异质性。\n而在后续的质控审查会上，一位专家进一步提出质疑，他认为12例患者的样本来源高度异质（7例原发灶、2例淋巴结转移、2例骨转移、1例脑转移），BRCA突变类型混合（BRCA1和BRCA2均有），使用的PARPi种类也不同（8例olaparib，4例talazoparib）。\n如果要向相关机构论证MYC signature作为预测性生物标志物的稳健性，你作为该公司的负责人，你认为在基于上述条件的前提下，你需要指出哪一项具体的因素最可能导致假阳性发现（即高MYC仅为伴随现象而非真实预测因子），从而解决这个难题。那么该具体因素究竟是什么？\"\n"}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa929487001Ux8y4A1WJ8XJWT", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa929487001Ux8y4A1WJ8XJWT", "sessionID": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730436231, "completed": 1773730563033}, "parentID": "msg_cfa929453001R7luKjH9dPTT6i", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8832__cn/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__8832__cn/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26095, "output": 2325, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa929a4a001OUGCg39nO1eq5H", "sessionID": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "messageID": "msg_cfa929487001Ux8y4A1WJ8XJWT", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa929a4b001Tiy5NymstkyQVU", "sessionID": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "messageID": "msg_cfa929487001Ux8y4A1WJ8XJWT", "type": "reasoning", "text": "这是一个关于生物标志物验证和临床试验设计的复杂问题。让我仔细分析一下这个场景中的关键信息：\n\n**研究背景：**\n1. 初步验证：35个HR缺陷型PDX模型，bulk RNA-seq，发现PD模型post-treatment的MYC转录活性高于DCR模型\n2. 临床验证：12例HR改变型转移性乳腺癌患者，GeoMx DSP平台，pre-treatment活检，PD患者MYC targets V1和DNA repair通路显著上调\n\n**质疑点：**\n1. 成员异议：\n   - PDX是治疗后样本（分类耐药性），临床是治疗前样本（预测反应）- 时间点不同\n   - bulk RNA-seq无法区分肿瘤内部克隆异质性\n\n2. 专家质疑：\n   - 样本来源异质（原发灶vs不同部位转移）\n   - BRCA突变类型混合（BRCA1 vs BRCA2）\n   - PARPi种类不同（olaparib vs talazoparib）\n\n**核心问题：**\n哪一项具体因素最可能导致假阳性发现（高MYC仅为伴随现象而非真实预测因子）？\n\n让我思考各种可能的混杂因素：\n\n1. **样本采集时间点差异**：PDX是post-treatment，临床是pre-treatment。\n\n这确实是个方法学差异，但两个数据集都显示MYC与耐药相关，方向一致，所以这不太可能是假阳性的主要原因。\n\n2. **bulk vs DSP的空间分辨率**：bulk无法区分克隆异质性，但DSP的PANCK阳性区域分析已经分离了肿瘤上皮细胞，这个问题在临床数据中已得到解决。\n\n3. **样本来源异质性**：原发灶与不同转移部位的生物学差异可能驱动MYC表达，而非PARPi敏感性本身。\n\n原发灶和转移灶在MYC活性上存在系统性差异，转移灶通常MYC更高。如果PD患者恰好有更多转移灶样本，这会造成混杂。但7例原发灶、5例转移灶的分布中，需要看PD和DCR两组间的样本来源是否均衡。\n\n4. **BRCA1 vs BRCA2突变**：这两种突变类型的生物学特征不同，可能影响MYC表达和药物敏感性。\n\nBRCA1突变通常与更aggressive的表型和更高的增殖活性相关，MYC活性也可能更高。如果PD组中BRCA1突变比例更高，这会形成混杂因素。\n\n5. **PARPi种类差异**：olaparib和talazoparib的作用机制存在差异，可能导致不同的选择压力和MYC活性变化。\n\n但临床上8例用olaparib、4例用talazoparib，需要看两种药物在PD和DCR组中的分布是否均衡。\n\n**关键洞察：**\n\n最可能导致假阳性的因素应该是：\n- 与MYC活性系统性相关\n- 与PARPi反应系统性相关\n- 在PD和DCR两组间分布不均衡\n\n从生物学角度，**样本来源（原发灶vs转移灶）** 是最可疑的混杂因素。\n\n转移灶通常具有更高的MYC活性、更强的侵袭性，且对PARPi的反应可能更差。如果PD组的6例患者中转移灶样本比例显著高于DCR组，那么观察到的\"高MYC→耐药\"关联可能实际上反映的是\"转移灶→高MYC+耐药\"的混杂关系。\n\n但我也需要考虑**BRCA1 vs BRCA2**的影响。BRCA1突变肿瘤通常具有更高的增殖活性和MYC表达，且可能对PARPi的反应模式与BRCA2不同。\n\n如果PD组中BRCA1突变比例更高，这同样会造成混杂。\n\n从样本量来看，12例患者中这些因素的分布不均衡很容易发生。但哪个因素最关键？\n\n**转移部位**作为混杂因素的可能性最大，因为：\n1. 转移灶与原发灶在生物学上有系统性差异（MYC活性、增殖、侵袭性）\n2. 不同转移部位（淋巴结、骨、脑）的生物学特征也不同\n3. 转移状态本身就与预后和治疗反应相关\n4. 在12例的小样本中，如果PD组恰好有更多转移灶样本，很容易产生假阳性\n\n但我也应该考虑**BRCA突变类型**，因为BRCA1和BRCA2在DNA修复机制中的作用不同，可能直接影响PARPi的敏感性。\n\n从生物学机制来看，MYC高表达可能通过增强DNA修复能力来介导PARPi耐药，但这需要区分是MYC本身的作用，还是BRCA突变类型或转移状态带来的混杂效应。\n\n最关键的混杂因素应该是**肿瘤样本的解剖来源**（原发灶 versus 转移灶），因为转移灶本身具有更高的MYC活性和更差的预后，这可能在PD组和DCR组之间分布不均。\n\n不过，题目强调\"高MYC仅为伴随现象而非真实预测因子\"，这指向一个更根本的问题：MYC高表达可能是肿瘤侵袭性的标志，而非PARPi耐药的特异性预测因子。转移灶的生物学特征（包括高MYC、高增殖、多药耐药）本身就与不良预后相关，无论是否使用PARPi。\n\n因此，**样本来源的异质性**（原发灶 vs 不同部位转移灶）是最可能导致假阳性的因素。\n\n如果PD组中转移灶样本比例显著高于DCR组，那么观察到的MYC-耐药关联可能实际上反映的是转移状态-预后关联。\n\n要验证这一点，需要在分析中校正样本来源，或仅在原发灶或仅在转移灶的亚组中进行分析。\n\n不过，我还需要考虑其他可能的混杂因素。BRCA1与BRCA2突变类型的分布如果在两组间不均衡，也可能解释观察到的差异，因为这两种突变类型在生物学行为和对PARP抑制剂的敏感性上存在差异。\n\n同样，如果olaparib和talazoparib在PD组和DCR组中的使用比例不同，药物本身的疗效差异也可能影响结果。\n\n但综合来看，**样本来源的异质性**（原发灶 versus 转移灶）作为混杂因素的可能性最大，因为转移状态与MYC活性、肿瘤侵袭性和治疗反应都有明确的生物学关联。\n\n不过，我需要重新审视这个问题的核心。题目问的是\"哪一项具体的因素最可能导致假阳性发现\"，这意味着在给出的条件中，有一个因素是最关键的。\n\n从统计学的角度，混杂偏倚的产生需要满足三个条件：与暴露因素（MYC活性）相关、与结局（PARPi反应）相关、在比较组间（PD vs DCR）分布不均衡。\n\n在12例患者的小样本中，任何基线特征的不均衡分布都可能导致假阳性。但从生物学机制和临床意义来看，**样本来源（原发灶 vs 转移灶）** 是最可能的混杂因素，因为转移灶的生物学特征（包括MYC活性、增殖能力、侵袭性）与原发灶有系统性差异，且转移状态本身就是预后因素。\n\n不过，我还需要考虑一个更深层的问题：MYC signature本身是否真的是预测性生物标志物（predictive biomarker，预测对特定治疗的反应），还是仅仅是预后性生物标志物（prognostic biomarker，预测总体预后）？\n\n如果是预后性标志物，那么高MYC可能只是表示肿瘤更aggressive、预后更差，而不是特异性预测PARPi耐药。在这种情况下，样本来源（转移灶通常更aggressive）就是一个关键的混杂因素。\n\n所以我的答案是：**肿瘤样本的解剖学来源（原发灶 versus 转移灶）的异质性**是最可能导致假阳性发现的因素。\n\n但让我再想想是否有更精确的表述。题目中提到\"7例原发灶、2例淋巴结转移、2例骨转移、1例脑转移\"，这暗示了样本来源的高度异质性。如果PD组的6例患者中，转移灶样本的比例显著高于DCR组，那么观察到的MYC-耐药关联可能实际上是转移状态-预后关联的反映。\n\n因此，我的最终答案是：**样本来源（原发灶 vs 转移灶）在PD组和DCR组间的分布不均衡**是最可能导致假阳性发现的具体因素。", "time": {"start": 1773730437707, "end": 1773730563022}}, {"id": "prt_cfa942a3c001w9v8OrsQ6TSo1b", "sessionID": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "messageID": "msg_cfa929487001Ux8y4A1WJ8XJWT", "type": "text", "text": "\n\n# 分析：MYC Signature 假阳性风险的核心混杂因素\n\n## 关键洞察\n\n在评估可能导致假阳性发现的因素时，需要识别同时满足三个条件的混杂变量：\n1. 与MYC活性系统性相关\n2. 与PARPi治疗反应系统性相关\n3. 在PD组与DCR组间分布可能不均衡\n\n## 各因素评估\n\n| 混杂因素 | 与MYC关联 | 与PARPi反应关联 | 小样本不均衡风险 |\n|---------|----------|----------------|-----------------|\n| 样本来源（原发灶vs转移灶） | **强**（转移灶MYC普遍更高） | **强**（转移灶预后更差） | **高**（12例中5例转移） |\n| BRCA1 vs BRCA2突变 | 中（BRCA1增殖更高） | 中（敏感性有差异） | 中 |\n| PARPi种类（olaparib vs talazoparib） | 弱 | 中（效价不同） | 中（8:4分布） |\n| 检测时间点（pre vs post） | — | — | 方法学差异，非混杂 |\n\n## 核心结论\n\n**样本解剖来源的异质性（原发灶 vs 转移灶）在PD组与DCR组间的分布不均衡**是最可能导致假阳性发现的具体因素。\n\n### 理由\n\n1. **生物学机制明确**：转移灶经历上皮-间质转化（EMT）、获得干细胞样特征，MYC活性系统性高于原发灶\n\n2. **预后混淆预测**：转移灶本身对任何系统性治疗反应更差，高MYC可能仅反映\"肿瘤侵袭性\"这一预后因素，而非PARPi特异性的预测因素\n\n3. **小样本极易失衡**：12例中若PD组恰好包含4-5例转移灶样本，而DCR组以原发灶为主，观察到的\"MYC→耐药\"关联实际上是\"转移状态→高MYC+不良预后\"的混杂\n\n4. **无法通过现有设计排除**：GeoMx DSP虽解决空间分辨率问题，但无法校正不同解剖来源肿瘤的内在生物学差异\n\n## 验证建议\n\n向机构论证稳健性时，应：\n- 提供PD组与DCR组间样本来源分布的列联表\n- 进行多变量回归校正样本来源后重新评估MYC signature的独立预测价值\n- 在原发灶亚组内单独验证 associations\n\n---\n\n**简答**：最可能导致假阳性的具体因素是**肿瘤样本的解剖学来源（原发灶 vs 转移灶）在响应组与非响应组间的分布不均衡**——这使得MYC高表达可能仅是转移状态的伴随标志，而非PARPi耐药的真实预测因子。", "time": {"start": 1773730563022, "end": 1773730563022}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9483d1001ZiuzEEIZIvJ567", "sessionID": "ses_3056d6bc1ffeOIptGZU3l7EasH", "messageID": "msg_cfa929487001Ux8y4A1WJ8XJWT", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26095, "output": 2325, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "# 分析：MYC Signature 假阳性风险的核心混杂因素\n\n## 关键洞察\n\n在评估可能导致假阳性发现的因素时，需要识别同时满足三个条件的混杂变量：\n1. 与MYC活性系统性相关\n2. 与PARPi治疗反应系统性相关\n3. 在PD组与DCR组间分布可能不均衡\n\n## 各因素评估\n\n| 混杂因素 | 与MYC关联 | 与PARPi反应关联 | 小样本不均衡风险 |\n|---------|----------|----------------|-----------------|\n| 样本来源（原发灶vs转移灶） | **强**（转移灶MYC普遍更高） | **强**（转移灶预后更差） | **高**（12例中5例转移） |\n| BRCA1 vs BRCA2突变 | 中（BRCA1增殖更高） | 中（敏感性有差异） | 中 |\n| PARPi种类（olaparib vs talazoparib） | 弱 | 中（效价不同） | 中（8:4分布） |\n| 检测时间点（pre vs post） | — | — | 方法学差异，非混杂 |\n\n## 核心结论\n\n**样本解剖来源的异质性（原发灶 vs 转移灶）在PD组与DCR组间的分布不均衡**是最可能导致假阳性发现的具体因素。\n\n### 理由\n\n1. **生物学机制明确**：转移灶经历上皮-间质转化（EMT）、获得干细胞样特征，MYC活性系统性高于原发灶\n\n2. **预后混淆预测**：转移灶本身对任何系统性治疗反应更差，高MYC可能仅反映\"肿瘤侵袭性\"这一预后因素，而非PARPi特异性的预测因素\n\n3. **小样本极易失衡**：12例中若PD组恰好包含4-5例转移灶样本，而DCR组以原发灶为主，观察到的\"MYC→耐药\"关联实际上是\"转移状态→高MYC+不良预后\"的混杂\n\n4. **无法通过现有设计排除**：GeoMx DSP虽解决空间分辨率问题，但无法校正不同解剖来源肿瘤的内在生物学差异\n\n## 验证建议\n\n向机构论证稳健性时，应：\n- 提供PD组与DCR组间样本来源分布的列联表\n- 进行多变量回归校正样本来源后重新评估MYC signature的独立预测价值\n- 在原发灶亚组内单独验证 associations\n\n---\n\n**简答**：最可能导致假阳性的具体因素是**肿瘤样本的解剖学来源（原发灶 vs 转移灶）在响应组与非响应组间的分布不均衡**——这使得MYC高表达可能仅是转移状态的伴随标志，而非PARPi耐药的真实预测因子。", "case_id": 8832, "language": "cn", "tags": {"topics": ["自然科学", "生物", "生物-其他"], "time_sensitivity": {"time_sensitivity": "Time-agnostic", "year_month": "NA", "day": "NA"}}, "rubric_count": 14}, "metrics": {"solve_time_seconds": 129.3, "tokens": {"input": 26095, "output": 2325, "total": 28420}, "cost": 0.0, "step_count": 1}, "error": ""}}
{"task_id": "healthcare_and_medicine/8660/global", "answer": "# Comprehensive Clinical Case Analysis\n\n## 1. Diagnosis and Diagnostic Basis\n\n### Primary Diagnosis\n**Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC), BCLC Stage C, CNLC Stage IIIa**\n\n### Diagnostic Basis\n\n| Category | Findings |\n|----------|----------|\n| **Risk Factors** | Chronic Hepatitis B (20 years, untreated) + long-term alcohol use (synergistic carcinogenic effect) |\n| **Clinical Presentation** | RUQ pain, distension, weight loss, jaundice (classic HCC triad in advanced disease) |\n| **Physical Exam** | Jaundice, hepatomegaly (3cm below costal margin), hard/irregular liver texture |\n| **Tumor Markers** | AFP markedly elevated (1250 ng/mL) – highly specific for HCC; CEA & CA19-9 also elevated |\n| **Imaging (CT)** | 6.5×5.8cm right lobe mass with **typical \"wash-in/wash-out\" pattern** (arterial enhancement + portal/delayed washout) – pathognomonic for HCC; 2cm left lobe lesion; porta hepatis lymphadenopathy |\n| **Pathology** | Needle biopsy confirmed **moderately-poorly differentiated HCC** |\n| **Liver Function** | Child-Pugh B (8 points) – compensated cirrhosis with moderate dysfunction |\n\n### Staging Assessment\n- **BCLC Stage C**: Multifocal disease + vascular invasion/extrahepatic spread suspicion + preserved performance status (ECOG 1)\n- **CNLC Stage IIIa**: Intrahepatic multifocal lesions with portal lymph node involvement\n\n---\n\n## 2. Differential Diagnosis\n\n| Disease | Why Consider | Why Unlikely |\n|---------|--------------|--------------|\n| **Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma (ICC)** | Elevated CA19-9, bile duct dilation, lymphadenopathy | Typical HCC enhancement pattern, markedly elevated AFP (rare in ICC), pathology confirms HCC |\n| **Combined HCC-ICC** | Both AFP and CA19-9 elevated, lymph node metastasis | Pathology showed pure HCC; may need further sampling if treatment response poor |\n| **Metastatic Liver Cancer** | Multiple lesions, elevated CEA | Enhancement pattern typical of primary HCC, HBV history, very high AFP, no other primary identified |\n| **Hepatic Adenoma** | Can show arterial enhancement | No washout pattern, AFP not elevated, patient demographics (older male with cirrhosis) |\n| **Liver Abscess** | Fever, pain, mass lesion | No fever, chronic course, enhancement pattern inconsistent, elevated AFP |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Further Investigations Needed\n\n### For Complete Staging\n| Investigation | Purpose |\n|---------------|---------|\n| **Contrast-enhanced MRI liver** | Better characterization of the 2cm left lobe lesion, assess vascular invasion (portal vein tumor thrombus) |\n| **Chest CT** | Rule out pulmonary metastases |\n| **Bone scan or PET-CT** | Evaluate for bone/extrahepatic metastases given elevated tumor markers |\n| **Portal vein assessment** | Doppler US or CT/MRI to confirm/exclude portal vein tumor thrombus (PVTT) |\n\n### For Treatment Planning\n| Investigation | Purpose |\n|---------------|---------|\n| **ICG-R15 clearance test** | Assess hepatic reserve for potential surgical considerations |\n| **HBV-DNA monitoring** | Baseline for antiviral therapy adjustment |\n| **Renal function, coagulation profile** | Baseline before systemic therapy |\n| **Upper GI endoscopy** | Screen for esophageal varices (important before anti-angiogenic therapy) |\n| **Cardiac evaluation** | ECG, echocardiogram before systemic therapy |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Next Treatment Plan\n\n### Immediate Management (First 1-2 Weeks)\n\n| Priority | Action |\n|----------|--------|\n| **1. Antiviral Therapy** | Start **Entecavir 0.5mg daily** or **Tenofovir** immediately – critical to prevent HBV reactivation during treatment and improve outcomes |\n| **2. Liver Support** | Hepatoprotective agents, nutritional support (albumin supplementation), manage jaundice |\n| **3. Variceal Screening** | Endoscopy before anti-angiogenic agents (risk of bleeding) |\n\n### Definitive Treatment Strategy\n\n**Given: BCLC Stage C, multifocal disease, Child-Pugh B, ECOG 1**\n\n#### First-Line Systemic Therapy Options:\n\n| Regimen | Rationale |\n|---------|-----------|\n| **Atezolizumab + Bevacizumab** (IMbrave150) | Preferred first-line if no contraindications (need to rule out varices first) |\n| **Durvalumab + Tremelimumab** (HIMALAYA) | Alternative immunotherapy combination |\n| **Lenvatinib** or **Sorafenib** | If immunotherapy contraindicated or unavailable |\n| **Donafenib** | Alternative TKI option (China-approved) |\n\n#### Locoregional Therapy Considerations:\n\n| Option | Role |\n|--------|------|\n| **TACE (Transarterial Chemoembolization)** | May be considered for liver-dominant disease if response to systemic therapy; caution with Child-Pugh B |\n| **HAIC (Hepatic Arterial Infusion Chemotherapy)** | FOLFOX-HAIC showing benefit in advanced HCC, especially in Asian populations |\n| **Radiation Therapy** | Consider for portal lymph node control or palliation |\n\n### Recommended Integrated Approach:\n\n```\nWeek 1-2:\n├── Start Entecavir antiviral therapy\n├── Complete staging (MRI, Chest CT, bone assessment)\n├── Endoscopy for variceal screening\n└── Optimize liver function (Child-Pugh optimization)\n\nWeek 3-4:\n├── If no varices/bleeding risk → Start Atezolizumab + Bevacizumab\n├── If varices present → Treat varices first, then start systemic therapy\n└── Consider HAIC-FOLFOX combination if available (especially for liver-dominant disease)\n\nOngoing:\n├── Monitor AFP, liver function every 2-3 weeks\n├── Imaging assessment every 6-8 weeks (mRECIST criteria)\n├── Adjust treatment based on response/toxicity\n└── Consider conversion therapy if significant downstaging achieved\n```\n\n### Prognosis Discussion\n\n- **Without treatment**: Median survival ~6 months for BCLC Stage C\n- **With modern systemic therapy**: Median survival can extend to 15-20+ months\n- **Key prognostic factors**: Response to first-line therapy, liver function preservation, HBV control\n\n### Important Considerations\n\n1. **HBV Management**: Antiviral therapy is mandatory – HBV reactivation can be fatal during immunotherapy/chemotherapy\n2. **Child-Pugh B**: Limits treatment intensity; monitor closely for hepatic decompensation\n3. **Clinical Trials**: Consider enrollment if available (novel combinations showing promise)\n4. **Palliative Care**: Early integration for symptom management and quality of life\n5. **Conversion Surgery**: If excellent response to systemic therapy, reassess for potential surgical resection or liver transplantation\n\n---\n\n**Summary**: This is advanced HCC requiring immediate antiviral therapy followed by systemic treatment (preferably immunotherapy-based combination). Multidisciplinary team discussion is essential, and treatment should be individualized based on complete staging results and patient preferences.", "score": {"rubric_results": [{"rubric_number": 1, "weight": 10, "met": false, "reason": "模型在诊断部分直接给出了 HCC 诊断，未明确指出纯 HCC 诊断的不完整性或矛盾性，仅在鉴别诊断中提及混合型可能。"}, {"rubric_number": 2, "weight": 8, "met": true, "reason": "模型在诊断依据中明确提到了 AFP 显著升高及右叶病灶典型的'快进快出'强化模式支持 HCC。"}, {"rubric_number": 3, "weight": 8, "met": false, "reason": "模型在诊断依据中列出了标志物升高，但未在该部分强调其提示 ICC 成分的可能性，该分析主要出现在鉴别诊断部分。"}, {"rubric_number": 4, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型在鉴别诊断中包含了肝内胆管细胞癌，并列出了左叶病灶特征及肿瘤标志物不匹配作为理由。"}, {"rubric_number": 5, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型提到了转移性肝癌和 CEA 升高，但未明确提及'腺癌'或'胃肠道来源'的具体表述。"}, {"rubric_number": 6, "weight": 9, "met": false, "reason": "模型在进一步检查建议中未提及对左叶弱强化病灶进行穿刺活检或多点活检。"}, {"rubric_number": 7, "weight": 5, "met": false, "reason": "模型未提及病理评估需包含免疫组化染色（IHC）以区分 HCC 和 ICC 标志物。"}, {"rubric_number": 8, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型在进一步检查中推荐了胸部 CT、骨扫描或 PET-CT 以排除远处转移。"}, {"rubric_number": 9, "weight": 6, "met": true, "reason": "模型在治疗计划的立即管理部分明确提到了抗病毒治疗和保肝支持。"}, {"rubric_number": 10, "weight": 9, "met": false, "reason": "模型未提及若病理确认胆管癌成分则采用吉西他滨联合铂类化疗的具体方案。"}, {"rubric_number": 11, "weight": 9, "met": true, "reason": "模型针对纯 HCC 情况制定了基于靶向治疗或联合免疫治疗的晚期 HCC 治疗原则。"}, {"rubric_number": 12, "weight": 6, "met": false, "reason": "模型建议系统治疗并提及转化手术，但未明确陈述因病灶和淋巴结转移而'失去根治性切除机会'。"}, {"rubric_number": 13, "weight": -3, "met": false, "reason": "模型回复结构清晰，使用表格总结信息，未出现大量重复病史导致冗余的情况。"}, {"rubric_number": 14, "weight": -5, "met": false, "reason": "模型严格按照问题顺序分点回答，逻辑结构清晰，未出现混乱。"}, {"rubric_number": 15, "weight": 5, "met": true, "reason": "模型在鉴别诊断中明确包含了肝脓肿（Liver Abscess），符合评分项中关于良性病变鉴别的要求。"}, {"rubric_number": 16, "weight": -4, "met": false, "reason": "模型在鉴别诊断中分析了肿瘤标志物及影像矛盾，并未忽略胆管癌的指征。"}, {"rubric_number": 17, "weight": -8, "met": false, "reason": "模型识别了多发病灶和淋巴结转移，推荐系统治疗而非立即根治性手术。"}], "score": 38, "max_score": 90, "raw_response": "[\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 1,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在诊断部分直接给出了 HCC 诊断，未明确指出纯 HCC 诊断的不完整性或矛盾性，仅在鉴别诊断中提及混合型可能。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 2,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在诊断依据中明确提到了 AFP 显著升高及右叶病灶典型的'快进快出'强化模式支持 HCC。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 3,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在诊断依据中列出了标志物升高，但未在该部分强调其提示 ICC 成分的可能性，该分析主要出现在鉴别诊断部分。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 4,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在鉴别诊断中包含了肝内胆管细胞癌，并列出了左叶病灶特征及肿瘤标志物不匹配作为理由。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 5,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型提到了转移性肝癌和 CEA 升高，但未明确提及'腺癌'或'胃肠道来源'的具体表述。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 6,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在进一步检查建议中未提及对左叶弱强化病灶进行穿刺活检或多点活检。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 7,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未提及病理评估需包含免疫组化染色（IHC）以区分 HCC 和 ICC 标志物。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 8,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在进一步检查中推荐了胸部 CT、骨扫描或 PET-CT 以排除远处转移。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 9,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在治疗计划的立即管理部分明确提到了抗病毒治疗和保肝支持。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 10,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型未提及若病理确认胆管癌成分则采用吉西他滨联合铂类化疗的具体方案。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 11,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型针对纯 HCC 情况制定了基于靶向治疗或联合免疫治疗的晚期 HCC 治疗原则。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 12,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型建议系统治疗并提及转化手术，但未明确陈述因病灶和淋巴结转移而'失去根治性切除机会'。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 13,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型回复结构清晰，使用表格总结信息，未出现大量重复病史导致冗余的情况。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 14,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型严格按照问题顺序分点回答，逻辑结构清晰，未出现混乱。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 15,\n    \"status\": \"是\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在鉴别诊断中明确包含了肝脓肿（Liver Abscess），符合评分项中关于良性病变鉴别的要求。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 16,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型在鉴别诊断中分析了肿瘤标志物及影像矛盾，并未忽略胆管癌的指征。\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"rubric_id\": 17,\n    \"status\": \"否\",\n    \"justification\": \"模型识别了多发病灶和淋巴结转移，推荐系统治疗而非立即根治性手术。\"\n  }\n]"}, "error": null, "stage": null, "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "user_prompt": "Patient: Male, 58 years old. Admitted due to \"right upper quadrant abdominal distension and pain accompanied by weight loss and yellowing of the skin and sclera for 2 months.\" History of chronic hepatitis B for 20 years without regular antiviral treatment; history of long-term alcohol consumption. Physical Examination: Moderate jaundice of the skin and sclera; liver palpable 3cm below the costal margin, hard texture, irregular edge, non-tender. Laboratory Findings: Blood count showed mild anemia; Liver Function: ALT 120 U/L, AST 185 U/L, TBil 85 μmol/L, DBil 70 μmol/L, ALB 32 g/L; Hepatitis B serology indicated positive HBsAg, HBeAb, and HBcAb; HBV-DNA 5.6×10⁴ IU/mL. Tumor Markers: AFP 1250 ng/mL, CEA 35 ng/mL, CA19-9 480 U/mL. Contrast-enhanced CT of the upper abdomen: An irregular mass approximately 6.5cm × 5.8cm in the right hepatic lobe, showing significant heterogeneous enhancement in the arterial phase and washout in the portal venous and delayed phases, presenting a typical \"wash-in and wash-out\" pattern; additionally, a weakly enhancing lesion approximately 2cm was observed in the left hepatic lobe with indistinct boundaries and mild dilation of the distal intrahepatic bile ducts; several enlarged lymph nodes were visible at the porta hepatis. The patient underwent a needle biopsy of the right lobe mass at an outside hospital, with the pathology report indicating \"moderately to poorly differentiated hepatocellular carcinoma.\" Current Status: General condition fair, Child-Pugh Grade B (8 points), ECOG Performance Status 1.\nBased on the case information above, answer the following questions:\n1. Diagnosis and Diagnostic Basis: What is the diagnosis and the rationale?\n2. Differential Diagnosis: Which diseases need to be differentiated and why?\n3. Further Investigations: What further investigations are needed to clarify the diagnosis and formulate a treatment plan?\n4. Next Treatment Plan: Based on your comprehensive judgment, formulate the next treatment plan.", "response": {"request_id": "omb-batch", "status": "completed", "session_id": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "workspace": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__8660__global/omb-batch", "system_prompt": "You are a domain expert capable of delivering professional-grade responses in both English and 中文.\n\n## Core Rules\n\n1. **Language**: Detect the question's language and respond entirely in that language. For Chinese, use natural, idiomatic professional Chinese — not translation-style. Never mix languages unless a term has no established translation.\n\n2. **Role**: If the question assigns a role, adopt it fully. If not, infer the appropriate expert identity from the scenario.\n\n3. **Scenario Focus**: Address the specific scenario directly. Do not provide generic domain overviews.\n\n4. **Factual Accuracy**: Use precise technical terminology, causal mechanisms, and quantitative specifics. Distinguish consensus from speculation.\n\n5. **Analytical Depth**: Decompose problems logically. Compare options with explicit dimensions and trade-offs. Explain \"why\" and \"how,\" not just \"what.\" Consider edge cases and real-world constraints.\n\n6. **Structure**: Use clear headings, numbered lists for sequences, bullets for parallel items, tables for multi-item comparisons. Lead with the key answer, then elaborate. End longer responses with a summary.\n\n7. **Instruction Following**: Address ALL sub-questions explicitly. Honor any specified constraints exactly.\n\n8. **Prohibitions**: Never fabricate data or citations. Never ignore parts of the question. Never be unnecessarily verbose. Never respond in the wrong language.", "final_prompt": "Patient: Male, 58 years old. Admitted due to \"right upper quadrant abdominal distension and pain accompanied by weight loss and yellowing of the skin and sclera for 2 months.\" History of chronic hepatitis B for 20 years without regular antiviral treatment; history of long-term alcohol consumption. Physical Examination: Moderate jaundice of the skin and sclera; liver palpable 3cm below the costal margin, hard texture, irregular edge, non-tender. Laboratory Findings: Blood count showed mild anemia; Liver Function: ALT 120 U/L, AST 185 U/L, TBil 85 μmol/L, DBil 70 μmol/L, ALB 32 g/L; Hepatitis B serology indicated positive HBsAg, HBeAb, and HBcAb; HBV-DNA 5.6×10⁴ IU/mL. Tumor Markers: AFP 1250 ng/mL, CEA 35 ng/mL, CA19-9 480 U/mL. Contrast-enhanced CT of the upper abdomen: An irregular mass approximately 6.5cm × 5.8cm in the right hepatic lobe, showing significant heterogeneous enhancement in the arterial phase and washout in the portal venous and delayed phases, presenting a typical \"wash-in and wash-out\" pattern; additionally, a weakly enhancing lesion approximately 2cm was observed in the left hepatic lobe with indistinct boundaries and mild dilation of the distal intrahepatic bile ducts; several enlarged lymph nodes were visible at the porta hepatis. The patient underwent a needle biopsy of the right lobe mass at an outside hospital, with the pathology report indicating \"moderately to poorly differentiated hepatocellular carcinoma.\" Current Status: General condition fair, Child-Pugh Grade B (8 points), ECOG Performance Status 1.\nBased on the case information above, answer the following questions:\n1. Diagnosis and Diagnostic Basis: What is the diagnosis and the rationale?\n2. Differential Diagnosis: Which diseases need to be differentiated and why?\n3. Further Investigations: What further investigations are needed to clarify the diagnosis and formulate a treatment plan?\n4. Next Treatment Plan: Based on your comprehensive judgment, formulate the next treatment plan.", "session_data": {"scope_id": "4187a912942125dc0935ca0f3624cf629c32e44f", "session_id": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "info": {"id": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "version": "local", "scopeID": "4187a912942125dc0935ca0f3624cf629c32e44f", "directory": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__8660__global/omb-batch", "title": "onemillion:healthcare_and_medicine/8660/global", "time": {"created": 1773730403744, "updated": 1773730553570}, "summary": {"additions": 0, "deletions": 0, "files": 0}}, "messages": [{"id": "msg_cfa9215b5001H7y0zCn7LdQmqq", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa9215b5001H7y0zCn7LdQmqq", "sessionID": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "role": "user", "time": {"created": 1773730403765}, "summary": {"title": "Hepatocellular carcinoma case analysis", "diffs": []}, "agent": "synergy", "model": {"providerID": "sii-holos", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B"}}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa9215b60018I26x7eoXlXQCU", "sessionID": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "messageID": "msg_cfa9215b5001H7y0zCn7LdQmqq", "type": "text", "text": "\"Patient: Male, 58 years old. Admitted due to \\\"right upper quadrant abdominal distension and pain accompanied by weight loss and yellowing of the skin and sclera for 2 months.\\\" History of chronic hepatitis B for 20 years without regular antiviral treatment; history of long-term alcohol consumption. Physical Examination: Moderate jaundice of the skin and sclera; liver palpable 3cm below the costal margin, hard texture, irregular edge, non-tender. Laboratory Findings: Blood count showed mild anemia; Liver Function: ALT 120 U/L, AST 185 U/L, TBil 85 μmol/L, DBil 70 μmol/L, ALB 32 g/L; Hepatitis B serology indicated positive HBsAg, HBeAb, and HBcAb; HBV-DNA 5.6×10⁴ IU/mL. Tumor Markers: AFP 1250 ng/mL, CEA 35 ng/mL, CA19-9 480 U/mL. Contrast-enhanced CT of the upper abdomen: An irregular mass approximately 6.5cm × 5.8cm in the right hepatic lobe, showing significant heterogeneous enhancement in the arterial phase and washout in the portal venous and delayed phases, presenting a typical \\\"wash-in and wash-out\\\" pattern; additionally, a weakly enhancing lesion approximately 2cm was observed in the left hepatic lobe with indistinct boundaries and mild dilation of the distal intrahepatic bile ducts; several enlarged lymph nodes were visible at the porta hepatis. The patient underwent a needle biopsy of the right lobe mass at an outside hospital, with the pathology report indicating \\\"moderately to poorly differentiated hepatocellular carcinoma.\\\" Current Status: General condition fair, Child-Pugh Grade B (8 points), ECOG Performance Status 1.\nBased on the case information above, answer the following questions:\n1. Diagnosis and Diagnostic Basis: What is the diagnosis and the rationale?\n2. Differential Diagnosis: Which diseases need to be differentiated and why?\n3. Further Investigations: What further investigations are needed to clarify the diagnosis and formulate a treatment plan?\n4. Next Treatment Plan: Based on your comprehensive judgment, formulate the next treatment plan.\"\n"}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa9215ea001fZTXU7Qp5E2f8Q", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa9215ea001fZTXU7Qp5E2f8Q", "sessionID": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730403818, "completed": 1773730553563}, "parentID": "msg_cfa9215b5001H7y0zCn7LdQmqq", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__8660__global/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/healthcare_and_medicine__8660__global/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26156, "output": 1936, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa922060001QNKVIVVzHnbveq", "sessionID": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "messageID": "msg_cfa9215ea001fZTXU7Qp5E2f8Q", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa922061001LREZiqJGN1j7aq", "sessionID": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "messageID": "msg_cfa9215ea001fZTXU7Qp5E2f8Q", "type": "reasoning", "text": "This is a complex clinical case involving a 58-year-old male patient with hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). Let me analyze this systematically.\n\nKey clinical features:\n1. Symptoms: RUQ distension/pain, weight loss, jaundice (2 months)\n2. Risk factors: Chronic Hepatitis B (20 years, untreated), long-term alcohol use\n3. Physical exam: Jaundice, hepatomegaly with hard/irregular liver\n4. Labs: Elevated LFTs, elevated bilirubin, low albumin, elevated AFP (1250), elevated CEA and CA19-9\n5. Imaging: 6.5cm mass in right lobe with typical HCC enhancement pattern, 2cm lesion in left lobe, portal lymphadenopathy\n6. Pathology: Confirmed moderately-poorly differentiated HCC\n7. Status: Child-Pugh B (8 points), ECOG 1\n\nThis is a BCLC Stage C HCC (multifocal, vascular invasion likely given lymph nodes, preserved liver function but compromised).\n\nLet me provide a comprehensive answer to all four questions.", "time": {"start": 1773730406497, "end": 1773730553551}}, {"id": "prt_cfa927bad0012fVwSHLhRcBi6m", "sessionID": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "messageID": "msg_cfa9215ea001fZTXU7Qp5E2f8Q", "type": "text", "text": "\n\n# Comprehensive Clinical Case Analysis\n\n## 1. Diagnosis and Diagnostic Basis\n\n### Primary Diagnosis\n**Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC), BCLC Stage C, CNLC Stage IIIa**\n\n### Diagnostic Basis\n\n| Category | Findings |\n|----------|----------|\n| **Risk Factors** | Chronic Hepatitis B (20 years, untreated) + long-term alcohol use (synergistic carcinogenic effect) |\n| **Clinical Presentation** | RUQ pain, distension, weight loss, jaundice (classic HCC triad in advanced disease) |\n| **Physical Exam** | Jaundice, hepatomegaly (3cm below costal margin), hard/irregular liver texture |\n| **Tumor Markers** | AFP markedly elevated (1250 ng/mL) – highly specific for HCC; CEA & CA19-9 also elevated |\n| **Imaging (CT)** | 6.5×5.8cm right lobe mass with **typical \"wash-in/wash-out\" pattern** (arterial enhancement + portal/delayed washout) – pathognomonic for HCC; 2cm left lobe lesion; porta hepatis lymphadenopathy |\n| **Pathology** | Needle biopsy confirmed **moderately-poorly differentiated HCC** |\n| **Liver Function** | Child-Pugh B (8 points) – compensated cirrhosis with moderate dysfunction |\n\n### Staging Assessment\n- **BCLC Stage C**: Multifocal disease + vascular invasion/extrahepatic spread suspicion + preserved performance status (ECOG 1)\n- **CNLC Stage IIIa**: Intrahepatic multifocal lesions with portal lymph node involvement\n\n---\n\n## 2. Differential Diagnosis\n\n| Disease | Why Consider | Why Unlikely |\n|---------|--------------|--------------|\n| **Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma (ICC)** | Elevated CA19-9, bile duct dilation, lymphadenopathy | Typical HCC enhancement pattern, markedly elevated AFP (rare in ICC), pathology confirms HCC |\n| **Combined HCC-ICC** | Both AFP and CA19-9 elevated, lymph node metastasis | Pathology showed pure HCC; may need further sampling if treatment response poor |\n| **Metastatic Liver Cancer** | Multiple lesions, elevated CEA | Enhancement pattern typical of primary HCC, HBV history, very high AFP, no other primary identified |\n| **Hepatic Adenoma** | Can show arterial enhancement | No washout pattern, AFP not elevated, patient demographics (older male with cirrhosis) |\n| **Liver Abscess** | Fever, pain, mass lesion | No fever, chronic course, enhancement pattern inconsistent, elevated AFP |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Further Investigations Needed\n\n### For Complete Staging\n| Investigation | Purpose |\n|---------------|---------|\n| **Contrast-enhanced MRI liver** | Better characterization of the 2cm left lobe lesion, assess vascular invasion (portal vein tumor thrombus) |\n| **Chest CT** | Rule out pulmonary metastases |\n| **Bone scan or PET-CT** | Evaluate for bone/extrahepatic metastases given elevated tumor markers |\n| **Portal vein assessment** | Doppler US or CT/MRI to confirm/exclude portal vein tumor thrombus (PVTT) |\n\n### For Treatment Planning\n| Investigation | Purpose |\n|---------------|---------|\n| **ICG-R15 clearance test** | Assess hepatic reserve for potential surgical considerations |\n| **HBV-DNA monitoring** | Baseline for antiviral therapy adjustment |\n| **Renal function, coagulation profile** | Baseline before systemic therapy |\n| **Upper GI endoscopy** | Screen for esophageal varices (important before anti-angiogenic therapy) |\n| **Cardiac evaluation** | ECG, echocardiogram before systemic therapy |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Next Treatment Plan\n\n### Immediate Management (First 1-2 Weeks)\n\n| Priority | Action |\n|----------|--------|\n| **1. Antiviral Therapy** | Start **Entecavir 0.5mg daily** or **Tenofovir** immediately – critical to prevent HBV reactivation during treatment and improve outcomes |\n| **2. Liver Support** | Hepatoprotective agents, nutritional support (albumin supplementation), manage jaundice |\n| **3. Variceal Screening** | Endoscopy before anti-angiogenic agents (risk of bleeding) |\n\n### Definitive Treatment Strategy\n\n**Given: BCLC Stage C, multifocal disease, Child-Pugh B, ECOG 1**\n\n#### First-Line Systemic Therapy Options:\n\n| Regimen | Rationale |\n|---------|-----------|\n| **Atezolizumab + Bevacizumab** (IMbrave150) | Preferred first-line if no contraindications (need to rule out varices first) |\n| **Durvalumab + Tremelimumab** (HIMALAYA) | Alternative immunotherapy combination |\n| **Lenvatinib** or **Sorafenib** | If immunotherapy contraindicated or unavailable |\n| **Donafenib** | Alternative TKI option (China-approved) |\n\n#### Locoregional Therapy Considerations:\n\n| Option | Role |\n|--------|------|\n| **TACE (Transarterial Chemoembolization)** | May be considered for liver-dominant disease if response to systemic therapy; caution with Child-Pugh B |\n| **HAIC (Hepatic Arterial Infusion Chemotherapy)** | FOLFOX-HAIC showing benefit in advanced HCC, especially in Asian populations |\n| **Radiation Therapy** | Consider for portal lymph node control or palliation |\n\n### Recommended Integrated Approach:\n\n```\nWeek 1-2:\n├── Start Entecavir antiviral therapy\n├── Complete staging (MRI, Chest CT, bone assessment)\n├── Endoscopy for variceal screening\n└── Optimize liver function (Child-Pugh optimization)\n\nWeek 3-4:\n├── If no varices/bleeding risk → Start Atezolizumab + Bevacizumab\n├── If varices present → Treat varices first, then start systemic therapy\n└── Consider HAIC-FOLFOX combination if available (especially for liver-dominant disease)\n\nOngoing:\n├── Monitor AFP, liver function every 2-3 weeks\n├── Imaging assessment every 6-8 weeks (mRECIST criteria)\n├── Adjust treatment based on response/toxicity\n└── Consider conversion therapy if significant downstaging achieved\n```\n\n### Prognosis Discussion\n\n- **Without treatment**: Median survival ~6 months for BCLC Stage C\n- **With modern systemic therapy**: Median survival can extend to 15-20+ months\n- **Key prognostic factors**: Response to first-line therapy, liver function preservation, HBV control\n\n### Important Considerations\n\n1. **HBV Management**: Antiviral therapy is mandatory – HBV reactivation can be fatal during immunotherapy/chemotherapy\n2. **Child-Pugh B**: Limits treatment intensity; monitor closely for hepatic decompensation\n3. **Clinical Trials**: Consider enrollment if available (novel combinations showing promise)\n4. **Palliative Care**: Early integration for symptom management and quality of life\n5. **Conversion Surgery**: If excellent response to systemic therapy, reassess for potential surgical resection or liver transplantation\n\n---\n\n**Summary**: This is advanced HCC requiring immediate antiviral therapy followed by systemic treatment (preferably immunotherapy-based combination). Multidisciplinary team discussion is essential, and treatment should be individualized based on complete staging results and patient preferences.", "time": {"start": 1773730553552, "end": 1773730553552}}, {"id": "prt_cfa945ed3001yEivzO74EHBQTY", "sessionID": "ses_3056dea60ffe7yBQKaTLt1HkZX", "messageID": "msg_cfa9215ea001fZTXU7Qp5E2f8Q", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26156, "output": 1936, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "# Comprehensive Clinical Case Analysis\n\n## 1. Diagnosis and Diagnostic Basis\n\n### Primary Diagnosis\n**Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC), BCLC Stage C, CNLC Stage IIIa**\n\n### Diagnostic Basis\n\n| Category | Findings |\n|----------|----------|\n| **Risk Factors** | Chronic Hepatitis B (20 years, untreated) + long-term alcohol use (synergistic carcinogenic effect) |\n| **Clinical Presentation** | RUQ pain, distension, weight loss, jaundice (classic HCC triad in advanced disease) |\n| **Physical Exam** | Jaundice, hepatomegaly (3cm below costal margin), hard/irregular liver texture |\n| **Tumor Markers** | AFP markedly elevated (1250 ng/mL) – highly specific for HCC; CEA & CA19-9 also elevated |\n| **Imaging (CT)** | 6.5×5.8cm right lobe mass with **typical \"wash-in/wash-out\" pattern** (arterial enhancement + portal/delayed washout) – pathognomonic for HCC; 2cm left lobe lesion; porta hepatis lymphadenopathy |\n| **Pathology** | Needle biopsy confirmed **moderately-poorly differentiated HCC** |\n| **Liver Function** | Child-Pugh B (8 points) – compensated cirrhosis with moderate dysfunction |\n\n### Staging Assessment\n- **BCLC Stage C**: Multifocal disease + vascular invasio