, {"id": "prt_cfa9625830026N4YDfvANqsBYP", "sessionID": "ses_3056a44acffeBF0TOGYuM8Hsqb", "messageID": "msg_cfa9621e5001ljwX76YsfAExkh", "type": "text", "text": "\n\n让我尝试用学术搜索工具：", "time": {"start": 1773730671021, "end": 1773730671021}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9626a7001a27kVrrgm3sQEz", "sessionID": "ses_3056a44acffeBF0TOGYuM8Hsqb", "messageID": "msg_cfa9621e5001ljwX76YsfAExkh", "type": "tool", "callID": "call_c1bdb5a5b90343828f669226", "tool": "arxiv_search", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"query": "copper catalyzed three-component reaction boronic acid amine aldehyde", "topK": 10}, "output": "Found 10 papers (showing 10):\n\n| # | arXiv ID | Title | Authors | Categories | Published |\n|---|----------|-------|---------|------------|-----------|\n| 1 | 1712.05904v1 | Copper-catalyzed efficient synthesis of 5-arylindazolo[3,2-b | Zahra ArastehFard, Karim Akbari Dilmaghani, Mehdi Soheilizad et al. | physics.chem-ph | 2017-12-16 |\n| 2 | 2110.12819v1 | Molecular Diffusivity of Click Reaction Components: The Diff | Nasrollah Rezaei-Ghaleh, Jaime Agudo-Canalejo, Christian Griesinger et al. | physics.chem-ph | 2021-10-25 |\n| 3 | 2204.14062v1 | Multimodal Transformer-based Model for Buchwald-Hartwig and  | Shimaa Baraka, Ahmed M. El Kerdawy | cs.LG, cs.AI | 2022-04-27 |\n| 4 | 1307.2671v1 | Copper Replaces Tin: A Copper based Gelling Catalyst for Pol | Bibudha Parasar, Gao Wen Jing, Dandan Yuan et al. | cond-mat.mtrl-sci | 2013-07-10 |\n| 5 | 1912.06027v2 | Formation of copper boride on Cu(111) | Chengguang Yue, Xiao-Ji Weng, Guoying Gao et al. | cond-mat.mtrl-sci, physics.chem-ph | 2019-12-12 |\n| 6 | 2212.06014v2 | Reaction profiles for quantum chemistry-computed [3 + 2] cyc | Thijs Stuyver, Kjell Jorner, Connor Coley | physics.chem-ph | 2022-12-12 |\n| 7 | 2103.15392v1 | Synthesis of Three-Dimensionally Interconnected Hexagonal Bo | Zahid Hussain, Hye-Won Yang, Sookyung Choi et al. | cond-mat.mtrl-sci | 2021-03-29 |\n| 8 | 2511.01392 | Pd/Cu single atom alloys for catalysis: from single crystall | Philipp Haugg, Jan Smyczek, Carsten Schröder et al. | cond-mat.mtrl-sci | 2025-11-03 |\n| 9 | 1811.02633v2 | Molecular Transformer - A Model for Uncertainty-Calibrated C | Philippe Schwaller, Teodoro Laino, Théophile Gaudin et al. | physics.chem-ph, cs.LG | 2018-11-06 |\n| 10 | 2211.10829v1 | Depositing boron on Cu(111): Borophene or boride? | Xiao-Ji Weng, Jie Bai, Jingyu Hou et al. | cond-mat.mtrl-sci, cond-mat.mes-hall | 2022-11-20 |\n\n**Paper Details:**\n\n### 1712.05904v1: Copper-catalyzed efficient synthesis of 5-arylindazolo[3,2-b]quinazolin-7(5H)-ones from 2-nitrobenzaldehydes\n**Authors:** Zahra ArastehFard, Karim Akbari Dilmaghani, Mehdi Soheilizad, Mohammad Mahdavi\n**Categories:** physics.chem-ph\n**Published:** 2017-12-16\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/1712.05904v1\n\n**Abstract:** A novel and practical copper-catalyzed approach is developed for the\npreparation of 5-arylindazolo[3,2-b]quinazolin-7(5H)-ones. The\n2-amino-N'-arylbenzohydrazide, which easily prepared by reaction of isatoic\nanhydride with arylhydrazine, through a condensation/intramolecular cyclization\nreacted by 2-nitrobenzaldehydes in the present of CuI to afford corresponding\n5-arylindazolo[3,2-b]quinazolin-7(5H)-ones in good yields.\n\n---\n\n### 2110.12819v1: Molecular Diffusivity of Click Reaction Components: The Diffusion Enhancement Question\n**Authors:** Nasrollah Rezaei-Ghaleh, Jaime Agudo-Canalejo, Christian Griesinger, Ramin Golestanian\n**Categories:** physics.chem-ph\n**Published:** 2021-10-25\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.12819v1\n\n**Abstract:** Micrometer-sized objects are widely known to exhibit chemically-driven\nmotility in systems away from equilibrium. Experimental observation of\nreaction-induced motility or enhancement in diffusivity at the much shorter\nlength scale of small molecules is however still a matter of debate. Here, we\ninvestigate the molecular diffusivity of reactants, catalyst and product of a\nmodel reaction, the copper-catalyzed azide-alkyne cycloaddition click reaction,\nand develop new NMR diffusion approaches that allow the probing of\nreaction-induced diffusion enhancement in nano-sized molecular systems with\nhigher precision than the state of the art. Following two different approaches\nthat enable the accounting of time-dependent concentration changes during NMR\nexperiments, we closely monitored the diffusion coefficient of reaction\ncomponents during the reaction. The reaction components showed distinct changes\nin the diffusivity: while the two reactants underwent a time-dependent decrease\nin their diffusivity, the diffusion coefficient of the product gradually\nincreased and the catalyst showed only slight diffusion enhancement within the\nrange expected for reaction-induced sample heating. The decrease in diffusion\ncoefficient of the alkyne, one of the two reactants of click reaction, was not\nreproduced during its copper coordination when the second reactant, azide, was\nabsent. Our results do not support the catalysis-induced diffusion enhancement\nof the components of click reaction and, instead, point to the role of a\nrelatively large intermediate species within the reaction cycle with\ndiffusivity lower than both the reactants and product molecule.\n\n---\n\n### 2204.14062v1: Multimodal Transformer-based Model for Buchwald-Hartwig and Suzuki-Miyaura Reaction Yield Prediction\n**Authors:** Shimaa Baraka, Ahmed M. El Kerdawy\n**Categories:** cs.LG, cs.AI\n**Published:** 2022-04-27\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.14062v1\n\n**Abstract:** Predicting the yield percentage of a chemical reaction is useful in many\naspects such as reducing wet-lab experimentation by giving the priority to the\nreactions with a high predicted yield. In this work we investigated the use of\nmultiple type inputs to predict chemical reaction yield. We used simplified\nmolecular-input line-entry system (SMILES) as well as calculated chemical\ndescriptors as model inputs. The model consists of a pre-trained bidirectional\ntransformer-based encoder (BERT) and a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) with a\nregression head to predict the yield. We experimented on two high throughput\nexperimentation (HTE) datasets for Buchwald-Hartwig and Suzuki-Miyaura\nreactions. The experiments show improvements in the prediction on both datasets\ncompared to systems using only SMILES or chemical descriptors as input. We also\ntested the model's performance on out-of-sample dataset splits of\nBuchwald-Hartwig and achieved comparable results with the state-of-the-art. In\naddition to predicting the yield, we demonstrated the model's ability to\nsuggest the optimum (highest yield) reaction conditions. The model was able to\nsuggest conditions that achieves 94% of the optimum reported yields. This\nproves the model to be useful in achieving the best results in the wet lab\nwithout expensive experimentation.\n\n---\n\n### 1307.2671v1: Copper Replaces Tin: A Copper based Gelling Catalyst for Poly-Urethane from Discarded Motherboard\n**Authors:** Bibudha Parasar, Gao Wen Jing, Dandan Yuan, Wang Kun, Peng Wang, Arijit Dasgupta, Atharva Sahasrabudhe, Soumitra Barman, Rongxin Yuan, Soumyajit Roy\n**Categories:** cond-mat.mtrl-sci\n**Published:** 2013-07-10\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.2671v1\n\n**Abstract:** A discarded motherboard based eco-friendly copper catalyst has been\nprogrammed to replace the industrially used tin based catalyst DBTDL. The\ncatalyst has been characterized by UV-Vis spectroscopy, FT-IR and TEM. Using\nthe catalyst reaction conditions is optimized and under the optimized\ncondition, both polyurethane and polyurethane foam are prepared, thus proving\nthe generality of the catalyst to be used in industries. A possible mechanism\nhas also been proposed.\n\n---\n\n### 1912.06027v2: Formation of copper boride on Cu(111)\n**Authors:** Chengguang Yue, Xiao-Ji Weng, Guoying Gao, Artem R. Oganov, Xiao Dong, Xi Shao, Xiaomeng Wang, Jian Sun, Bo Xu, Hui-Tian Wang, Xiang-Feng Zhou, Yongjun Tian\n**Categories:** cond-mat.mtrl-sci, physics.chem-ph\n**Published:** 2019-12-12\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.06027v2\n\n**Abstract:** Boron forms compounds with nearly all metals, with notable exception of\ncopper and other group IB and IIB elements. Here, we report an unexpected\ndiscovery of ordered copper boride grown epitaxially on Cu(111) under ultrahigh\nvacuum. Scanning tunneling microscopy experiments combined with ab initio\nevolutionary structure prediction reveal a remarkably complex structure of\n2D-Cu8B14. Strong intra-layer p-d hybridization and a large amount of charge\ntransfer between Cu and B atoms are the key factors for the emergence of copper\nboride. This makes the discovered material unique and opens up the possibility\nof synthesizing ordered low-dimensional structures in similar immiscible\nsystems.\n\n---\n\n### 2212.06014v2: Reaction profiles for quantum chemistry-computed [3 + 2] cycloaddition reactions\n**Authors:** Thijs Stuyver, Kjell Jorner, Connor Coley\n**Categories:** physics.chem-ph\n**Published:** 2022-12-12\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.06014v2\n\n**Abstract:** Bio-orthogonal click chemistry based on [3 + 2] dipolar cycloadditions has\nhad a profound impact on the field of biochemistry and significant effort has\nbeen devoted to identify promising new candidate reactions for this purpose. To\ngauge whether a prospective reaction could be a suitable bio-orthogonal click\nreaction, information about both on- and off-target activation and reaction\nenergies is highly valuable. Here, we use an automated workflow, based on the\nautodE program, to compute over 5000 reaction profiles for [3 + 2]\ncycloadditions involving both synthetic dipolarophiles and a set of\nbiologically-inspired structural motifs. Based on a succinct benchmarking\nstudy, the B3LYP-D3(BJ)/def2-TZVP//B3LYP-D3(BJ)/def2-SVP level of theory was\nselected for the DFT calculations, and standard conditions and an (aqueous) SMD\nmodel were imposed to mimic physiological conditions. We believe that this\ndata, as well as the presented workflow for high-throughput reaction profile\ncomputation, will be useful to screen for new bio-orthogonal reactions, as well\nas for the development of novel machine learning models for the prediction of\nchemical reactivity more broadly.\n\n---\n\n### 2103.15392v1: Synthesis of Three-Dimensionally Interconnected Hexagonal Boron Nitride Networked Cu-Ni Composite\n**Authors:** Zahid Hussain, Hye-Won Yang, Sookyung Choi, Byang-Sang Choi\n**Categories:** cond-mat.mtrl-sci\n**Published:** 2021-03-29\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.15392v1\n\n**Abstract:** A three-dimensionally interconnected hexagonal boron nitride (3Di-hBN)\nnetworked Cu-Ni (3Di-hBN-Cu-Ni) composite was successfully synthesized in situ\nusing a simple two-step process which involved the compaction of mixed Cu-Ni\npowders (70 wt.% Cu and 30 wt.% Ni) into a disc followed by metal-organic\nchemical vapor deposition (MOCVD) process at 1000{\\deg}C. During MOCVD, the\nCu-Ni alloy grains acted as a template for the growth of hexagonal boron\nnitride (hBN) while decaborane and ammonia were used as precursors for boron\nand nitrogen, respectively. Boron and nitrogen atoms diffused into the Cu-Ni\nsolution during the MOCVD process, precipitated out and grew along the Cu-Ni\ninterfaces upon cooling. It was demonstrated that pores were generated during\nthe sintering process and then filled by bulk hBN during the MOCVD process\n(indicated by energy dispersive spectroscopy) as the pores also served as\ncatalytic sites for the nucleation and growth of hBN. Optical microscopy\nexamination indicated that there was a minimum amount of bulk hBN at certain\ncompaction pressure (280 MPa) and sintering time (30 min). Scanning electron\nmicroscopy and transmission electron microscopy revealed that the\ninterconnected network of hBN layers surrounding the Cu-Ni grains was developed\nin the 3Di-hBN-Cu-Ni composite. This 3Di-hBN network is expected to enhance the\nresistance of the 3Di-hBN-Cu-Ni composite against mechanical, thermal and\nchemical attacks. Moreover, foam-like 3Di-hBN was extracted from 3Di-hBN-Cu-Ni\ncomposite which could be further applied in the fields of biomedicine and\nenergy storage.\n\n---\n\n### 2511.01392: Pd/Cu single atom alloys for catalysis: from single crystalline to nanostructured model systems for highly selective butanol dehydrogenation\n**Authors:** Philipp Haugg, Jan Smyczek, Carsten Schröder, Paul Fröhlich, Paul Kohlmorgen, Stephan Appelfeller, Konstantin Neyman, Swetlana Schauermann\n**Categories:** cond-mat.mtrl-sci\n**Published:** 2025-11-03\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.01392\n\n**Abstract:** Heterogeneous catalysts based on Single Atom Alloys (SAA) play a significant\nrole in numerous technical processes. The fundamental working principles of\nthese systems, however, remain poorly understood, especially the aspects\nrelated to the nanoscopic nature of bimetallic particles and the associated\nstructure-reactivity relationships. In this study, we developed for the first\ntime well-defined SAA catalysts consisting of Pd atomically dispersed in Cu\nnanoparticles prepared under ultra-high vacuum (UHV) conditions on model\nAl2O3/NiAl(110) support. Employing a unique combination of surface sensitive\ntechniques - scanning tunneling microscopy (STM), infrared reflection\nabsorption spectroscopy (IRAS), molecular beams - and density functional theory\n(DFT) calculations, we performed detailed structural characterization of these\nsystems at the microscopic level. We demonstrate that Pd disperses atomically\nin Cu nanoparticles and becomes partly negatively charged. Importantly, these\nPd/Cu nanostructured systems show an outstanding catalytic performance in\nselective dehydrogenation of butanol and exhibit 100 % selectivity toward\nbutanal over a broad range of Pd loadings - the property that cannot be\nreproduced employing simplified single crystalline Pd/Cu(111) counterparts. The\ndeveloped approach for preparation and characterization of these nanostructured\nSAA-catalysts lays a foundation for further fundamental-level catalytic studies\non this important class of materials and their rational design for practical\napplications.\n\n---\n\n### 1811.02633v2: Molecular Transformer - A Model for Uncertainty-Calibrated Chemical Reaction Prediction\n**Authors:** Philippe Schwaller, Teodoro Laino, Théophile Gaudin, Peter Bolgar, Costas Bekas, Alpha A Lee\n**Categories:** physics.chem-ph, cs.LG\n**Published:** 2018-11-06\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/1811.02633v2\n\n**Abstract:** Organic synthesis is one of the key stumbling blocks in medicinal chemistry.\nA necessary yet unsolved step in planning synthesis is solving the forward\nproblem: given reactants and reagents, predict the products. Similar to other\nwork, we treat reaction prediction as a machine translation problem between\nSMILES strings of reactants-reagents and the products. We show that a\nmulti-head attention Molecular Transformer model outperforms all algorithms in\nthe literature, achieving a top-1 accuracy above 90% on a common benchmark\ndataset. Our algorithm requires no handcrafted rules, and accurately predicts\nsubtle chemical transformations. Crucially, our model can accurately estimate\nits own uncertainty, with an uncertainty score that is 89% accurate in terms of\nclassifying whether a prediction is correct. Furthermore, we show that the\nmodel is able to handle inputs without reactant-reagent split and including\nstereochemistry, which makes our method universally applicable.\n\n---\n\n### 2211.10829v1: Depositing boron on Cu(111): Borophene or boride?\n**Authors:** Xiao-Ji Weng, Jie Bai, Jingyu Hou, Yi Zhu, Li Wang, Penghui Li, Anmin Nie, Bo Xu, Xiang-Feng Zhou, Yongjun Tian\n**Categories:** cond-mat.mtrl-sci, cond-mat.mes-hall, physics.app-ph\n**Published:** 2022-11-20\n**PDF:** http://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.10829v1\n\n**Abstract:** Large-area single-crystal surface structures were successfully prepared on\nCu(111) substrate with boron deposition, which is critical for prospective\napplications. However, the proposed borophene structures do not match the\nscanning tunneling microscopy (STM) results very well, while the proposed\ncopper boride is at odds with the traditional knowledge that ordered\ncopper-rich borides normally do not exist due to small difference in\nelectronegativity and large difference in atomic size. To clarify the\ncontroversy and elucidate the formation mechanism of the unexpected copper\nboride, we conducted systematic STM, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy and\nangle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy investigations, confirming the\nsynthesis of two-dimensional copper boride rather than borophene on Cu(111)\nafter boron deposition under ultrahigh vacuum. First-principles calculations\nwith defective surface models further indicate that boron atoms tend to react\nwith Cu atoms near terrace edges or defects, which in turn shapes the\nintermediate structures of copper boride and leads to the formation of stable\nCu-B monolayer via large-scale surface reconstruction eventually.\n\n---\n", "title": "10 papers found", "metadata": {"total": 10, "shown": 10, "truncated": false}, "time": {"start": 1773730670970, "end": 1773730674197}}}, {"id": "prt_cfa96361700176K5IQGgmcARql", "sessionID": "ses_3056a44acffeBF0TOGYuM8Hsqb", "messageID": "msg_cfa9621e5001ljwX76YsfAExkh", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "tool-calls", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 26087, "output": 64, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}, {"id": "msg_cfa96362100133CryRzdfESgwc", "info": {"id": "msg_cfa96362100133CryRzdfESgwc", "sessionID": "ses_3056a44acffeBF0TOGYuM8Hsqb", "role": "assistant", "time": {"created": 1773730674209, "completed": 1773730760158}, "parentID": "msg_cfa95bb68001aTKXXZ90M9fTGz", "modelID": "Qwen 3.5 397B A17B", "providerID": "sii-holos", "mode": "synergy", "agent": "synergy", "path": {"cwd": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__5683__cn/omb-batch", "root": "/inspire/hdd/project/multi-agent/niexiaohang-25130061/holos-synergy/benchmark/workspaces/onemillion/natural_science__5683__cn/omb-batch"}, "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 30638, "output": 1997, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}, "finish": "stop"}, "parts": [{"id": "prt_cfa964112001u8xVHm21QbAPPk", "sessionID": "ses_3056a44acffeBF0TOGYuM8Hsqb", "messageID": "msg_cfa96362100133CryRzdfESgwc", "type": "step-start", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}, {"id": "prt_cfa964113001Qb3XzRRWKifh7h", "sessionID": "ses_3056a44acffeBF0TOGYuM8Hsqb", "messageID": "msg_cfa96362100133CryRzdfESgwc", "type": "text", "text": "\n\narXiv 的搜索结果没有直接匹配这个反应。让我基于有机合成知识来分析这个反应。\n\n根据您提供的反应条件：\n- **反应物**：双(4-甲氧基苯基)胺 + 2-氧代-2-苯基乙醛（苯甲酰甲醛）+ 苯硼酸\n- **催化剂**：Cu(OTFA)₂·xH₂O（三氟乙酸铜）\n- **溶剂**：1,2-二氯乙烷，80°C\n\n这是一个典型的**三组分偶联反应**，属于**Petasis 反应**（或称 Petasis 硼酸-Mannich 反应）的变体。让我详细分析：\n\n## 产物 A 的结构与 IUPAC 名称\n\n这个反应是**α-酮醛、二芳胺和芳基硼酸的三组分偶联**，产物是**α-氨基酮**类化合物。\n\n**反应过程**：\n1. 2-氧代-2-苯基乙醛（Ph-CO-CHO）的醛基与双(4-甲氧基苯基)胺缩合形成亚胺中间体\n2. 苯硼酸对亚胺进行亲核加成（铜催化活化）\n3. 最终得到α-氨基酮\n\n**产物 A 的结构**：\n```\n                    O\n                    ||\nPh - C - CH - N(4-MeO-Ph)₂\n         |\n         Ph\n```\n\n**IUPAC 名称**：\n**2-(双(4-甲氧基苯基)氨基)-1,2-二苯基乙酮**\n英文：*2-(bis(4-methoxyphenyl)amino)-1,2-diphenylethanone*\n\n---\n\n## 预期的波谱数据\n\n### ¹H NMR (预计, 400 MHz, CDCl₃)\n\n| 化学位移 (δ, ppm) | 积分 | 裂分 | 归属 |\n|-------------------|------|------|------|\n| ~7.80-7.75 | 2H | d | 苯甲酰基邻位 H (与羰基共轭) |\n| ~7.50-7.20 | 8H | m | 两个苯基的芳香 H |\n| ~6.90-6.80 | 4H | d | 4-甲氧基苯基的芳香 H (邻位到 N) |\n| ~6.70-6.60 | 4H | d | 4-甲氧基苯基的芳香 H (邻位到 OMe) |\n| ~6.50 | 1H | s | CH (手性中心，连接 N 和 Ph) |\n| ~3.75 | 6H | s | 两个 OCH₃ |\n\n### ¹³C NMR (预计, 100 MHz, CDCl₃)\n\n| 化学位移 (δ, ppm) | 归属 |\n|-------------------|------|\n| ~195-200 | C=O (酮羰基) |\n| ~155-157 | C-O (甲氧基连接的碳) |\n| ~140-145 | 季碳 (与 N 相连的苯环 ipso 位) |\n| ~135-138 | 苯基季碳 (连接 CH) |\n| ~128-133 | 芳香 CH (多个) |\n| ~114-115 | 甲氧基苯环的芳香 CH |\n| ~65-70 | CH (手性中心) |\n| ~55-56 | OCH₃ |\n\n### DEPT 谱\n\n- **DEPT-135**：\n  - **正信号**（CH, CH₃）：~55 ppm (OCH₃), ~65 ppm (CH), 所有芳香 CH\n  - **负信号**（CH₂）：无（该分子没有 CH₂）\n  - **无信号**（季碳）：C=O (~195 ppm), C-O (~155 ppm), 季芳香碳\n\n- **DEPT-90**：\n  - 仅显示 CH：~65 ppm (手性 CH), 所有芳香 CH\n\n### ESI-MS (电喷雾电离质谱)\n\n**分子式**：C₂₈H₂₅NO₃  \n**分子量**：423.18 g/mol\n\n- **[M+H]⁺**：m/z = 424.2\n- **[M+Na]⁺**：m/z = 446.2\n- **[M+K]⁺**：m/z = 462.1\n\n---\n\n## 苯硼酸在反应中的作用\n\n苯硼酸在这个反应中扮演**亲核试剂**的角色，具体作用如下：\n\n### 1. **作为碳亲核试剂**\n苯硼酸中的苯基通过铜催化被活化，对亚胺中间体进行亲核加成，形成新的 C-C 键。\n\n### 2. **反应优势**\n- **温和性**：相比有机锂或格氏试剂，硼酸更稳定、更易处理\n- **选择性**：只与活化的亚胺反应，不与其他官能团反应\n- **原子经济性**：硼酸副产物无害\n\n---\n\n## 反应机理\n\n这是一个**铜催化的三组分 Petasis-type 反应**，可能的机理如下：\n\n```\n步骤 1: 亚胺形成\n                    OH\n                    |\nPh-CO-CHO + HN(Ar)₂ → Ph-CO-CH=N(Ar)₂ + H₂O\n(Ar = 4-MeO-Ph)\n\n步骤 2: 铜催化剂活化硼酸\nPh-B(OH)₂ + Cu(II) → [Ph-Cu(II)-B(OH)₂] 活性络合物\n\n步骤 3: 转金属化\n[Ph-Cu(II)-B(OH)₂] → Ph-Cu(III) 或 Ph-Cu(I) 活性物种\n\n步骤 4: 对亚胺的亲核加成\n                    O                    O\n                    ||                   ||\nPh-CO-CH=N(Ar)₂ + Ph-Cu → Ph-CO-CH-N(Ar)₂\n                              |\n                              Ph\n\n步骤 5: 质子化/产物释放\n                    O\n                    ||\nPh-CO-CH-N(Ar)₂ + H⁺ → 产物 A + Cu 催化剂再生\n         |\n         Ph\n```\n\n### 详细机理说明：\n\n1. **亚胺形成**（缩合）\n   - 2-氧代-2-苯基乙醛的醛基与双(4-甲氧基苯基)胺的氨基发生缩合\n   - 失去一分子水，形成亚胺（席夫碱）中间体\n   - 酮羰基保持不变（酮的反应性低于醛）\n\n2. **铜催化剂活化**\n   - Cu(II) 与苯硼酸配位，形成有机铜物种\n   - 可能经过转金属化生成 Ph-Cu(III) 或 Ph-Cu(I) 活性中间体\n\n3. **亲核加成**\n   - 活化的苯基对亚胺的 C=N 双键进行亲核进攻\n   - 这是反应的决速步\n   - 铜同时活化亚胺（作为路易斯酸）和苯硼酸（转金属化）\n\n4. **产物形成与催化剂再生**\n   - 加合物质子化，释放产物\n   - 铜催化剂再生，进入下一个催化循环\n\n### 反应的关键特点：\n\n- **区域选择性**：反应只发生在醛基位置，酮羰基不参与\n- **立体化学**：生成手性中心，但非手性条件下得到外消旋体\n- **铜的作用**：路易斯酸活化 + 转金属化媒介\n\n---\n\n## 总结\n\n| 项目 | 内容 |\n|------|------|\n| **产物 A** | 2-(双(4-甲氧基苯基)氨基)-1,2-二苯基乙酮 |\n| **分子式** | C₂₈H₂₅NO₃ |\n| **ESI-MS [M+H]⁺** | m/z 424.2 |\n| **关键 ¹H NMR** | δ ~6.5 (s, 1H, CH), ~3.75 (s, 6H, OMe) |\n| **关键 ¹³C NMR** | δ ~195 (C=O), ~65 (CH) |\n| **苯硼酸作用** | 提供苯基亲核试剂，形成新的 C-C 键 |\n| **反应类型** | 铜催化三组分 Petasis-type 偶联 |\n\n如果您需要更详细的机理图或实验验证建议，请告诉我！", "time": {"start": 1773730760150, "end": 1773730760150}}, {"id": "prt_cfa9785d7001i9LjSf4FAp0sYh", "sessionID": "ses_3056a44acffeBF0TOGYuM8Hsqb", "messageID": "msg_cfa96362100133CryRzdfESgwc", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "snapshot": "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904", "cost": 0, "tokens": {"input": 30638, "output": 1997, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"read": 0, "write": 0}}}]}]}, "result": {"answer": "arXiv 的搜索结果没有直接匹配这个反应。让我基于有机合成知识来分析这个反应。\n\n根据您提供的反应条件：\n- **反应物**：双(4-甲氧基苯基)胺 + 2-氧代-2-苯基乙醛（苯甲酰甲醛）+ 苯硼酸\n- **催化剂**：Cu(OTFA)₂·xH₂O（三氟乙酸铜）\n- **溶剂**：1,2-二氯乙烷，80°C\n\n这是一个典型的**三组分偶联反应**，属于**Petasis 反应**（或称 Petasis 硼酸-Mannich 反应）的变体。让我详细分析：\n\n## 产物 A 的结构与 IUPAC 名称\n\n这个反应是**α-酮醛、二芳胺和芳基硼酸的三组分偶联**，产物是**α-氨基酮**类化合物。\n\n**反应过程**：\n1. 2-氧代-2-苯基乙醛（Ph-CO-CHO）的醛基与双(4-甲氧基苯基)胺缩合形成亚胺中间体\n2. 苯硼酸对亚胺进行亲核加成（铜催化活化）\n3. 最终得到α-氨基酮\n\n**产物 A 的结构**：\n```\n                    O\n                    ||\nPh - C - CH - N(4-MeO-Ph)₂\n         |\n         Ph\n```\n\n**IUPAC 名称**：\n**2-(双(4-甲氧基苯基)氨基)-1,2-二苯基乙酮**\n英文：*2-(bis(4-methoxyphenyl)amino)-1,2-diphenylethanone*\n\n---\n\n## 预期的波谱数据\n\n### ¹H NMR (预计, 400 MHz, CDCl₃)\n\n| 化学位移 (δ, ppm) | 积分 | 裂分 | 归属 |\n|-------------------|------|------|------|\n| ~7.80-7.75 | 2H | d | 苯甲酰基邻位 H (与羰基共轭) |\n| ~7.50-7.20 | 8H | m | 两个苯基的芳香 H |\n| ~6.90-6.80 | 4H | d | 4-甲氧基苯基的芳香 H (邻位到 N) |\n| ~6.70-6.60 | 4H | d | 4-甲氧基苯基的芳香 H (邻位到 OMe) |\n| ~6.50 | 1H | s | CH (手性中心，连接 N 和 Ph) |\n| ~3.75 | 6H | s | 两个 OCH₃ |\n\n### ¹³C NMR (预计, 100 MHz, CDCl₃)\n\n| 化学位移 (δ, ppm) | 归属 |\n|-------------------|------|\n| ~195-200 | C=O (酮羰基) |\n| ~155-157 | C-O (甲氧基连接的碳) |\n| ~140-145 | 季碳 (与 N 相连的苯环 ipso 位) 